    "2026-05-08T02:38:20.318Z": {
      "msg_index": 0,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "The land remembers nothing of what was done to it.\r\n\r\nFrom high enough above, the valley is nothing but green — a deep, unbroken green that has no interest in the past, that has swallowed roads and foundations and the rusted skeletons of things without names and grown fat on them. The forests press in close on every side, dark at the treeline and lighter where the canopy thins toward the hilltops, and between them the river moves in its unhurried way toward the city as though it has always done this and intends to keep doing it long after the city is gone too.\r\n\r\nThe city itself appears first as a smudge of grey against the valley floor — smoke and stone, the particular density of a place where many people have agreed to be in the same location for a long time. It is not large by the standards of the world that preceded it. By the standards of the world that exists now, it is enormous. The largest thing most people who live in it have ever seen.\r\n\r\nCloser, and the smudge resolves into detail. The industrial districts first, squat and practical along the river's western bank, chimneys exhaling their slow grey breath into the morning air. The market district behind them, quiet at this hour, stalls shuttered, the cobblestones still damp from last night. The residential streets fanning outward from the center in the organic, unplanned way of cities that grew rather than were designed — narrow here, wider there, following the logic of old paths that no one remembers the reason for anymore. Crude oil lamps still burning at the intersections, yellow and slightly hazy, waiting for someone to come around and snuff them when the sun gets high enough to make them redundant.\r\n\r\nAnd at the center, where the city's streets seem to arrive and stop as though they have been leading here all along: the Cathedral.\r\n\r\nIt rises above everything else the way things built to intimidate are built to rise — not gracefully, but with a kind of massive, deliberate authority, dark stone against the lightening sky. The forecourt at its foot is empty at this hour, the great ceremonial doors closed, the iron candle-stands in the narthex visible in faint outline through the narrow windows on either side of the entrance. The building has the settled, immovable quality of something that expects to outlast everything around it and is probably right.\r\n\r\nInside, the nave is dim and still, the stained glass not yet catching enough light to do what it does later in the day — for now the figures in the glass are just shapes, the colors muted, Lady Idella in her white-and-blue a suggestion rather than a statement. The pews stand in their rows. The chancel waits. The confessionals on either side of it are closed and quiet. The whole space is holding its breath the way large empty rooms do, aware that it will shortly be asked to perform.\r\n\r\nIn the narthex, one thing moves.\r\n\r\nShe is small against the scale of the room — a slight figure in a black habit, her face framed by the white head-covering, a taper in one hand and a fresh candle in the other. She works methodically, moving along the tiered iron stands with the particular efficiency of someone who has done this so many times that her hands know what to do without being asked. The old candles come out. The new ones go in. The taper touches each wick in sequence, and small flames appear one by one in her wake, pushing the darkness back by increments.\r\n\r\nHer name is Sister Eliza. She is twenty-one years old. She has been doing this since before she was old enough to reach the upper tiers without standing on her toes, and she will do it again tomorrow, and the day after that.",
