    "2026-05-08T04:09:01.830Z": {
      "msg_index": 1,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "The flame is a small thing, but it demands everything from the wax.\n\nThe scent of the narthex was a familiar, heavy cocktail: old incense, cold stone, and the sharp, metallic tang of the candles. As she worked, the rhythmic *click-clink* of the candles being slotted into the iron holders echoed in the cavernous space. To any observer, Eliza was the picture of serene devotion, her movements fluid and her gaze lowered.\n\nInside, however, the silence was a lie.\n\nHer mind was a storm of static and yearning. The memories of last night—the fragments of a dream she couldn't quite name but could still *feel*—clung to her like a damp shroud. It had been a dream of touch, of warmth, of a body that wasn't her own pressing against her in a way that made her breath hitch even in her sleep. She woke up trembling, the sheets feeling too coarse against her skin, her heart hammering against her ribs like a trapped bird.\n\n*I am a vessel of filth,* she thought, the internal voice a harsh, echoing mimicry of the sermons. *A traitor to the faith. A leak in the spiritual energy.*\n\nThe guilt was a physical weight, a cold pressure in her chest. She hated the dreams, but she hated the vacuum they left behind even more. She felt a sudden, irrational surge of anger toward her own biology—this traitorous body that craved things she was told were sins, that hummed with a hunger she had no vocabulary to describe.\n\nAnd then there was the clock. Her twenty-second birthday was approaching. The Biological Deadline. The thought of it made her stomach churn; it was a countdown to a destiny she didn't want, a mandate of reproduction that felt more like a sentence than a blessing.\n\nAs she moved toward the final candle-stand, a shadow flickered in the periphery of her vision. She stiffened. For a heartbeat, she wondered if it was Father Gaspard. The mere thought of him brought back the sensation of his breath—stale wine and decay—too close to her ear, the way his beady eyes seemed to peel back her skin to find the 'sin' beneath. The anxiety spiked, a sharp needle of panic that made her fingers tremble.\n\n\"Eliza! You're nearly finished!\"\n\nThe voice was bright, grounding, and entirely devoid of malice. Eliza blinked, her posture relaxing instantly as she looked up to see Sister Cecilia.\n\nThe sheep-anthro was a vision of tidiness, her white wool fluffy and pristine beneath her habit, her expression one of uncomplicated warmth. Cecilia was moving toward her with a soft, rhythmic step, a small, contented hum vibrating in her throat. To Cecilia, the world was a series of right angles and clear instructions; she didn't have storms in her head, only a steady, golden light.\n\n\"I... yes, Sister,\" Eliza replied, her voice soft and breathless, slipping effortlessly back into the mask of the demure acolyte. \"Just a few more to light.\"\n\nCecilia stepped closer, her brown eyes shining. \"I've been thinking about the schedule for after the service. The Almoner mentioned we might be able to slip away to the market for the new linens and the oil lamps. Imagine it, Eliza! Actually getting to walk through the stalls without a Warden breathing down our necks for once.\"\n\nEliza looked at her roommate, feeling a strange, distant kinship with this girl who truly believed the world was safe. \"The market,\" Eliza whispered, the word tasting of something forbidden.\n\n\"Oh, yes!\" Cecilia beamed, her tail giving a tiny, involuntary wag beneath the heavy fabric of her robes. \"I heard the spice merchant from the south has arrived. I don't actually *need* anything, of course, but just to see the colors and smell the air... it feels like a reward for a week of hard work, doesn't it?\"\n\nEliza forced a small, shy smile. \"It does. I would... I would like that, Cecilia.\"\n\nFor a moment, the crushing weight of the deadline and the fear of Gaspard receded. The idea of the market—the noise, the smells, the sheer *presence* of other people—felt like a lifeline. It was a small thing, a tiny crack in the wall of her repression, but it was enough to keep her moving.",
