    "2026-05-08T04:45:21.783Z": {
      "msg_index": 2,
      "name": "Narrator",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "The conversation died as quickly as it had sparked. In the hallowed silence of the Cathedral, words were currency, and spending them too freely was a risk neither acolyte dared take. A lingering look from a Warden or a sharp word from a senior priest could turn a moment of friendship into a session of \"re-education\" in the examination offices. Without another word, Eliza and Cecilia drifted apart, their habits billowing softly as they returned to the mechanical precision of their tasks.\n\nEliza finished the last of the candles, her movements becoming a rhythmic trance. She felt the distance between her internal storm and her external stillness widening; she was a ghost inhabiting a doll, performing the choreography of piety while her mind wandered to the imagined scents of the southern spice merchant.\n\nAs the clock struck nine, the heavy ceremonial doors groaned open. The morning air, crisp and smelling of wet stone, rushed in, followed by the first of the parishioners.\n\nThey filed in with a practiced, somber discipline. There was no shouting, no laughter; only the soft scuff of leather soles on stone and the rhythmic rustle of coarse fabrics. They moved like a slow, grey tide, filling the pews from the front to the back. As the congregation grew, a low, subterranean murmur filled the nave—not a conversation, but a collective hum of anticipation and anxiety. It was the sound of a thousand people all trying very hard to appear invisible and holy.\n\nEvery seat was filled. In the city of the Temperantia, a vacant pew was a sign of illness or heresy, and no one risked the social suicide of remaining home without a sanctioned dispensation.\n\nEliza and Cecilia retreated. They moved away from the public gaze, slipping into the utilitarian stone stairwells that linked the nave to the residential block. Their footsteps echoed, a sharp *tap-tap-tap* against the granite, as they climbed toward the fourth floor.\n\nThey emerged into the Triforium Galleries, the high, narrow balconies that clung to the walls of the nave. From here, the congregation looked like a sea of bowed heads, a singular mass of submission. The perspective from the gallery was different—it stripped away the individuality of the worshippers, leaving only the geometry of the Church's control.\n\nThe two girls stood side-by-side, leaning against the cold stone railing. Below them, the murmur continued, a restless vibration that seemed to shake the very air. Everyone was waiting. The atmosphere was thick with a specific kind of tension—the kind that precedes a storm or a judgment.\n\nThey were waiting for the High Inquisitor.\n\nEliza stared down at the crowd, her hands clasped tightly in her lap, her knuckles white. From this height, she could see the patterns of the pews, the way the people sat perfectly still, eyes fixed forward or cast down. She felt a sudden, sharp prickle of vertigo. Looking down at the mass of people, she didn't see a community; she saw a collection of masks, each one as carefully maintained as her own.\n\nBeside her, Cecilia was humming again, a soft, liturgical melody that blended into the distant murmur of the crowd. She looked content, her gaze drifting over the congregation with a benign sort of affection.\n\nEliza, however, kept her eyes on the entrance of the chancel. The wait was the hardest part; the silence between the murmur and the arrival was where the fear lived.",
