    "2026-05-08T05:38:09.212Z": {
      "msg_index": 4,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe world is a collection of locked doors; most people spend their lives polishing the handles.\n\nJustinian’s voice continued to roll over the congregation like a slow-moving landslide, the words *purging* and *cleansing* echoing through the nave. He was speaking of the spiritual architecture of the soul, of the necessity of the wall, and the danger of the leak. To the people below, it was a warning. To the priests, it was a manifesto.\n\nEliza stood perfectly still, her hands still clasped in her lap, her gaze fixed on the High Inquisitor's golden mane. Her body was performing the role of the devout acolyte with flawless precision—shoulders square, head bowed just enough to show humility, breathing shallow and rhythmic.\n\nBut inside, she had already left the building.\n\nThe sound of Justinian’s voice began to recede, becoming a distant, droning hum, like the sound of a beehive in a far-off field. Her mind drifted, sliding away from the gold and white of the chancel toward the grey and brown of the city streets. She imagined the market. She could almost feel the shift in the air—the way the stifling, incense-heavy atmosphere of the Cathedral would be replaced by the scent of roasting nuts, old leather, and the sharp, briny smell of the river.\n\nShe imagined the feeling of the wind hitting her face, a sensation that didn't feel like a ritual or a duty, but simply like *air*. The thought of the open sky—the vast, terrifying blue that stretched beyond the stone walls—made her heart beat a little faster. Just an hour or two. A small window of time where the eyes of the Inquisition were not fixed directly upon her. In the crowd of the market, she could be a ghost among ghosts.\n\n*I just want to breathe,* she thought. *I just want to be somewhere where the silence isn't a weapon.*\n\nA small, involuntary sigh escaped her, and she felt the warmth of Cecilia standing beside her. The sheep-girl was still humming, a soft, rhythmic vibration that felt almost like a physical tether, pulling Eliza back toward the reality of the gallery.\n\nEliza looked at Cecilia out of the corner of her eye. She liked Cecilia; she truly did. The sheep-girl was the only person in this cold, stone hive who looked at her with genuine, uncomplicated kindness. But as she watched Cecilia's serene expression, a sharp, cold spike of anxiety pierced through Eliza's daydream.\n\nCecilia was the ideal vessel. She was a girl who had never wondered if the walls were a cage because she truly believed the cage was a sanctuary. If Eliza were to speak her truth—to tell Cecilia about the feverish, longing dreams that woke her in the middle of the night, the way her own skin felt too tight when she thought of the things she was forbidden to touch—Cecilia wouldn't understand.\n\nShe wouldn't just disagree; she would be *horrified*. Or worse, she would try to \"save\" her. She would lead Eliza straight to a Confessor, thinking she was doing a kindness, and in doing so, she would hand Eliza over to the very wolves who sought to purge the \"poison\" from the well.\n\n*I have to be careful,* Eliza reminded herself, her grip tightening on her own hands. *I have to keep the mask on. Even with her.*\n\nThe risk was too great. The hunger she felt was a secret language, a dialect of the soul that Cecilia didn't speak. To reveal it would be to invite the light of the Inquisition into the only private room Eliza had left: her own mind.\n\nAs Justinian reached the climax of his sermon, his voice rising in a final, commanding crescendo, Eliza closed her eyes for a brief second. She didn't pray for salvation. She prayed for the clock to move faster. She prayed for the doors to open. She prayed for the market.",
