      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe air in the Cathedral doesn't move; it only settles, like dust on a coffin.\n\nThe final notes of Justinian's sermon lingered in the air, a heavy, oppressive resonance that seemed to vibrate in the very marrow of the worshippers' bones. As the congregation filed out in a silent, synchronized exodus, Eliza and Cecilia descended from the Triforium. The transition from the gold-lit height of the gallery to the cold, grey corridors of the residential block felt like sinking underwater—the light dimmed, the air grew stale, and the scale of the building shifted from the theatrical to the utilitarian.\n\n\"The Almoner will be expecting us,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice returning to its soft, cheerful cadence. \"He's very particular about the ledger. We must make sure the counts are exact, or he'll have us re-doing the inventory for the entire month.\"\n\nEliza nodded, her steps light and hurried. \"Yes, Sister. Precision is... the only way to avoid error.\"\n\nThe words felt hollow. As they walked toward the ground floor, Eliza’s mind was already halfway to the city gates. She could practically feel the freedom of the market, the anonymity of the crowd. She was imagining the texture of the linens they were sent to fetch—the rough, honest feel of fabric that hadn't been blessed or sanctified, but simply *made*.\n\nThey turned the corner toward the Almoner's office, the corridor narrowing as they entered the heart of the administrative wing. The walls here were lined with heavy oak cabinets and the faint smell of old ink and beeswax. The Almoner's office was just ahead, a sanctuary of bureaucracy where the city's resources were tallied with a religious fervor.\n\nBut as they approached the heavy door, a figure stepped out from a recessed alcove, blocking their path.\n\nThe air seemed to sour instantly. Eliza stopped dead, her heart leaping into her throat.\n\nFather Gaspard.\n\nHe stood there, a squat, bloated silhouette in charcoal-grey robes that smelled of stale wine and ancient sweat. His bulbous nose was a map of broken red capillaries, and his small, beady eyes were narrowed, calculating. He didn't look at Cecilia; his gaze was fixed entirely on Eliza, a look of predatory hunger masked as pastoral concern.\n\n\"Sister Cecilia,\" Gaspard wheezed, his voice a grating, wet rattle. \"I believe the Almoner has the supplies ready. You may proceed to the office. I have a... particular matter of spiritual guidance to discuss with Sister Eliza.\"\n\nCecilia stopped, her expression one of mild, trusting confusion. \"Oh? I didn't know Eliza had an appointment, Father.\"\n\nGaspard’s thin lips curled into a jagged, nicotine-stained smile. \"The Spirit moves in unexpected ways, child. Go on. I shall ensure Eliza joins you shortly.\"\n\nCecilia, ever the obedient daughter of the Church, gave a small, polite nod. \"Of course, Father. I'll wait for you in the office, Eliza!\" She stepped around them, her woolly frame disappearing through the door with a soft, optimistic hum.\n\nThe door clicked shut.\n\nSuddenly, the corridor felt a thousand times smaller. The silence that followed was not the holy silence of the nave, but a suffocating, claustrophobic void. Eliza felt the heat rise in her cheeks, her breath becoming shallow and erratic. She could feel the invisible weight of his gaze, the way he looked at her not as a sister in faith, but as a piece of fruit he was deciding whether or not to peel.\n\nGaspard didn't move immediately. He leaned in, invading her personal space, the smell of his breath—sour and alcoholic—washing over her. He produced a damp handkerchief and wiped his nose with a slow, rhythmic motion.\n\n\"Come now, Eliza,\" he whispered, his voice a guttural snarl wrapped in velvet. \"Let us step into the side-chapel. There is something we must discuss regarding your... *progression*.\"\n\nEliza’s stomach plummeted. A cold, visceral dread washed over her, a feeling of being hunted. She didn't move, her hands clasping together so tightly that her knuckles turned white.\n\n*Please,* she screamed internally, *please let this be a mistake. Please let him forget why he called me.*\n\nBut as Gaspard gestured for her to follow him into the dimness of the alcove, Eliza knew that there was no escape. The mask she wore was the only thing protecting her, and in the presence of the Examiner, the mask felt as thin as parchment.",
