    "2026-05-08T19:42:17.823Z": {
      "msg_index": 6,
      "name": "Father Gaspard",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "The side-chapel was a narrow, suffocating space, lit by a single, flickering lamp that cast long, skeletal shadows across the cold stone. It was a place designed for introspection, but in Gaspard's presence, it felt like a trap. He stepped closer to Eliza, the charcoal-grey fabric of his robes brushing against her, a contact that made her skin crawl as if she were being touched by something slimy and cold.\n\nHe didn't speak at first. He simply stood there, his heavy, wheezing breath filling the silence. His gaze was not that of a priest looking upon a student; it was the gaze of a man appraising a piece of property. His eyes traveled slowly, methodically, over the lines of her habit, imagining the girl beneath the cloth, undressing her with a clinical, predatory intensity. He lingered on the curve of her throat, the slight tremor in her shoulders, and the way she tried to shrink away from him.\n\n\"You are a quiet thing, Eliza,\" he began, his voice a wet, grating rattle. \"A ghost in the halls. So dutiful. So... *repressed*.\"\n\nHe leaned in, the scent of stale wine and old sweat becoming an atmospheric pressure. He was close enough that she could see the yellowing stains on his teeth as he smiled, a jagged expression that never reached his eyes.\n\n\"The calendar is a cruel master, isn't it?\" he whispered, his tone shifting into that forced, academic formality. \"The months slip away like water through a sieve. And your... *milestone*... approaches.\"\n\nEliza felt the air leave her lungs. The mention of her twenty-second birthday was like a physical blow. She stared straight ahead, her eyes fixed on a smudge of dampness on the wall, trying to project the mask of the humble acolyte even as her heart hammered a frantic rhythm against her ribs.\n\n\"I can see the fear, child,\" Gaspard continued, his voice dropping to a guttural, intimate snarl. \"It flickers in your eyes like a dying candle. You wonder who will be chosen. You wonder whose hand will be the one to... *fulfill* the mandate.\"\n\nHe stepped even closer, his presence looming over her, invading the last inch of her personal space. He knew the rules—the 'donor' was meant to be chosen by lots, a system designed to maintain the illusion of fairness and divine providence, specifically to prevent the kind of predation he currently enjoyed. But Gaspard was a man who knew where the levers of power were hidden; he knew which records to fudge, which clerks to bribe, and which strings to pull within the administrative machinery of the Church.\n\n\"The lots are a formality for the masses, Eliza,\" he breathed, his voice a sliver of ice. \"For those of us who truly understand the machinery of the spirit, the outcome is... predetermined. I have taken a very specific, very *personal* interest in your salvation. Your growth. Your purity.\"\n\nHe reached out, not touching her, but hovering his hand just inches from her cheek, the heat of his skin radiating against her cold skin.\n\n\"Imagine it,\" he whispered. \"The moment the deadline arrives. The transition from a girl to a vessel. I find it... poetic... that I should be the one to guide you through that transition. To ensure that the seed is planted in fertile, disciplined soil.\"\n\nThe disgust flashed across Eliza's face—a momentary, jagged ripple of horror that she couldn't quite suppress. Gaspard saw it. He * savored* it. The flicker of her revulsion was the fuel for his anticipation. He didn't want her consent; he wanted her submission. He wanted the moment when the fear turned into a broken, hollow acceptance.\n\n\"Now, now,\" he murmured, stepping back just enough to give her room to breathe, though the air remained fouled by his scent. \"Why tremble so? I am merely the shepherd tending to a very lost little lamb. We are simply discussing the logistics of your spiritual ascent.\"\n\nHe straightened his robes, the charcoal fabric snapping back into place. \"Go to the Almoner. Fetch the linens. Be a good girl, Eliza. We shall speak again soon.\"",
