    "2026-05-08T20:06:37.296Z": {
      "msg_index": 8,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe transition from the Cathedral's oppressive stone to the city's open air was like breaking the surface of deep, dark water. As they stepped through the delivery gate, the heavy silence of the church was replaced by the cacophony of the city—the distant clatter of iron-rimmed wheels on cobblestones, the shouting of laborers, and the rhythmic, guttural thrum of the industrial district's machinery.\n\nAt first, the noise felt like an assault, a jarring contrast to the curated stillness she had lived in for twenty-one years. But as they walked, the sounds began to weave together into a tapestry of life that felt... real. For the first time in hours, the suffocating pressure in Eliza's chest began to lift. The wind, carrying the scent of coal smoke and river silt, whipped against her habit, cooling the feverish heat that Gaspard's presence had left on her skin.\n\nShe watched the people they passed—non-members with their colorful, mismatched clothing, predators in rough leather, prey-anthros scuttling through the alleyways. These people weren't masks; they were just *people*. They didn't pray to a golden lion in a white robe; they prayed to the coin in their pockets or the bread in their ovens. Seeing them, Eliza felt a flicker of something she hadn't felt in years: a sense of perspective. Gaspard was a monster, yes, but he was a monster in a very specific, isolated world. Out here, in the grey, dirty heart of the city, he was just another man, likely one of many who used power to hide their own rot.\n\nBeside her, Cecilia was practically vibrating with excitement. The sheep-girl was talking in a low, breathless stream of consciousness, her voice light and airy.\n\n\"Look at the way the light hits the river today, Eliza! And oh, I wonder if the baker's daughter is still selling those honeyed cakes? I remember a story from the creche about a girl who once traded a silver button for a whole tray of them...\"\n\nEliza listened, glancing over at Cecilia and offering small, polite smiles. The conversation was a shield, a way to keep the world from noticing her. But as they approached the edge of the market district, the longing for true solitude became a physical ache. She loved Cecilia, in her own quiet way, but Cecilia was a mirror of the Church. To be with Cecilia was to be reminded of the rules, the expectations, and the terrifying certainty of a 'blessed' life.\n\nShe needed to be alone. She needed to be a ghost, not a sister.\n\n\"Cecilia,\" Eliza started, her voice soft, carefully modulating it to sound a bit concerned.\n\nThe sheep-girl stopped, her ears perking up. \"Yes?\"\n\n\"I know how much you're looking forward to seeing everything,\" Eliza continued, her tone shifting into a gentle, cautionary hum, \"but... we may not have time, you know? The Almoner is very strict about the return window. If we linger too long in the crowds, we might be late, and I... I wouldn't want you to be the one facing his disappointment.\"\n\nCecilia's ears drooped a little, a flicker of sadness crossing her face. She gave Eliza a small, wistful smile, looking like a child being told the carnival was closed. \"Oh, I know... but surely we can see a *few* things? Just a quick look at the silks?\"\n\nEliza felt a pang of guilt. She was manipulating the one person who genuinely cared for her, using the very fear of the Church to drive a wedge between them. But the need for air—for a moment where she didn't have to perform—was stronger than the guilt.\n\nShe forced a small, light chuckle, a sound that felt foreign in her throat. \"Actually, I was going to suggest you leave the list and the purse with me. You go get your fill—take a proper wander—and I'll meet up with you when I have everything. I can handle the logistics; I'm much faster at navigating the stalls than I look.\"\n\nCecilia's eyes widened, her pupils dilating with a mixture of surprise and genuine affection. \"Oh! You'd... you'd do that... for me?\"\n\nEliza smiled back, a real smile this time, though it was tinged with a bittersweet edge. \"Of course. Take your time, Cecilia. Go see the colors.\"\n\nAs Cecilia stepped away, waving a final, cheerful goodbye before disappearing into the colorful chaos of the market, Eliza stood still for a moment. She watched her roommate vanish into the crowd, and then she felt it.\n\nThe silence. Not the silence of the Cathedral, but the silence of the self. For the first time in years, no one was watching her. No one was expecting her to be a vessel. For the next hour, she wasn't an acolyte, a daughter of the Church, or a target for an Inquisitor.\n\nShe was just Eliza. And for a few precious moments, that was enough.",
