    "2026-05-08T20:56:24.636Z": {
      "msg_index": 10,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n<T/D>\n\n\nThe market was a symphony of chaos, and Eamon was its most attentive conductor. From his vantage point behind a carefully curated mountain of sundries—brass buttons, coils of hempen twine, and jars of preserved preserves that looked vaguely edible—he watched the world slide by. He didn't just see people; he read them. He read the tension in a shoulder, the hesitation in a step, the way a person’s eyes darted when they thought no one was looking.\n\nHe had spotted them the moment they stepped through the gate. Two acolytes. Two silhouettes of stark, uncompromising black and white. In a sea of muddy browns and garish, patched fabrics, they were like ink blots on a watercolor painting. Most people in the market gave them a wide berth—not out of respect, but out of a subconscious, ingrained fear. The habits were more than clothes; they were signals of the Inquisition’s reach. To be too close to a member of the Temperantia was to risk being noticed by the Temperantia.\n\nEamon leaned back, a small, copper coin dancing between his fingers, flipping rhythmically. *One’s a little bird, the other’s a stone,* he mused, watching them separate.\n\nHe watched the one who stayed—the one who drifted through the stalls with a focused, quiet intensity—as she checked off her list. She moved like a ghost, her steps light, almost tentative. He’d seen a thousand acolytes in his time; usually, they were drones, hollowed out by the creches and filled with a sterile, dutiful silence. But this one... there was a vibration to her. A restlessness.\n\nHe was content to let her be a mere curiosity until she reached Sable’s stall.\n\nSable, the herb-monger's daughter, was a spark of life in a city of grey. She had a way of making the most mundane transaction feel like a secret shared between friends. Eamon watched as the acolyte approached, and he saw the shift. The girl didn't just buy herbs; she froze. He saw the way her posture stiffened, the way she suddenly seemed to forget how to breathe.\n\nFrom his position, Eamon had a clear view of the girl's face. The blush didn't start slowly; it arrived like a wildfire, staining her cheeks a deep, vivid crimson that clashed violently with the stark white of her veil. When she bolted away, nearly tripping over her own hem in her haste to escape, Eamon let out a soft, wheezing chuckle, the coin in his hand coming to a stop.\n\n\"Poor little thing,\" he murmured, a playful glint in his amber eyes. \"Almost burned herself alive just by looking at a girl.\"\n\nHe smiled, imagining the internal conflict—the clash between the rigid, cold doctrines of the Church and the sudden, searing heat of a crush. It was a common enough comedy in a city built on repression.\n\nBut then, the smile faded. The coin stopped moving.\n\nEamon’s head tilted slightly—the same subtle shift he used when he stopped *performing* listening and started *actually* listening to the world. He looked at the girl’s retreating form, her silhouette shrinking as she disappeared into the crowd, and the realization hit him like a physical weight.\n\nThe Temperantia didn't just discourage desire; they weaponized it. They viewed any union that couldn't produce a child—any love that didn't serve the biological mandate—as a \"leak\" of spiritual energy, a betrayal of the species' survival. For a girl like that, an attraction like *that* wasn't just a secret; it was a death sentence for the soul. If the Church found out she was yearning for women, she wouldn't be \"guided\" or \"corrected.\" She would be dismantled.\n\nThe comedy vanished, replaced by a sharp, keen interest.\n\nEamon looked at the space where she had been, his gaze calculating and warm all at once. He didn't see a clumsy acolyte anymore. He saw a person who was living a double life in a city that punished the heart. He saw someone who was, in the most literal sense, starving in a house full of food.\n\n*Well now,* he thought, his voice a slow, internal drawl. *That’s a very interesting problem. And I’ve always been a fan of a good solution.*\n\nHe shifted his weight, glancing at the empty space in the market, already beginning to map out the geometry of her movements. He wasn't in a hurry—the best catches were always the ones who didn't know they were being hunted—but for the first time in months, Eamon felt the thrill of a new project.\n\nThe fourteenth member of the cell was standing right in front of him, and she didn't even know it.",
