      "msg_index": 11,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n<T/D>\n\n\nThe market was a kaleidoscope, a dizzying explosion of color and sound that made the sterile halls of the Cathedral feel like a distant, grey memory. For Cecilia, this wasn't just a shopping trip; it was a pilgrimage of the senses. She moved through the crowds with a wide-eyed wonder, her head tilting this way and that, her fluffy white wool contrasting sharply with the grime of the street. She flitted from stall to stall, her spirit soaring at the sight of hand-woven baskets, iridescent glass beads, and piles of shimmering fabrics that looked like captured rainbows.\n\nShe was a creature of pure, uncomplicated curiosity. She stopped to watch a street performer juggling rusted gears, her mouth slightly open in a small \"o\" of amazement. She breathed in the scent of roasting chestnuts and old leather, her tail giving a rhythmic, happy twitch beneath her heavy robes.\n\n\"Here, little sister! Try a piece!\"\n\nA vendor, a plump man with a small, amused smile, held out a golden-brown ball of fried dough, glistening with a dusting of coarse sugar. It was a small thing, a simple pleasure, but Cecilia froze.\n\nShe looked at the dough, then at the man, then back at the dough. A flicker of internal conflict crossed her face, her brow furrowing. She didn't want to be greedy—greed was a leak, a betrayal of the spirit. But she also didn't want to be rude.\n\n*Does the handbook say anything about free samples?* she wondered. *The Church gives to the poor, but does the poor receive from the world? Is accepting a gift from a stranger a form of vanity?*\n\nShe stood there for a long moment, the vendor's expression shifting from friendly to bemused as he watched the acolyte engage in a silent, spiritual debate with a piece of fried dough. Finally, she straightened her posture, her expression becoming somberly determined.\n\n\"I believe,\" she informed the man with a grave, measured dignity, \"that since the heart is open to all, I would be honored to accept this offering.\"\n\nThe vendor chuckled, a low, hearty sound, and handed over the treat. Cecilia took a bite, the warmth and sweetness blooming on her tongue, a sensation so simple and pure it felt like a revelation.\n\nBut as she chewed, the sweetness faded, replaced by a lingering, nagging thought. The memory of the Cathedral corridor crept back into her mind, a cold draft in a warm room.\n\nShe remembered the way Father Gaspard had stepped out of the alcove. She remembered the predatory tilt of his head, the wet rattle of his voice. Most of all, she remembered the look on Eliza's face.\n\n*She looked... scared,* Cecilia thought, the fried dough now tasting a bit like ash.\n\nIt didn't make sense. Eliza was the gold standard of piety. She was the quietest, most obedient acolyte in the creche. She never complained, never questioned, and never muttered a single word of dissent against the High Inquisitor's decrees. If anyone was \"safe\" from the scrutiny of the examiners, it was Eliza.\n\n*Did she do something?* Cecilia wondered. *Something she's hiding?*\n\nThe thought didn't bring fear, but rather a small, sharp prick of confusion. And then, a tiny, unfamiliar pang of jealousy.\n\nThey were roommates. They were friends. They shared a room, a life, and a devotion. Why wouldn't Eliza tell her? Why was there a secret locked away in that quiet, trembling heart?\n\nCecilia looked around at the vibrant market, the colors suddenly seeming a bit less bright. She felt a strange, hollow sensation in her chest. It wasn't the jealousy of a rival, but the jealousy of a confidante. She wanted to be the one Eliza turned to; she wanted to be the one to offer comfort and a shared prayer.\n\n*Weren't we friends?* she wondered, her ears drooping slightly.\n\nShe realized, with a start, that while she knew the *shape* of Eliza—her habits, her silences, her devotion—she might not actually know Eliza at all. For the first time in her life, Cecilia felt a flicker of doubt, not in the Church, but in the transparency of her own world.",
