      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n<T/D>\n\nThe stone wall was cold against her back, a stark contrast to the heat still radiating from her skin. She leaned there for several minutes, her eyes closed, focusing on the rhythm of her breath—*in, out, in, out*—trying to force the adrenaline to recede. She felt as though she had committed a crime, a transgression of the soul that left her exposed and raw. The touch of the herb-monger's fingers felt like a brand, a mark of her own forbidden nature that might be visible to everyone in the market.\n\n*Be still,* she commanded herself. *Be a vessel. Be a ghost.*\n\nShe smoothed the heavy black fabric of her habit, brushing away nonexistent dust with a trembling hand. She retrieved her bags from the dirt, her movements returning to that rehearsed, ecclesiastical precision. She had to finish the list. She had to return to the Cathedral. She had to return to the mask.\n\nThe next item on the list was the candles.\n\nShe navigated toward the sundries section, where the stalls grew narrower and the goods more practical. She found a stall that smelled of beeswax and old twine, the counters cluttered with jars of nails, coils of rope, and miscellaneous hardware. Behind the counter sat a raccoon, his dark mask-markings giving him a look of permanent, inquisitive skepticism. He was leaning back, a small object dancing between his fingers, watching her approach with an intensity that made her feel like a specimen under a glass.\n\nEliza stepped forward, keeping her gaze lowered, her voice returning to that soft, deferential hum.\n\n\"A box of candles, if you please, sir,\" she said, the words carefully measured, devoid of the panic that still simmered beneath her skin.\n\nThe raccoon didn't answer immediately. He nodded slowly, his amber eyes tracking her every movement. He turned away to gather the candles from a shelf behind him, but as he did, he glanced at her out of the corner of his eye. His voice, when it came, was a low, easy drawl that sounded like a secret being shared in the dark.\n\n\"You'll have to get a little more confident if you want to ask someone like Sable out on a date, you know.\"\n\nEliza froze. The world seemed to tilt on its axis. The noise of the market vanished, replaced by a sudden, deafening roar of blood in her ears. A familiar, searing warmth rushed up her neck, dyeing her skin a vivid, incriminating red.\n\n*He... he saw?*\n\nThe thought was a bolt of lightning. *Oh mother... I thought... I thought I was being careful. I thought I was invisible.*\n\nPanic flared, a sharp, cold needle of terror. If a stranger could see it, could a Warden see it? Could Father Gaspard see it? The fear of exposure was a physical weight, pressing the air from her lungs. She felt the sudden, desperate need to defend herself, to scrub the evidence of her desire away with words.\n\n\"I... I'm sure I... I don't know what you're talking about, sir,\" she said. She tried to inject a note of indignation into her tone, a flicker of the righteous modesty she had been taught. But it came out sounding thin and fragile, the voice of a girl who had been caught in a lie she didn't even know she was telling.\n\nShe braced herself. She expected the punchline. She expected him to laugh, to mock her, or perhaps to call out her 'filth' to the passing crowd. She waited for the derision that usually accompanied the discovery of an acolyte's weakness.\n\nInstead, the raccoon simply set the box of candles on the counter. He gave the cardboard a small, almost affectionate pat, his expression one of mild, seasoned amusement.\n\n\"There you go, sister,\" he said softly. \"Come back soon.\"\n\nEliza stared at him for a brief, blinking moment. There was no judgement in his eyes, only a strange, knowing warmth that she didn't know how to categorize. He wasn't reporting her. He wasn't mocking her. He was simply... acknowledging her.\n\nWith trembling hands, she gathered her candles and retreated from the stall. She didn't look back. She walked away at a brisk pace, her habit billowing around her, her heart still hammering. She was profoundly grateful to have been let off the hook, but as she hurried toward the final items on her list, a new feeling emerged: a simmering, quiet annoyance at herself.\n\nShe had spent her entire life building a fortress of silence, and in one single, clumsy moment, she had let a stranger walk right through the front gates.",
