      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n<T/D>\n\n\n<T/D>\n\n\nThe market had lost its luster. The vibrant silks and the smell of roasting cinnamon, which had seemed like a miracle only an hour ago, now felt like a distraction—a noisy, colorful curtain drawn over a deeper, more troubling silence. Cecilia had wandered for a while longer, her steps slowing as she drifted past a stall of carved wooden toys. She had seen most of what there was to see, the curiosity that usually fueled her now dampened by a lingering, heavy sense of unease.\n\nShe couldn't shake the image of Eliza in that corridor. The way her roommate had looked—not like a sister in faith, but like a creature facing a predator.\n\n*How do I help her?* Cecilia wondered, her brow furrowing. *Is there a prayer for this? A scripture I missed?*\n\nShe searched her internal library of doctrine, looking for the right words, but found nothing that fit. The Church taught that anxiety was a sign of a lack of faith, a failure to trust in the divine order. But this didn't feel like a lack of faith; it felt like a secret. And the idea of a secret between them felt like a cold draft in a warm room, a gap in the architecture of their friendship that Cecilia didn't know how to bridge.\n\nShe began to navigate back toward the meeting point, her gaze scanning the crowd. Then, she spotted her.\n\nEliza was stumbling through the press of the crowd, her silhouette burdened by the weight of the day's acquisitions. She was clutching the heavy linens and boxes of candles, her arms straining against the bulk of the packages. But it wasn't the weight of the goods that caught Cecilia's attention; it was Eliza's face.\n\nThe mask had slipped.\n\nEliza’s brows were drawn tight, her eyes darting around with a frantic, haunted energy. She looked small—not just in size, but in spirit—as if she were trying to merge with the stone walls of the buildings to avoid being seen. She looked worried, almost terrified, her lips pressed into a thin, hard line of distress.\n\nBut the moment Eliza’s eyes locked onto Cecilia’s, the transformation was instantaneous. It was as if a curtain had been drawn shut. The worry vanished, the tension in her shoulders dissolved, and the placid, obedient look returned with a precision that was almost frightening. A small, shy smile touched the corners of her mouth, and she smoothed her habit with a practiced motion as she approached.\n\n\"There you are,\" Eliza said, her voice returning to that soft, breathable hum. \"I've finished the shopping. Did you see everything you wanted to?\"\n\nCecilia felt a pang of disappointment—not because Eliza was acting normal, but because she had been so quickly hidden again. The \"placid look\" wasn't a sign of peace; it was a wall.\n\n\"I did,\" Cecilia replied, her voice warm but tinged with a lingering sadness. \"Thank you for doing this. I'll make it up to you sometime.\"\n\nShe looked at the packages weighing Eliza down—the awkward bulk of the linens, the clinking bottles of ink. Without waiting for an answer, Cecilia reached forward, her fluffy white wool brushing against Eliza's black sleeve.\n\n\"Here, let me help,\" Cecilia insisted, gently taking the heaviest bundles from Eliza's grip. \"You can't carry all that back by yourself. You'll exhaust yourself before we even reach the gates.\"\n\n\"I... thank you, Cecilia,\" Eliza whispered.\n\nAnd with that, they began making their way back through the market. They walked in a comfortable, shared silence, their habits billowing in the gentle breeze. For Cecilia, the sight of the great dark-stone Cathedral appearing on the horizon was a comfort. To her, the massive structure represented stability, a home where every hour was mapped and every desire was managed. It was the only world she had ever known, and she loved it for its clarity.\n\nBut as they passed through the delivery gate and the stone walls closed in around them, Cecilia glanced at Eliza out of the corner of her eye.\n\nShe saw the way Eliza’s pace quickened. She saw the way her shoulders hunched, her head bowing lower and lower as they entered the residential block. Eliza seemed to shrink, not into a ghost, but into a shell, folding herself inward as if trying to disappear entirely before the Cathedral could swallow her.\n\nCecilia looked up at the towering spires, the same spires she saw as a beacon of safety, and wondered if, for Eliza, the Cathedral didn't feel like a home at all.",
