      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe return to the Cathedral was a descent back into a world of curated silence. The air grew heavy with the scent of incense and cold minerals as Eliza and Cecilia navigated the stone arteries of the residential block. The logistics of the delivery were handled with the usual, sterile efficiency; the linens were stacked in the storage vaults, the inks and candles cataloged by a clerk who didn't look up from his ledger, and the Almoner gave a curt, singular nod of approval before dismissing them.\n\nOnce the duties were concluded, the two acolytes parted ways. Eliza retreated to the sacristy to begin the rhythmic, mind-numbing process of cleaning the ritual vestments, her mind a fragmented mess of market-echoes and a growing, cold dread of the night. Cecilia, meanwhile, was assigned to the creche, where she spent her afternoon guiding the youngest children through their lessons in modesty and duty, her presence a soothing, steadying force for the small, frightened souls under her care.\n\nThe hours stretched long and grey. The sun dipped below the valley's rim, casting the city into a twilight of flickering oil lamps and long, creeping shadows.\n\nBy the time the bells signaled the end of the day's labors, the energy in the Cathedral had shifted. The public areas were locked, the worshippers gone, and the residential wing became a place of hushed whispers and heavy footsteps.\n\nEliza and Cecilia finally reunited in their shared dormitory on the fourth floor. The room was small and austere, the air smelling of old lavender and stone. It was a space of absolute order: two narrow beds, two small wardrobes, and a single window that looked out over the distant, smog-choked industrial district.\n\nThe ritual of the evening was as precise as any liturgical service. There was no talking, only the soft rustle of fabric.\n\nFollowing the strict modesty codes of the *Sanctae Temperantia*, the two girls stood back-to-back in the center of the room. It was a practiced choreography of repression; by standing spine-to-spine, they ensured that no part of their bodies was exposed to the other's gaze during the transition from public habit to private sleepwear.\n\nEliza felt the coolness of the evening air hit her skin as she carefully slid the heavy black robes from her shoulders. The fabric pooled at her feet with a soft *thud*. She felt a sudden, sharp vulnerability, the sensation of the air on her skin feeling almost like a transgression. She moved slowly, her movements hesitant, her mind still looping back to the touch of the herb-monger's fingers and the predatory gaze of Father Gaspard.\n\nBehind her, she could hear Cecilia’s movements—the rhythmic, contented sound of her roommate shedding her own robes, the soft hum that always seemed to accompany Cecilia’s existence. To Cecilia, this was simply the end of a productive day. To Eliza, it felt like the stripping away of a shield.\n\nThey shifted simultaneously, reaching for their night-gowns. These were full-length, high-collared garments of coarse, bleached white linen that covered everything from the throat to the ankles. They were designed to erase the body, to turn the wearer into a blank slate of purity and submission.\n\nAs Eliza pulled the white linen over her head and smoothed the fabric down, she felt the mask return, but this time it was made of cloth. She stepped away from Cecilia, her gaze drifting toward the window.\n\nThe room was dim, the only light coming from a single, flickering candle on the bedside table. In the silence, the distance between the two girls felt wider than the room itself. They were back-to-back, they were roommates, they were friends—but as Eliza climbed into her bed, she felt as though she were drifting further and further away into a dark, solitary sea.",
