    "2026-05-09T02:45:32.426Z": {
      "msg_index": 16,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe moonlight was a cold, clinical light, stripping the room of its warmth and leaving everything in stark, silver relief. Eliza felt a sudden, violent shiver rake through her, despite the thick layers of the nightgown and the heavy blanket tucked around her. It wasn't the cold of the stone walls, but a chill that seemed to originate from the center of her own marrow—a reaction to the vulnerability of the moment.\n\n*Wrong?* she thought, her pulse drumming a frantic rhythm against the mattress. *Nobody's supposed to think anything's wrong. To admit something is wrong is to admit a flaw in the design. To admit a leak.*\n\nThe silence stretched, thick and suffocating, until the pressure became unbearable. She could feel Cecilia’s gaze—that earnest, soft, gold-brown gaze—waiting for an answer. Eliza’s mind raced, scouring for a safe response. She couldn't speak of the herb-monger; that was a sin of the flesh. She couldn't speak of the visceral revulsion she felt for Father Gaspard; that was a sin of the spirit. To tell Cecilia the truth was to hand her a weapon that, even if used with the best intentions, would eventually find its way to a Confessor or an Inquisitor.\n\nA cold knot of fear tightened in her gut, a physical manifestation of the trap she felt she was in. There was only one thing she could say—one truth that was acceptable, one fear that was sanctioned by the Church’s own laws. It was a truth that Cecilia would recognize, but would not truly *understand*.\n\nWith a small, defeated sigh that sounded like a piece of glass cracking, Eliza turned her head to look at Cecilia’s silhouette. In the silver light, Cecilia’s white wool glowed with a soft, ethereal prettiness, a beacon of purity that made Eliza feel suddenly, acutely dirty.\n\n\"You know that... that my birthday is coming up in a few months...?\" she whispered, her voice trembling, the formal ecclesiastical cadence failing her.\n\nShe heard the soft rustle of the sheets as Cecilia nodded, the movement almost rhythmic. \"Yes,\" Cecilia replied softly. \"Mine is too, come to think of it. Why?\"\n\nEliza closed her eyes and sighed again, a heavy, dragging sound. *She just... doesn't get it.* She wanted to scream that the \"blessing\" was a cage, that the \"duty\" was a sentence. But she tried to bridge the gap, to explain the terror that kept her awake while the rest of the city slept.\n\n\"Cecilia,\" Eliza whispered, her voice cracking, \"you know what will happen if I'm not married by then...?\"\n\nThere was a pause, a brief moment of Cecilia’s instinctive, uncomplicated faith. \"Of course,\" Cecilia answered, her tone comforting, almost maternal. \"But... but that's nothing to be worried about. It's a part of every woman's journey toward the species' survival. It is a holy obligation, Eliza. A gift of the Church.\"\n\nThe words felt like stones being dropped into a deep well. Each \"holy\" and \"gift\" sounded like a joke told in a graveyard.\n\nThe dissonance became too much. The mask didn't just slip; it shattered. Eliza rolled away from Cecilia, her movements sudden and jagged, pulling her knees up to her chest in a fetal position. She wanted to curl into a ball so small that the world could no longer find her.\n\n\"Just stop, Cecilia... please,\" she pleaded, her voice a raw, breathless whisper.\n\nA small, choked sound escaped Cecilia—a gasp of confusion and genuine distress. The sheep-girl was floundering, her map of the world failing her for the first time.\n\n\"I'm... I'm sorry, Eliza,\" Cecilia stammered, her voice losing its steadiness. \"I just... I don't understand. Does it... does it have something to do with Father Gaspard? I... I'm sure he's only worried for you. He is an Examiner, after all. His only goal is to ensure you are ready.\"\n\nEliza felt a sob rise in her throat, a hot, suffocating wave of grief and anger. She clamped her jaw shut, fighting the urge to let the sound out. If she cried, she was weak. If she screamed, she was mad. She waited, breathing through the tremor in her chest, until she could trust her voice to be a weapon rather than a plea.\n\nWhen she finally spoke, her voice was a cold, sharp blade, devoid of its usual deference.\n\n\"Not every shepherd is kind to their flock, Cecilia.\"\n\nShe said no more. She stayed curled in her ball, staring at the indigo shadows on the wall, the silence returning—but this time, it was a silence of a wall that had been built, a wall that Cecilia could not climb.",
