    "2026-05-09T02:49:51.813Z": {
      "msg_index": 17,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe words hit her with the force of a physical blow, leaving her breathless. *Not every shepherd is kind to their flock.*\n\nThe sentence hung in the air, poisonous and jagged. To Cecilia, the phrase was more than just a statement; it was a heresy. It was a denial of the very foundation upon which her world was built. The shepherds—the priests, the examiners, the inquisitors—were the architects of the light. They were the ones who held the map. To suggest that one of them was *unkind* was like suggesting the sun was cold or the river flowed uphill.\n\nFor a long moment, Cecilia didn't move. She remained frozen on her back, staring at the ceiling, her heart hammering a frantic, confused rhythm. The silence in the room had changed. It was no longer the silence of a secret, but the silence of a void.\n\n*She doesn't mean it,* Cecilia told herself, the thought a desperate, clinging hope. *She's just... she's just frightened. The stress of the coming months has made her confused. She's just projecting her anxiety onto Father Gaspard.*\n\nShe tried to imagine the world Eliza was describing—a world where the shepherds were wolves—and found she couldn't. It was a landscape that didn't exist in her mind's eye.\n\n\"Eliza...\" Cecilia whispered, her voice trembling. She shifted, turning on her side to face the curled-up silhouette of her roommate. \"You can't say things like that. Not even in private. It... it isn't right. It isn't *true*.\"\n\nShe reached out, her hand hovering just inches from Eliza's shoulder, wanting to offer comfort but fearing the jagged edge of Eliza's current mood.\n\n\"Father Gaspard is a senior member of the order,\" Cecilia continued, her voice regaining some of its steady, instructional quality. \"He has seen a thousand acolytes. He knows exactly what we need, even when it feels harsh. Discipline *is* kindness, Eliza. The pruning of a vine is a harsh act, but it is the only way the fruit can grow. If he is stern with you, it is because he sees a potential in you that you are too blind to see yourself.\"\n\nShe paused, her brow furrowing. \"Please, just... tell me what he did. If he said something that hurt your feelings, I can help you phrase a prayer of apology. Maybe if you show him more submission, he'll be gentler.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Cecilia felt a strange, flickering sensation in her chest. For the first time, she felt a shadow of the fear Eliza was experiencing—not fear *of* the Church, but fear *for* her friend. She realized that if Eliza continued to harbor these thoughts—this \"unkindness\" of the shepherds—she was in grave danger.\n\n*If an Examiner hears this... if a Confessor notices this rot...*\n\nSuddenly, the protective instinct flared. Cecilia didn't see the \"wolf\" in the shepherd; she saw a \"wolf\" in Eliza's mind—a parasite of doubt that was eating away at her friend's piety.\n\n\"You have to be careful, Eliza,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice now urgent, almost pleading. \"You have to let go of these thoughts. Please. For your own sake. If you keep this inside, it will only grow. You have to let the Church help you cleanse it.\"\n\nShe waited for a response, but the room remained silent. Eliza didn't move. She remained a tight, white knot of linen and grief, curled away from the light.\n\nCecilia lay back down, her eyes closing, but sleep would not come. She felt as though she were lying next to a stranger, a person she had known for years but who had suddenly become a foreigner in her own bed. She stayed awake for a long time, listening to the distant, lonely honking of geese migrating south over the city, wondering when the girl she loved as a sister had disappeared, and who had been left in her place.",
