    "2026-05-09T03:32:57.288Z": {
      "msg_index": 18,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe room remained a sanctuary of silence, but for Eliza, that silence had become a suffocating weight. Beside her, Cecilia’s breathing had slowed, shifting into the rhythmic, deep cadence of the truly content. The sheep-girl had drifted off into the dreams of the purehearted—dreams of golden light, orderly gardens, and the unwavering approval of the High Inquisitor. For Cecilia, the world was a puzzle that fit perfectly together; she slept because she believed the darkness was merely a pause between two days of service.\n\nEliza, however, remained awake, her eyes wide and staring into the indigo void of the room.\n\nShe lay there, frozen in her fetal position, feeling the slow, agonizing erosion of her own safety. Tonight had been a series of punctures. The predatory hunger of Gaspard’s gaze had stripped her bare; the casual, knowing observation of the raccoon-merchant had exposed her secrets; and finally, the well-meaning, blind piety of Cecilia had severed the last thread of genuine connection she possessed.\n\n*I have lost her,* she thought, a single, hot tear tracking a path down her cheek.\n\nIt wasn't that Cecilia had abandoned her; it was worse. Cecilia had simply failed to see her. The gap between them—the distance between a person who *believes* and a person who *performs*—had become an abyss. In trying to reach out, Eliza had only discovered how truly alone she was. The shield she had spent years forging—the mask of the invisible, obedient ghost—wasn't just cracked; it was crumbling. The world was suddenly too observant, too keen, and she felt as though she were standing naked in the center of the nave for all to see.\n\nThen, the grief shifted, turning from a sharp pain into a hollow, aching hunger.\n\nShe thought of the world beyond the walls, the world that existed in the margins of the old texts she wasn't supposed to read. She imagined a life where her heart didn't have to be a fortress. She dreamed of a place where \"belonging\" wasn't a matter of compliance, but a natural state of being. She imagined a home where she could hold a woman's hand without it being a death sentence; a family that loved the parts of her that the Church sought to prune away. The freedom to be herself—not a vessel, not a tool, not a piece of property—felt like a phantom limb, something she had never possessed but could feel pulsing with a dull, distant ache.\n\nA sudden, violent surge of desperation rose in her chest. For a fleeting, manic second, she wanted to scream. She wanted to lunge across the bed and throttle the serene, sleeping Cecilia for her blissful ignorance. She wanted to shatter the window and throw herself into the smog-choked valley below, just to feel the wind rip the breath from her lungs one last time before the end.\n\nBut she didn't move.\n\nShe couldn't. The habit of repression was a chain that ran deeper than the skin. She stayed curled in her ball, her muscles locked in a state of rigid tension. She reached up with a trembling hand and silently brushed away the tears, her movements frantic and hushed, terrified that the sound of her sobbing might wake the girl beside her. To wake Cecilia would be to invite more \"guidance,\" more prayers for a \"cleansing\" she didn't want.\n\nShe lay there for hours, the indigo of the room slowly bleeding into the grey of the pre-dawn light. She fought the turmoil, the anger, and the longing until the mental exhaustion became a physical weight, dragging her down. Her thoughts began to fray, the images of the market and the Cathedral blurring into a grey smudge.\n\nFinally, broken by the sheer effort of surviving her own mind, Eliza slipped into an unconsciousness that felt less like sleep and more like a surrender.",
