      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\n\nThe washhouse was a sanctuary of the discarded. In the heavy, humid air, the social castes of the city blurred into a single, sweating mass of labor. Here, the people the Temperantia preferred to forget—the broken, the shunned, the biologically \"unfit\"—toiled in the steam. They were the invisible hands that kept the city's linens white and its robes pristine, their identities erased by the thick, clinging mist. To most, this place was a chore, a damp purgatory of lye and labor. To Eamon, it was a filtering system.\n\nHe scanned the room, his amber eyes cutting through the white haze. And then, he saw her.\n\nShe was a mountain of pristine white scales amidst the grey-brown grime of the washhouse. Verali stood near one of the great boiling vats, her imposing frame casting a long shadow over the smaller workers around her. Even in the utilitarian environment, she possessed a quiet, terrifying grace. Her golden eyes were steady, her movements fluid and slow, a stark contrast to the frantic scrubbing of the humans around her. She was a goddess masquerading as a servant, a dragon-kin playing the part of a pariah with a dignity that could make a man feel small just by standing in her presence.\n\nEamon felt a familiar, grounding warmth in his chest. To the city, she was a freak, a biological error to be tolerated in the shadows. To him, she was the Anchor.\n\nHe didn't approach her directly—that would be too obvious, too loud. Instead, he sidled through the steam, mirroring the rhythms of the other workers, until he was close enough to lean in. He waited for a moment when the head washer was distracted by a spilling bucket of lye, then he caught Verali's eye.\n\n\"You're making the soap look lazy, Matron,\" he murmured, his voice a low, conversational drawl that barely rose above the bubbling of the vats.\n\nVerali didn't turn her head sharply; she simply shifted her gaze, those liquid gold eyes reflecting the steam. A small, knowing tilt of her head was her only answer.\n\nEamon gestured with a subtle flick of his paw toward a narrow alcove behind a stack of drying racks, a blind spot where the steam was thickest and the noise of the machinery drowned out speech. He didn't need to ask; she understood. With a grace that belied her size, Verali stepped away from the vats, her long, muscular tail trailing behind her, and followed him into the dimness of the alcove.\n\nAs soon as they were shielded from prying eyes, the atmosphere changed. The humidity felt less like a weight and more like a cocoon. Eamon stopped, his usual playful air softening into something more focused, more sincere.\n\n\"I saw a girl yesterday,\" Eamon began, his voice a hushed, conspiratorial whisper. \"A little bird in a black habit. An acolyte.\"\n\nHe paused, his dark amber eyes searching Verali's expression. \"She's... leaking, Matron. Not the kind of leak that can be patched with a prayer. I saw her look at a woman in the market—really *look* at her. It was a hunger I haven't seen in years. Raw. Terrified. The kind of thing that makes a person realize they've been starving in the dark for twenty years.\"\n\nEamon leaned back against the damp stone wall, his tail giving a thoughtful twitch. \"She's a prime candidate. The kind of soul that would thrive under your light. But...\" He paused, the gravity of the situation settling in. \"She's inside the walls. She's an acolyte of the Temperantia. Bringing her in isn't just a rescue; it's an infiltration. If she's compromised, or if she's being watched by an Examiner... she could be a thread that leads the Inquisition straight to our door.\"\n\nHe looked up at the Matron, his expression open and requesting. He didn't want to move without her blessing; the risk was too high, and the soul was too precious.\n\n\"I'm thinking of making a move,\" he whispered. \"A gentle nudge. A sign that there's a door open if she's brave enough to walk through it. But I wanted to ask you first. Do we have room for a girl who's still wearing the robes of the enemy? Do you think she's worth the risk of the fire?\"",
