    "2026-05-09T20:02:04.441Z": {
      "msg_index": 21,
      "name": "Verali",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\n\nThe alcove was a world of grey shadows and white vapor, a pocket of stillness amidst the churning industry of the washhouse. The air here smelled of wet stone and caustic soda, a scent that usually signified the stripping away of impurities. Now, it felt as though the stakes were being stripped bare as well.\n\nVerali remained still, her golden eyes fixed on Eamon. She didn't answer immediately; she lived in the spaces between words, knowing that haste was the tool of the desperate. Eamon's instinct for finding \"leaks\" was legendary within the cell, and his gut had never steered them wrong. Yet, the nature of this particular find was different. An acolyte wasn't just a citizen; she was a piece of the machine.\n\n*The girl is not the danger,* Verali mused, her tongue flicking briefly, tasting the heavy, metallic air. *The danger is the hand that holds the leash. If she is being groomed by an Examiner, or if her piety is a mask for something more sinister, she is not a rescue—she is a lure.*\n\nThe risk was not merely the girl's soul, but the very existence of the cell. One wrong move, one slip of a tongue in a confession booth, and the anchor they had built could be dragged into the light of the Inquisition.\n\n\"I'd like to think about it, Eamon,\" she said finally, her voice a melodic, low-frequency hum that seemed to vibrate in the damp air. \"And maybe see her for myself first. Do you know if that's possible?\"\n\nEamon tilted his head, his amber eyes narrowing as he mentally sifted through the rhythms of the market and the movements of the Cathedral's staff. He shifted his weight, a piece of string in his paw that he began to twist absent-mindedly.\n\n\"To tell the truth, I'd never really noticed her before,\" he admitted, his voice returning to that effortless, conversational drawl. \"And I'm not privy to the church's work-schedules. I don't exactly have a key to the archives.\" He paused, a small, calculating smile touching his lips. \"But... maybe I can make something happen. A misplaced note, a coincidental meeting. I can be very persuasive when I'm being unremarkable.\"\n\nVerali’s expression didn't soften. She stepped slightly closer, her white scales shimmering under the dim light, her presence filling the small space. She didn't move to threaten, but the protective instinct of the Matron was a heavy thing.\n\n\"Don't put yourself in danger, Eamon,\" she cautioned, her voice soft but imbued with an unbreakable gravity. \"Or her, for that matter. It isn't worth a life if the price is the collapse of the sanctuary.\"\n\nEamon looked away, his gaze drifting toward the billowing steam of the vats. The playful mask slipped for a moment, revealing the raw, quiet conviction that drove him.\n\n\"It could be worth it to *her*,\" he replied in a low, tight voice. He looked back at Verali, his amber eyes reflecting a deep, empathetic ache. \"Can you even imagine what it must be like to live under their thumb... to be told every day that your very nature is a leak, that your desire is a sin...?\"\n\nHe trailed off, the words catching in his throat. He looked away again, a flicker of embarrassment crossing his features. \"Sorry, Matron. Of course you know. I'm stating the obvious.\"\n\nVerali let out a slow, heavy sigh, a sound like the wind moving through an ancient cave. She didn't need to be reminded of the weight of the Church's thumb; she felt it every time she stepped outside her door, every time she filed her talons blunt to avoid being seen as a predator. But she also knew that compassion, when left unchecked, could become a blind spot.\n\n\"Nevermind, Eamon,\" she said, her voice returning to its steady, maternal warmth. She stepped back, yielding the space of the alcove. \"Do what you think is best. But I want you to take the safety of *everyone* in mind—not just hers. We are a chain, Eamon. If one link breaks, we all fall.\"",
