      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\nEamon’s gaze lingered on the Matron for a second longer than necessary, his expression reflecting a rare, somber clarity. The playfulness that usually served as his armor had been stripped away by the humidity and the gravity of their conversation. He knew the stakes; he knew that in the eyes of the Inquisition, a \"mistake\" wasn't a lapse in judgment—it was a heresy punishable by erasure.\n\n\"I'll be careful, Matron,\" he said, his voice devoid of its usual drawl, sounding instead like a promise made in a sanctuary. \"And if I *do* slip-up, I'll make sure it only comes back to me.\"\n\nIt was a dangerous sentiment, one that would have made any other recruiter shudder, but Eamon spoke it with a quiet, absolute certainty. He had spent a decade becoming a shadow; he believed he knew how to disappear if the wind turned cold.\n\nHe took a step out of the alcove, the thick, alkaline steam swallowing him once more as he merged back into the flow of the laboring masses. He paused just as he reached the edge of the drying racks, glancing back over his shoulder. The Matron stood as a white monolith in the grey mist, her golden eyes watching him with a mixture of hope and caution.\n\n\"If I can arrange something, I'll let you know,\" he added, his voice regaining a hint of that familiar, conversational lightness. \"But just to be clear on the logistics... do you just want to see her, or actually talk to her?\"\n\nVerali didn't answer immediately. She looked toward the boiling vats, where the steam rose in great, churning columns, mimicking the chaotic currents of the city's spiritual life. She thought of the girl—the \"little bird\"—and the precariousness of her position. To speak to an acolyte was to offer a hand; to see her was merely to observe the wound.\n\n\"Just see her for now, I think,\" Verali replied, her voice a low, resonant hum that seemed to settle the air around them. \"You should get a second impression as well, Eamon. First impressions are often the same as the mask we want to see. The second one... that's where the truth usually hides.\"\n\nEamon gave a short, sharp nod of agreement. The logic was sound; he was a man of patterns, and patterns required a second data point to be confirmed.\n\n\"Understood,\" he murmured. \"I'll keep my eyes open. And my mouth shut.\"\n\nHe turned and walked away, his figure gradually dissolving into the white haze of the washhouse. As he stepped out of the heavy wooden doors and back into the crisp, golden light of the autumn morning, the scent of crude oil and dead leaves rushed to meet him. Eamon breathed it in deeply, his amber eyes already scanning the horizon toward the Cathedral.\n\nThe game was set. The target was identified. Now, all that remained was the slow, patient art of the nudge.",
