      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\nThe days that followed were marked by the rhythmic, suffocating repetition of the Cathedral's calendar. Thursday and Friday passed in a blur of incense, heavy black wool, and the whispered prayers of the faithful. For the acolytes, the week was a cycle of ritual cleaning and silent submission, a slow grinding down of the spirit to fit the shape of the vessel.\n\nThroughout the city, the autumn air grew sharper, the gold of the foliage deepening into a bruised crimson. The smog remained a constant, a heavy blanket of crude-oil soot that settled on every ledge and clung to every lung, reminding the populace that their survival was bought with a dirty, choking currency.\n\nSaturday arrived with the gravity of a focal point. The weekend services were the peak of the Church's social machinery, a time when the flock gathered in the nave to be reminded of their inadequacies and the necessity of their repression. The air in the Cathedral was thick with the scent of melted wax and the heavy, floral musk of liturgical oils, a smell that aimed to elevate the soul but often felt like it was merely masking the scent of old stone and fear.\n\nAs the service concluded and the congregation began to bleed out into the streets, the Great Doors of the nave groaned open. The worshippers filed out in a disciplined stream, their faces masks of practiced piety, their footsteps echoing on the cold stone of the Narthex.\n\nThrough this tide of black and white robes, a different kind of presence arrived.\n\nEamon stepped through the front doors, his movements fluid and inconspicuous. He didn't enter with the bowed head of a penitent or the rigid posture of a believer; he entered as a ghost in the machine, his amber eyes already scanning the environment for patterns and vulnerabilities. He didn't rush. He didn't linger. He moved with a curated purpose, his ringed tail giving a subtle, calculating twitch.\n\nHe paused in the center of the Narthex, beneath the towering, vaulted ceiling and the banks of iron candle-stands. He slowed his pace, shifting his weight, and began to look around with a look of mild, harmless confusion—the expression of a man who had misplaced a trinket or forgotten a detail. He looked at the notice boards, then at the offering boxes, his gaze drifting as if searching for something small and elusive on the floor.\n\nHe didn't look for the acolytes; he looked for the *Staff*.\n\nOnce he spotted a member of the Church personnel—a figure of authority but not too high in the hierarchy to be unreachable—Eamon adjusted his posture. He softened his expression into one of gentle, slightly embarrassed apology. He stepped forward, closing the distance with an unremarkable stride, and approached the staff member, his mouth opening to speak.",
