      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\n\nThe service had ended, but the air in the Narthex remained heavy, stagnant with the lingering scent of expensive incense and the oppressive weight of a thousand suppressed desires. Gaspard stood near the edge of the nave, his charcoal-grey robes smelling of stale wine and the faint, metallic tang of the dragon-kin essence he’d indulged in before the service. He was in a foul mood. The sermon had been adequate, but the congregation's faces—those blank, terrified masks of submission—had bored him.\n\nHe was adjusting the damp handkerchief in his hand, wiping a bead of sweat from his bulbous nose, when he felt a presence enter his personal space.\n\nHe looked down. Standing before him was a raccoon. A small, wiry thing dressed in the nondescript, drab clothing of a commoner. A lowborn, clearly. The creature didn't have the trembling deference Gaspard usually enjoyed; instead, he possessed a loose, unhurried ease that the priest found instantly grating. It was the ease of someone who didn't fear the shadow of the cross, and that, to Gaspard, was a scent of heresy.\n\n\"Excuse me, Father,\" the raccoon began, his voice a soft, conversational drawl.\n\nGaspard let out a wheezing sigh, his beady hazel eyes narrowing. \"Speak quickly, child. My time is not a charity.\"\n\nThe raccoon began to spin a yarn—something about a lost note. A scrap of paper, perhaps, containing a family matter or a misplaced bit of business. The priest only half-listened, his mind already drifting toward the administrative duties he’d rather avoid. He nodded appropriately, the movement mechanical and devoid of warmth, his jaw shifting with a slow, rhythmic chewing motion.\n\n\"No, I haven't seen it,\" Gaspard wheezed, the sound like a bellows full of gravel. \"And I cannot possibly be expected to track every stray piece of parchment that flutters through these hallowed halls. The wind, perhaps?\"\n\nThe raccoon continued, seeming to grow more desperate. He mentioned the cleaning staff—the acolytes who scrubbed the stone and polished the brass.\n\n\"Well...\" Gaspard paused, his bulbous nose twitching. He wanted this creature gone. The raccoon's presence was a smudge on his carefully maintained aura of ecclesiastical authority. He didn't want to be the one to guide this lowborn through the labyrinth of the Cathedral; it was a waste of his superior intellect.\n\nHe cast his gaze around the Narthex, scanning the crowd for someone—anyone—who could take this 'person' off his hands. His eyes landed on a familiar, soft shape.\n\nCecilia.\n\nThe sheep-girl was standing nearby, her expression one of serene, uncomplicated devotion, her white wool glowing in the dim light of the foyer. She was the perfect tool: reliable, pious, and fundamentally incapable of saying no to a superior.\n\n\"Sister Cecilia!\" Gaspard barked, his voice a guttural snarl that sliced through the ambient noise of the departing crowd.\n\nCecilia jumped, her head snapping toward him, her expression instantly shifting into one of alert submission. She hurried toward him, her footsteps light and hurried. \"Yes, Father Gaspard?\"\n\nGaspard didn't look at her; he looked at the raccoon with a flicker of barely concealed annoyance. He leaned in too close to the raccoon's face, invading his space with a smell of old sweat and nicotine.\n\n\"This... parishioner has lost something,\" Gaspard wheezed, his tone shifting into a forced, academic formality. \"A note. A trifle. He is quite distressed. Assist him, will you? Ensure he finds his little scrap of paper so he may find peace.\"\n\nBefore Cecilia could even offer an acknowledgment, Gaspard stepped back, his robes swirling around his ankles.\n\n\"Now, if you'll excuse me,\" he added, his voice returning to a grating, demanding tone as he turned away. \"I have urgent business with the High Inquisitor. Matters of... spiritual purification.\"\n\nWithout a second glance, he retreated hastily toward the residential block, leaving the raccoon and the sheep-girl standing together in the silver light of the Narthex. He didn't look back; he was already thinking about the bottles of wine he kept in his dresser.",
