    "2026-05-10T22:20:22.749Z": {
      "msg_index": 25,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\n\nAs Father Gaspard retreated, his robes billowing behind him like a charcoal cloud, Eamon felt a surge of quiet triumph. The priest was exactly as he'd expected: a man of appetites and arrogance, so blinded by his own self-importance that he was practically begging to be manipulated. Eamon watched the man waddle away, a small, inward smile tugging at the corner of his mouth. *The fat old wolf is far too busy chasing his own tail to notice a raccoon in the hen house,* he thought.\n\nNow, the real work began.\n\nHe turned his attention to Sister Cecilia. Up close, she was every bit the \"perfect\" acolyte. Her white wool was impeccably groomed, and her expression was one of such earnest, fragile kindness that it almost made Eamon feel a twinge of guilt. Almost.\n\nHe shifted his weight, letting his shoulders slump slightly, and allowed his expression to soften into a look of gentle, heartbreaking vulnerability. He didn't want to look like a beggar; he wanted to look like a man who had lost the only thing that mattered.\n\n\"Thank you, Sister,\" he began, his voice a low, melodic drawl that sounded like a sigh. \"I'm sorry to bother you. I know you have your duties, and I'm sure the Father's request was... a bit abrupt.\"\n\nHe paused, glancing around the Narthex as if the very air were holding a secret. He began to weave the tale, his words flowing with a natural, unhurried rhythm.\n\n\"It's a scrap of paper. Small. Just a bit of parchment with some ink on it,\" he explained, his amber eyes searching Cecilia's face for a flicker of empathy. \"But it holds something... well, it's a bit of a personal matter. A message from someone who isn't here anymore. It's the only thing I have left of them, really. I must have dropped it while I was praying in the nave, or perhaps as I was walking through the doors.\"\n\nHe let out a soft, shaky breath, a carefully timed piece of theater. \"I've looked everywhere, but the Cathedral is so vast, and the crowds... they just sweep everything away. I'm worried some kind of helpful soul might have picked it up, thinking it was trash, or perhaps they're holding onto it, waiting for someone to claim it.\"\n\nHe leaned in a fraction, not crowding her, but creating a sense of shared intimacy in the cold, echoing space of the foyer.\n\n\"Surely, someone saw it? Someone who knows these halls better than I do?\" He looked at her with a hopeful, pleading gaze. \"Please, Sister. If you could just ask around... maybe the others who were cleaning the pews? I'd be more than happy to pay for the trouble, or... well, I just want it back. I can't imagine the thought of it just... vanishing into the soot.\"\n\nHe didn't push. He didn't demand. He simply stood there, the picture of a grieving, unremarkable man, waiting for Cecilia's innate, uncomplicated desire to be helpful to take the lead. He was fishing, and he knew the bait—the idea of a lost, precious memory—was something a heart like Cecilia's couldn't possibly ignore.",
