    "2026-05-10T22:27:01.521Z": {
      "msg_index": 26,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\nAs Eamon spoke, Cecilia felt a genuine, warm ache in her chest. The idea of a precious memory—a scrap of paper being the only remaining link to a lost loved one—struck a chord of deep, instinctive sympathy within her. She didn't see a stranger; she saw a soul in distress, and in Cecilia's world, the highest calling of an acolyte was to be a vessel of comfort for the weary.\n\nShe looked at the raccoon, her brown eyes softening with a tender, uncomplicated sadness. She could almost picture the missing note, the fading ink, the grief of its loss. It felt like a small tragedy, the kind that required a gentle hand to resolve.\n\n\"Oh, you poor thing,\" she murmured, her voice hushed and full of genuine concern. She stepped a bit closer, her wool shifting softly. \"I... I really wish I could help you, sir, but I haven't seen *anything* like that today. I would certainly tell you if I had. I've been keeping a very close eye on the floors while I worked.\"\n\nShe gave him a sad, supportive smile, the kind of look a shepherd gives to a lost lamb. She truly wanted to solve this for him; the thought of him leaving the Cathedral empty-handed, burdened by a lingering grief, felt wrong. It was an imbalance in the order of things.\n\nEamon let out a small, helpless sigh, his shoulders slumping as if the weight of the loss had finally broken him. He looked down at his paws, his voice trailing off into a whisper. \"Well... perhaps... someone else did? Isn't there anyone else I could ask? I'd feel better knowing I'd tried everything.\"\n\nCecilia paused, her mind whirring. She began to scan the mental map of the Cathedral's staff, thinking of who had been stationed in the nave and narthex during the final hour of the service. Most of the acolytes were tucked away in the sacristy or the residential wing, but there was one person who was almost always assigned to the cleanup of the public areas.\n\nThe thought of Eliza sparked a sudden, bright realization. Eliza was quiet, observant, and lived in the margins of the Cathedral's daily life—she was the one most likely to notice a stray piece of parchment tucked under a pew or caught in a draft by the doors.\n\n\"Perhaps!\" Cecilia said, her expression brightening instantly, a spark of hope returning to her eyes. \"I can't promise anything, and she's very shy, but let me go fetch her. She's the one who usually handles the nave's fine-cleaning. She has a very keen eye for things others miss.\"\n\nShe looked back at Eamon, her voice encouraging and warm. \"Wait right here. Don't move an inch. I'll go find her and bring her back to you. Just stay right here in the Narthex, and we'll see what she says.\"\n\nWith a final, reassuring nod, Cecilia turned on her heel. She hurried away toward the residential corridor, her habit fluttering behind her, her mind already imagining how relieved the poor man would be if Eliza had found his treasure. She didn't see the raccoon's amber eyes tracking her departure with a calculated, patient intensity; she only saw a mission of mercy.",
