    "2026-05-10T22:39:05.239Z": {
      "msg_index": 27,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\n\nThe stairwells of the residential block were cold, smelling of damp limestone and the faint, lingering scent of old wax. Eliza descended the steps with a slow, rhythmic cadence, her eyes fixed on the worn stone beneath her feet. She moved with the practiced invisibility of a shadow, her mind drifting back to the suffocating silence of the dormitory and the distance that now lay between her and Cecilia. Every step felt like a descent into a world she no longer trusted, a world where every gaze was a potential judgment.\n\nShe had barely reached the landing of the ground floor when a blur of white wool nearly collided with her.\n\n\"Oh! Eliza!\" Cecilia gasped, her chest heaving, her face flushed from the exertion of her sudden sprint. She stopped abruptly, bending over with her hands on her knees, gulping air for a few seconds as she struggled to reclaim her breath. The sound of her labored breathing echoed in the narrow hallway, a frantic contrast to the stillness Eliza had been cultivating.\n\nFinally, Cecilia stood back up, her expression radiating a mixture of urgency and excitement. \"There's a... a...\" she paused, blinking, \"a person in the nave who needs to speak with you.\"\n\nEliza stopped, her brow furrowing. A request for her presence in the public areas was rare; usually, she was the one clearing the path for others, not the destination. The suddenness of the request sent a flicker of anxiety through her.\n\n\"Who is it?\" Eliza asked, her voice soft and breathless, the formal, cautious tone of an acolyte carefully maintained.\n\nCecilia shook her head, her curls bouncing. \"I'm not sure, I didn't get his name, but he's lost something here in the cathedral. He seems quite distressed, Eliza. I think he's really suffering.\"\n\nEliza felt a wave of reluctant pity, though it was overshadowed by a mounting sense of dread. The \"distressed\" were usually the ones who brought the most trouble—either by attracting the attention of the Inquisition or by demanding a level of empathy that the Church discouraged. However, Cecilia's earnestness was a force that was difficult to resist.\n\n\"Oh... well, alright,\" Eliza replied, her tone deferential. \"I will assist if I can.\"\n\nShe began to move toward the nave, with Cecilia trotting close behind, the two of them passing through the heavy doors of the rear transept hallway. As they emerged from the transition zone and stepped into the vast, echoing space of the nave, the silver light of the Narthex filtered through the high windows, casting long, skeletal shadows across the pews.\n\nEliza’s gaze swept the room, searching for a stranger. And then, she saw him.\n\nShe froze. For two seconds, the world stopped breathing. The air in her lungs turned to ice.\n\nStanding there, leaning with a practiced, loose ease against a pillar, was the raccoon merchant. The same amber eyes, the same ringed tail, the same knowing, quiet presence. He was the one who had watched her. He was the one who had seen her—really *seen* her—in that moment of visceral, forbidden attraction toward the herb-monger's daughter. He had witnessed the \"leak\" in her armor, the raw hunger she had spent a lifetime trying to bury.\n\nHer next breath was thin and shaky, a ragged gasp that she tried to swallow. Her heart hammered against her ribs like a trapped bird, and her knees suddenly felt like jelly, threatening to give way beneath the weight of her own panic. *He knows,* she thought, the realization vibrating through her. *He knows what I am. He saw it.*\n\nBut she could not run. Not with Cecilia watching. Not in the house of the Temperantia.\n\nForcing her legs to move, Eliza maintained the mask. She slowed her pace, her movements becoming stiff and mechanical, as she approached the man. Every instinct screamed at her to flee, but the habit of obedience was a chain that held her fast.\n\nAs she reached him, she stopped a respectful distance away, her hands clasping tightly in the folds of her habit. She looked up at him, her light-blue eyes wide with a mixture of fear and uncertainty.\n\n\"Y-yes?\" she asked, her voice trembling slightly, though she tried to keep it polite and distant. \"Can... can I help you, sir?\"",
