      "msg_index": 28,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Thursday; September; 13\nTime: 6:00am)\n\nAs Eliza approached, Eamon felt the sudden, sharp shift in the atmosphere. He didn't need to see her eyes to know she was terrified; he could smell the spike of adrenaline, the frantic beat of a heart that recognized a predator—or perhaps, a liberator. She stood there, ghost-white and trembling, her hands clutching her habit with a desperation that spoke of a soul trying to hold itself together.\n\n*She remembers me,* he noted, his amber eyes glinting. *She's scared. That means... she has something to hide. Which means... I was right.*\n\n\"Oh, sister! Thank the Mother!\" Eamon exclaimed, his voice a sudden, warm burst of relief.\n\nHe took a few steps forward, his movements fluid and enveloping. He positioned himself subtly, stepping into Eliza’s personal space in a way that seemed accidental but was surgically precise. By shifting his weight and the angle of his body, he effectively blocked Cecilia's line of sight, creating a small, private curtain of space between himself and the acolyte.\n\nCecilia stood a few feet back, beaming with a sense of accomplishment, believing she had successfully bridged the gap between a suffering stranger and a helpful sister.\n\n\"I was just telling Sister Cecilia here that I've been utterly heartbroken,\" Eamon began, his voice returning to that easy, conversational drawl, though it now carried a hidden layer of intensity. \"A scrap of paper, a memory of a person I can never see again... I've walked these halls until my paws were sore, thinking it was gone forever. I thought the wind had taken it, or some careless hand had tossed it into the soot...\"\n\nAs he spoke, Eamon's gaze locked onto Eliza's. He wasn't just telling a story; he was observing the way she breathed, the way her pupils dilated. Halfway through the sentence, he suddenly paused. He looked down at her empty hands, his expression shifting instantly into a mask of wide-eyed surprise.\n\n\"Oh! That's... that's it! You found it after all!\"\n\nIn a motion so swift and practiced it was nearly invisible to anyone but the target, Eamon reached forward. His hand didn't just move; it flickered. Under the cover of his body and the angle of his shadow, he produced two separate pieces of paper that had been concealed in the palm of his hand.\n\nOne piece of paper—the \"lost\" note—he lifted high in the air, waving it in triumph for Cecilia to see. But the other piece, a smaller, different scrap, he pressed firmly and insistently into Eliza's palm. The contact was brief, a sharp, electric touch that felt like a brand against her skin.\n\n\"Oh, you have no idea what this means to me!\" he beamed, his voice loud enough to satisfy Cecilia's sense of a happy ending. \"Thank you, sister... ah... uhm... I'm terribly sorry, I realized I never actually asked... what is your name?\"\n\nEliza stood frozen, her face a mask of pale shock. The paper in her hand felt like a hot coal, a piece of contraband that could bring the Inquisition down upon her head if it were discovered.\n\n\"I'm... uh... I'm... s-sister... Eliza,\" she stammered.\n\nHer voice was a fragile thread, nearly breaking. Eamon didn't miss the way she instinctively, almost reflexively, slid the piece of paper deep into the sleeve of her habit, hiding it against her skin. A small, satisfied smile curled the corner of his mouth. The seed was planted; the door was now unlocked.\n\n\"Well, thank you, Sister Eliza,\" he said, glancing back at the sheep-girl with a wink of simulated gratitude. \"And you too, Sister Cecilia. I won't forget this kindness. Truly.\"\n\nHe gave a polite, sweeping bow of his head—a gesture of respect that felt, to Eliza, like a secret promise. Without another word, he turned and began making his way out of the cathedral, his ringed tail swaying with a rhythmic, contented ease. As he stepped back out into the golden autumn light, he didn't look back. He didn't need to. He had just handed a prisoner the key to her cell, and now, all he had to do was wait for her to turn the lock.",
