      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 15\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\nCecilia practically glowed with a sense of quiet triumph. Seeing the raccoon’s face light up with that sudden, radiant joy when the note was recovered—it was a small victory, but in a world governed by duty and repression, these moments of genuine human connection were the only things that made the long hours of service feel worthwhile. To her, it wasn't just about a piece of parchment; it was about the restoration of a soul's peace. She felt a surge of pride for Eliza, a warmth that she believed was shared.\n\nStill, as she watched the raccoon turn to leave, a tiny, nagging seed of doubt flickered in the back of her mind.\n\n*I wonder why Eliza gasped when she first saw him?* Cecilia mused, her brown eyes tracking the receding figure of the man. *Did they know each other?*\n\nShe paused, analyzing the interaction. *No... that doesn't make sense. He didn't even know her name until just now.* The logic was sound. The gasp must have been a reaction to the suddenness of the encounter or perhaps a momentary lapse in composure. Cecilia brushed the thought aside, treating it like a piece of dust on a polished altar. There was no room for suspicion in a heart full of faith.\n\nAs the raccoon's footsteps echoed faintly against the stone of the Narthex and faded into the autumn wind outside, the silence of the nave returned.\n\nEliza turned back toward the residential section, her movements stiff, almost robotic. She didn't look at Cecilia; she kept her gaze fixed on the grey stone of the corridor. \"I... I should get back to what I was doing,\" she murmured, her voice sounding thin and distant, as if she were speaking from the bottom of a well.\n\nCecilia followed close behind, her footsteps light and rhythmic. She felt an impulse to lean into Eliza, to offer a comforting touch, but she kept a respectful distance, her voice soft and melodic in the echoing hallway.\n\n\"Oh, I'm so glad you were able to help him, Eliza,\" Cecilia said, her tone warm and genuinely appreciative. \"That was so smart of you to hold onto it in case someone came looking. You really do have the best eye for these things. It's a kindness the Father would certainly approve of.\"\n\nEliza didn't stop walking. She didn't lean into the praise. Instead, she gave a weak, flickering smile that didn't reach her eyes—a ghost of a gesture that vanished as quickly as it appeared.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Eliza whispered, her voice trembling slightly as she felt the hidden scrap of paper pressing against her skin, a secret weight that felt heavier than the stone walls around them. \"N-never know what might be important, y'know?\"\n\nThe two acolytes disappeared into the shadows of the residential block, the silence of the Cathedral closing in around them, unaware that the balance of Eliza's world had just shifted irrevocably.",
