    "2026-05-10T23:49:11.373Z": {
      "msg_index": 30,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 15\nTime: 7:30pm)\n\n\nThe remainder of the day was a blur of mechanical devotion. Eliza moved through the Cathedral like a clockwork doll, performing the ritual cleansings and the silent prayers with a precision that bordered on the surgical. But beneath the heavy black and white fabric of her habit, her skin felt as though it were on fire. The scrap of paper was no longer just a piece of parchment; it was a hot stone, a heavy, pulsing weight that seemed to radiate a heat that could be felt in the very marrow of her bones.\n\nEvery time she passed a senior member of the clergy, she felt a jolt of electric terror. She was certain that Father Gaspard could smell the heresy on her, that the Inquisitors could see the secret etched in the tension of her shoulders. She kept her head bowed lower than usual, her voice a mere whisper, playing the part of the humble acolyte with a desperation that made her heart ache.\n\nAfter the evening meal—a bland, tasteless porridge that felt like ash in her mouth—she navigated the corridors of the fourth floor with a wary, flickering gaze. She didn't speak to Cecilia. She couldn't. The sheep-girl’s presence was a reminder of the purity Eliza was currently betraying.\n\nSlipping into the communal washroom, Eliza entered the furthest stall and slid the bolt home with a click that sounded like a gunshot in the sterile silence of the room.\n\nShe leaned her back against the cold stone wall, her chest heaving. The air in the washroom was damp and smelled of lye and old plumbing, a scent that usually brought a sense of order, but now felt suffocating. With shaking hands, she reached into the deep fold of her sleeve and slowly withdrew the paper.\n\nShe unfolded it with the delicacy of someone handling a live explosive.\n\n*'Sometimes those lost in spirit need only a candle to light their path.'*\n\nEliza stared at the words. She read them once, then twice, then a third time, her eyes searching for a hidden code, a map, or a command. It was so vague—so infuriatingly, dangerously vague—that it felt less like a message and more like a riddle. It didn't tell her who he was, what he wanted, or why he had targeted her.\n\nAnd then, the word *candle* clicked into place.\n\n*I bought the candles from him...* she remembered. The market stall. The scent of beeswax and oil. The way he had looked at her—not as a vessel, not as a servant, but as a person.\n\nA wave of nausea hit her, followed by a surge of paralyzing fear. *It's a trap. He's a spy. He's an agent of the Inquisition playing a game. I should burn this. I should pray for forgiveness and forget he ever existed.*\n\nBut as she stared at the blank stone of the stall wall, another thought rose, colder and more honest. *But how can I keep going like this? Is this really how I'll spend the rest of my life?*\n\nShe thought of the \"Biological Deadline,\" the looming shadow of a future where her body was no longer her own, and the predatory glint in Father Gaspard's eyes. The walls of the Cathedral, which had always felt like a protective fortress, suddenly felt like the walls of a tomb.\n\nA small, thin, reedy resolve began to form itself, wriggling out of the barren, salted soil of her independence. It was a fragile thing, a tiny spark in a void, but it was the first thing she had ever owned that didn't belong to the Church.\n\n*I will go,* she decided, her voice a silent, shaking vow in the dark. *I will go speak with the raccoon merchant. I will find out what this is.*\n\nShe knew the risks. She knew that one wrong step, one misplaced word, could lead to her \"cleansing\" or worse. But the alternative—the slow, suffocating death of her soul within these walls—was suddenly more terrifying than the risk of the fire.\n\nHer mind made up, she didn't hesitate. She dropped the paper into the latrine, the white scrap floating for a moment on the surface of the water. She reached out and pulled the heavy iron chain. A roar of water surged into the bowl, a violent, cleansing flood that swept the evidence away, carrying the secret down into the blackness of the city sewers.\n\nEliza stood there for a moment, listening to the water settle, before she adjusted her habit and stepped out of the stall, her face once again the mask of a humble acolyte, but her heart beating with a dangerous, new purpose.",
