      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 16\nTime: 12:00pm)\n\nThe two days that followed the destruction of the note were a slow torture of anticipation. Every hour felt like a minute; every prayer felt like a sentence. She moved through her duties with a frantic, internal energy, her mind a whirlwind of *what-ifs* and *how-tos*. She found herself staring at the heavy oak doors of the Cathedral. She was a prisoner counting the seconds until the cell door creaked open, terrified and electrified all at once.\n\nWhen the opportunity finally arrived—a routine requisition for supplies—the air felt thinner, the world sharper.\n\nAs they stepped beyond the sanctuary's walls, the city rushed in to meet them. The smell of coal smoke, the distant clang of industrial hammers, and the low hum of a populace living in a state of managed anxiety. Beside her, Cecilia walked with her usual, uncomplicated grace, humming a soft liturgical melody that usually brought Eliza peace, but today sounded like a siren warning her of the danger.\n\nAs the market came into view, the colorful stalls and the chaotic bustle of the commercial district blurred into the background. Eliza's eyes were searching, scanning the rows of vendors for a specific shade of grey-brown fur and a pair of amber eyes.\n\nThe panic began to rise. Cecilia was too close. She was always too close.\n\n\"Do you want me to take care of the shopping while you look around, Cecilia? I don't mind,\" Eliza asked. Her voice was soft, breathless, the words tumbling out with a caution that she hoped sounded like generosity.\n\nCecilia paused, glancing at her roommate with a look of genuine, warm affection. She flashed a small, beaming smile, the kind of expression that made her look even more wholesome and trustful. \"That's sweet, Eliza, but I don't want to make a habit of shirking my duty. I'll help today.\"\n\n*Oh no... quick... think of something...*\n\nEliza felt a cold spike of desperation. The \"candle\" was out there, somewhere in the maze of the market, and the only person who could light her path was currently being blocked by a wall of fluffy, well-meaning wool. If she simply walked away, Cecilia would worry. If she stayed, she would remain a ghost in her own life.\n\n\"Oh... well...\" Eliza fumbled, her fingers twisting the heavy fabric of her robe. \"In that case... why don't we... split the list in half? We can each do part of the shopping and be done in half the time.\"\n\nShe tried to keep her voice steady, praying that the tremor of her nerves didn't betray her. She forced a small, deferential smile, though internally she felt a crushing disappointment. Every minute spent with Cecilia was a minute stolen from her own freedom. To halve the list was to halve her time, to shrink the window of her rebellion into a narrow, precarious sliver.\n\nBut it was the only way. She couldn't talk to the raccoon merchant with a sheep-girl hovering over her shoulder, reporting every word back to the Father.\n\n\"It would be more efficient,\" Eliza added, her ecclesiastical phrasing returning as a shield. \"A more... disciplined use of our time, wouldn't it?\"",
