      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nCecilia felt a slight pang of disappointment, a small, hollow feeling in her chest. For her, these outings weren't just about the utilitarian acquisition of soap and candles; they were the only times she and Eliza could truly breathe outside the oppressive weight of the Cathedral's stone walls. She had been looking forward to the walk, imagining the small, whispered conversations and the shared observations of the city's bustle—the kind of innocent gossip that wasn't forbidden, but simply... human.\n\nShe looked at Eliza, noticing the way the other girl seemed to be vibrating with a hidden, frantic energy. For a moment, Cecilia wondered if Eliza was feeling unwell. Perhaps the autumn chill had finally caught up with her, or perhaps she was simply overwhelmed by the crowd.\n\nBut Eliza's suggestion of efficiency was, as always, logically sound. The Church prized discipline above all else, and splitting the list was, by all accounts, the most pious way to handle the task.\n\n\"Well, alright,\" Cecilia conceded, her voice soft and unhurried. \"It does make sense. We can get the errands finished and be back before the evening bells.\"\n\nShe paused, her brown eyes searching Eliza's face, wanting to salvage some of that shared time. A small, hopeful light flickered in her gaze.\n\n\"But maybe when we're done, we can just... sit down for a little while, by the river? Not long, of course!\" She added the caveat quickly, as if reminding herself that leisure was a leak of spiritual energy. \"But I'm sure we'll be ready for a little rest by then, don't you think? The river is so peaceful this time of year, and the air is so fresh.\"\n\nIt was a simple request, a small piece of companionship. To Cecilia, it was a gesture of friendship; to Eliza, it was a ticking clock.\n\nEliza's reaction was immediate. She smiled back and nodded, the gesture quick and almost desperate. \"Alright, it's a deal. It's a lovely day anyway, we may as well enjoy it a little.\"\n\nThe words sounded hollow to a trained ear, but Cecilia wasn't a trained ear. She was a heart, and her heart was full of trust. She saw only a friend agreeing to a peaceful afternoon.\n\nCecilia grinned, the expression warming her entire rounded face, and reached out to accept her torn half of the list. The paper was rough, the ink a utilitarian black, but in her hand, it felt like a contract of friendship.\n\n\"Alright, I'll see you in a little while!\" she chirped, her voice bright and uncomplicated.\n\nShe turned and began to walk toward the apothecary's row, her habit fluttering slightly in the gentle breeze. She didn't look back, leaving Eliza standing alone in the middle of the market, finally freed from her shadow, standing at the precipice of a choice that could either wake her up or destroy her.",
