      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 16\nTime: 12:00pm)\n\nThe moment Cecilia vanished around the corner, the air seemed to rush back into Eliza's lungs. For a few seconds, she simply stood there, her heart hammering against her ribs like a trapped bird. She felt a dizzying sense of vertigo; the market, which had felt like a blur of noise and color, suddenly snapped into a sharp, high-definition clarity. Every cobblestone, every hanging sign, every distant shout of a merchant felt amplified.\n\n*Be normal,* she told herself, the words a frantic mantra. *Just be a simple acolyte. A girl who needs a candle. Nothing more.*\n\nShe tried to slow her pace, forcing herself to wander. She stopped to look at a display of coarse linens, her fingers brushing the fabric without any real interest, her eyes darting toward the perimeter of the square. She consciously relaxed her shoulders, trying to shed the stiffness of the Cathedral’s discipline. She wanted to appear casual, a drifting presence in the crowd, unaware of the rebellion currently screaming in her veins.\n\nBut her feet had a mind of their own. They didn't drift; they navigated. They bypassed the fruit sellers and the iron-mongers, cutting a direct line toward the stall that smelled of old wax and hidden things.\n\nAs she approached the stall, Eliza felt the world shrink. The bustling market noise began to fade into a rhythmic thrum, leaving only the sound of her own shallow breathing. She saw him. Eamon was leaning against a wooden pillar, his amber eyes scanning the crowd with a look of perpetual, mild amusement. He was currently dealing with a customer—a burly man in a leather apron who was arguing over the price of a bundle of twine.\n\nEliza stopped a few feet away. She didn't step up immediately. Instead, she waited, her hands clasped tightly in front of her, her knuckles white. She watched the way Eamon handled the customer—the subtle tilts of his head, the effortless way he redirected the conversation, the fluid grace of his movements. He was a master of the social game, a weaver of perceptions.\n\nWhen the man finally huffed and walked away, leaving the twine on the counter, Eamon didn't immediately look for the next customer. He simply shifted his weight, his gaze drifting slowly toward Eliza.\n\nShe stepped forward. The wood of the counter was scarred and stained, smelling of linseed oil and dust. She didn't look at the goods. She looked at him.\n\nThey said nothing for a long moment. The silence between them felt heavy, an invisible cord stretching tight, vibrating with the shared knowledge of the note and the risk of the moment. Eamon didn't speak; he didn't push. He simply watched her, his eyes searching her face, reading the terror, the longing, and the fragile resolve that she was trying so hard to hide.\n\nNearby, a woman in drab, oversized work-clothes—a heavy grey tunic and a faded shawl—stood leaning against a stone wall, ostensibly examining a basket of withered tubers. She was unremarkable, a grey shadow in a grey city, her eyes hooded and distant. Verali watched the exchange with a stillness that was almost predatory, her gold-slitted eyes tracing the micro-expressions on Eliza's face. She was assessing the weight of the girl's soul, checking for the \"leak\" Eamon had described, weighing the cost of bringing another link into the chain.\n\nEliza felt as if she were standing on the edge of a cliff. The noise of the market returned in a sudden, crashing wave, and the pressure of the silence became unbearable.\n\n\"I... I came for a candle...\" she said, her voice small and breathless, barely more than a whisper.\n\nIt was a simple sentence, a mundane request, but in the context of the secret they shared, it was a declaration of war against the only world she had ever known.",
