    "2026-05-11T02:34:08.469Z": {
      "msg_index": 34,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 16\nTime: 12:00pm)\n\nEamon didn't move for a heartbeat, letting the silence stretch just long enough to make Eliza feel the weight of her own boldness. Then, a slow, knowing smile crept across his face—not a grin of triumph, but something softer, almost sympathetic. He leaned in, the scent of old beeswax and something metallic clinging to him, his voice dropping to a low, intimate register that effectively walled off the rest of the market.\n\n\"Don't be embarrassed, lass,\" he murmured, the drawl in his voice acting like a velvet glove. \"I've made it my business to watch people. To see the things they think they're hiding.\"\n\nHe tilted his head slightly, his amber eyes locking onto hers with a clarity that felt like it was stripping away the layers of her habit. \"You're not obvious—you've got the mask down pat. But I can tell that living under the church's thumb must be suffocating for you, isn't it?\"\n\nThe question was a physical blow. It wasn't a suggestion; it was a diagnosis.\n\nEliza’s brow furrowed, and she instinctively looked away, her mouth working as she tried to find a pious answer, a denial, a way to retreat back into the safety of her silence. \"I...\" she started, her voice cracking.\n\nEamon waved a paw dismissively, as if brushing away the very concept of her hesitation. \"It's alright. You don't have to explain. I'm not the Inquisition; I don't need a confession.\" He paused, his gaze sharpening. \"The real question you have to answer is... how far are you willing to go? What does freedom mean to you?\"\n\nEliza looked back at him, a flash of genuine confusion crossing her features. The word 'freedom' was an abstraction, a fairy tale told in hushed tones or found in the margins of forbidden texts. It wasn't a thing one *had*.\n\n\"What do you mean... freedom?\"\n\n\"Freedom of the spirit, sister,\" Eamon replied, his voice smooth and steady. \"And of the body. Freedom from guilt, from the constant noise of being called a sinner for the crime of simply existing. How does that sound?\"\n\nA shudder went through Eliza. For a second, she imagined a world where the crushing weight in her chest simply vanished—where the heat in her blood wasn't a \"leak,\" but a light. But then the reality of her situation rushed back: the risk, the fear, the sheer impossibility of it.\n\nShe huffed, and for the first time, a bitter, fragile smile twisted her lips. \"Too good to be true,\" she whispered, the words sounding like a confession of hopelessness.\n\nEamon shrugged, a casual movement that suggested the impossible was merely a matter of perspective. \"And yet...\"\n\nEliza searched his face, her eyes wary, her mind racing. She was standing on a bridge that had already been burned behind her. \"What if I say no?\"\n\nEamon’s expression didn't change, but his voice took on a touch of clinical honesty. \"Then, I guess, you wither away in that cathedral, puttering around until you're old and gray...\" He paused, his eyes flickering with a peculiar intensity. \"...unless you marry some... *man*.\"\n\nThe word *man* was delivered with a pointed, almost playful emphasis. It was a needle precisely placed in a nerve. Eliza didn't miss it; she felt the blood rush to her cheeks, a vivid, betraying blush that warmed her skin. She glanced away, the image of the \"Biological Deadline\" and the predatory gaze of Gaspard flashing through her mind.\n\n\"And... what if I say yes?\" she asked, her voice barely audible, a tiny, trembling thread of hope.\n\nEamon didn't give her an answer—not a direct one. He didn't want to push her over the edge; he wanted her to jump.\n\n\"If you're really sure, come back and visit me again, sister,\" he said.\n\nThe moment was shattered as another customer, a harried-looking man with a ledger, stepped up behind Eliza, breaking the invisible circle they had formed. Eamon shifted instantly, the intimate confidant vanishing and the unremarkable merchant returning in a blink.\n\n\"Now,\" Eamon said, his voice returning to its loud, conversational drawl as he reached for the shopping list. \"What can I get for you today, sister?\"\n\nHe began filling her order with a practiced efficiency, his movements rhythmic and mundane, while a few feet away, Verali remained a grey shadow against the stone wall, her golden eyes watching the blonde acolyte with a silent, maternal curiosity. The invitation hadn't been extended—not yet—but the seed had been watered.",
