    "2026-05-11T03:17:54.527Z": {
      "msg_index": 35,
      "name": "Verali",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 16\nTime: 12:00pm)\n\nThe blonde acolyte had departed, her footsteps hurried and light, carrying her bundles of wax and tallow as if they were the only things keeping her anchored to the earth. Verali watched her go, her golden eyes tracking the swaying of the black habit until the girl disappeared into the press of the market crowd.\n\nThe air was still crisp, the scent of autumn decay mingling with the metallic tang of coal smoke, but Verali felt a lingering warmth in the space where the girl had stood.\n\nShe waited. She waited for the rhythm of the market to shift, for the wave of midday shoppers to crest and break, until a lull finally fell over Eamon's stall. With a slow, deliberate grace, she stepped away from the stone wall and drifted toward the counter. She didn't approach as a seeker or a devotee; she adopted the guise of a casual browser, her drab shawl wrapped tight around her shoulders, her movements unassuming. She picked up a small, carved wooden trinket from a display tray, turning it over in her talons with practiced indifference.\n\n\"So?\" Eamon asked.\n\nHis voice was a low, conversational hum, barely audible over the distant shouting of a fishmonger. He didn't look at her, instead focusing on reorganizing a stack of linens, but the tilt of his head betrayed his absolute attention.\n\nVerali did not answer immediately. She stared at the trinket, but her mind was elsewhere, replaying the encounter. She saw the way the girl's fingers had trembled, the way her voice had nearly failed her, and that particular, vivid flush of red that had climbed her cheeks.\n\nVerali felt a heavy, familiar ache in her chest. It was a visceral reaction—a recognition of a specific kind of suffering. She saw the crushing weight of the Temperantia's conditioning, the layers of guilt and self-doubt that the girl wore like a second skin, more suffocating than the habit itself.\n\n*This is a mistake,* Verali thought. *It is a tactical error.*\n\nEvery instinct honed by years of leading a secret cell screamed that this was a bad idea. The girl was fragile, barely holding together. Bringing her in was a risk; if she broke under the pressure of her own guilt, or if the Inquisition noticed her absence, it could lead a thread straight back to the sanctuary. There were others to think about—the brothers and sisters already in the fold, the delicate balance of their hidden existence. To introduce a volatile element was to invite instability.\n\nAnd yet... as she recalled the look in Eliza's eyes, Verali felt a surge of an almost forbidden impulse. She had felt a sudden, ridiculous urge to reach out—to wrap those slender, trembling shoulders in her own wings and whisper that she was not alone. She wanted to comfort her, to tell her that the hunger she felt was not a sin, but a signal.\n\n*It is unprofessional,* she reminded herself. *It is biased. I am letting a momentary surge of empathy flavor a decision that should be based on security and discretion.*\n\nVerali let out a slow, humming breath, her tongue flicking once to taste the salt and soot of the market air. The logic of the cell was clear, but the call of the same-suffering was louder.\n\n\"Alright,\" she said, her voice slightly husky, carrying the weight of a concession she knew she might regret.\n\nEamon didn't even look up from the linens, but the corners of his mouth curled upward into a sharp, triumphant grin. He looked for all the world like a man who had placed a bet on a long shot and won, grinning as if he had known the answer from the very moment he first spotted the blonde acolyte in the Cathedral.",
