    "2026-05-11T14:48:55.434Z": {
      "msg_index": 38,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 16\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\nAs they sat in the cooling air of the riverbank, Cecilia watched her friend with a quiet, observant tenderness. There was something different about Eliza today. The tension that usually clung to her like a second skin—the stiffness in her shoulders, the way she seemed to be bracing herself for a blow even in silence—had softened. When Eliza laughed, it wasn't the hollow, polite sound of a girl performing her duty; it was a genuine, airy sound that seemed to ripple through her entire being.\n\nCecilia felt a swell of relief. Only a few nights ago, Eliza had been a storm of anxiety, speaking in riddles about shepherds and flocks, her eyes wide with a fear that Cecilia couldn't quite name. She had worried that Eliza was falling into some deep, spiritual malaise, perhaps a crisis of faith that required a confessor's intervention.\n\n*Maybe... maybe the air is just doing her good,* Cecilia thought, glancing at the slate-grey water. *She's probably just a little stir-crazy being cooped up in the residential block all the time. The stones of the Cathedral are so heavy... they can weigh down a person's spirit if they don't have a way to let it breathe.*\n\nA small, determined idea began to take root in Cecilia's mind. If the open air and the sound of the river could bring back Eliza's smile, then perhaps the solution wasn't more prayer, but more movement. *Maybe I can speak with the Almoner,* she mused, *find some more tasks for her outside the church walls. Something with a different street, a different view. If she could just be away from the incense and the echoes for a few more hours a week, she might find her balance again.*\n\nThe golden light of the afternoon began to surrender to the deepening violets of dusk. The shadows of the pylons stretched long and thin across the water, and a sudden, sharp chill swept in from the north, making the fabric of their habits feel thin and insufficient.\n\nCecilia sighed, a soft sound of reluctant transition. She reached down, her fingers brushing the rough burlap of the shopping bags.\n\n\"Well, it's getting late,\" she said, her voice returning to that steady, unhurried cadence. \"We've stayed out longer than I intended. We wouldn't want the Wardens to wonder why we're lagging.\"\n\nShe paused, looking up at Eliza. The sheep-girl's expression was open, her brown eyes searching Eliza's face with a shy, tentative smile. For a moment, she looked less like an acolyte and more like a girl simply enjoying the company of a friend.\n\n\"But thank you,\" Cecilia added softly, \"for sitting with me.\"\n\nEliza looked back at her, and for a fleeting second, the mask of the humble acolyte was completely gone. She gave Cecilia a warm, genuine smile—one that reached her eyes and softened the lines of her face. It was a look of gratitude, perhaps, or perhaps a recognition of the only safe harbor she had left in a world of predators.\n\n\"You don't have to thank me, Cecilia,\" Eliza replied, her voice lacking its usual performative breathlessness. She reached down to gather her candles and tallow, her movements fluid. \"We should do this more often. I... I enjoyed it too.\"\n\nAs they stood up and began the trek back toward the looming silhouette of the Cathedral, Eliza let out a playful, weary sigh, the kind of sound that suggested she was finally ready to return to the fold, yet carried a secret spark of rebellion in her stride.\n\n\"Come on, let's get back,\" she said.\n\nCecilia walked beside her, humming a soft, liturgical tune, feeling a sense of peace. She believed she had helped her friend find her center again, unaware that the center Eliza had found was a doorway leading far, far away from the Church of Sanctae Temperantia.",
