    "2026-05-11T16:49:24.659Z": {
      "msg_index": 40,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 2:00pm)\n\n\nThe four days that followed the riverbank excursion had been a grueling exercise in endurance. The Cathedral felt smaller than usual, the ceiling lower, the air heavier. Every time she walked past the rear transepts or climbed the stairs to the fourth floor, the memory of Eamon’s amber eyes and the word *freedom* pulsed in her mind like a heartbeat. She had spent those days in a state of high-alert anxiety, convinced that the \"luck\" of her previous outing had been a fluke, a momentary lapse in the Church's surveillance. She had returned to her routine of silent piety, her hands clasped tight in her lap during sermons, her mind screaming for a way out while her lips whispered the approved prayers.\n\nThen, the impossible happened.\n\nWhen the requisition came, there was no mention of Cecilia. There was no chaperone, no soft-voiced sheep-girl humming a liturgical tune to keep her in line. To Eliza's stunned disbelief, the Almoner had simply handed her a sealed envelope and a set of directions. It was a simple delivery—a note to a small satellite church several blocks away, a task so mundane it barely warranted a second thought.\n\nAs she stepped through the heavy oak doors and felt the autumn wind hit her face, Eliza nearly stumbled. She stood on the cobblestones, staring back at the towering grey walls of the Cathedral, her breath coming in short, sharp hitches. *I'm alone. I'm actually alone.*\n\nShe didn't know that Cecilia had spent the last few days lobbying the Almoner, pleading for Eliza to have more \"spiritual breathing room\" through outward duties. To Eliza, this felt like a miracle—or a test.\n\nSteeling herself, she began her walk. She moved with a careful, practiced gait, her eyes forward, playing the part of the obedient messenger. She walked the prescribed route, her habit fluttering in the breeze, making sure she was well within the sight of the street-watchers and the peripheral guards. She didn't deviate until the great silhouette of the Cathedral was finally swallowed by the urban sprawl, the towering spires disappearing behind the grime-streaked rooftops of the industrial district.\n\nThe moment the sanctuary was out of sight, the mask slipped.\n\nEliza turned sharply into a narrow alleyway, the smell of damp brick and old soot filling her lungs. She navigated by a frantic, humming instinct, her heart hammering against her ribs. Every turn felt like a gamble, every shadow like a potential Inquisitor. She moved through the labyrinth of the city's backstreets, her breath ghosting in the cool air, until she emerged from a slim gap between two brightly-colored stalls—one selling dyed wools, the other a cacophony of copper pots and pans.\n\nShe paused, her back pressed against the cold stone of a building, her eyes darting left and right. The market was as loud and chaotic as ever, a sea of noise that provided the perfect cover for a ghost. She took a deep breath, smoothing the fabric of her habit with trembling hands, ensuring not a single fold was out of place.\n\nShe stepped out into the main thoroughfare, her expression shifting back into that of the demure, invisible acolyte. She began walking toward the raccoon's stall, her movements measured and routine, as if she were merely conducting the most boring of errands. But inside, the fire had returned.\n\n*I'm coming,* she thought, her gaze locked on the distant, familiar silhouette of the man who had promised her a candle in the dark. *I'm actually coming.*",
