    "2026-05-11T17:02:17.822Z": {
      "msg_index": 41,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 2:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 2:00pm)\n\n\nThe market was a cacophony of clashing sounds—the rhythmic *clink-clink* of a copper-smith’s hammer, the distant, guttural shouts of livestock traders, and the constant, low-frequency hum of the city's industrial breath. Eamon was in the middle of a transaction, leaning over a small, weathered ledger as he calculated the cost of some crude oil lanterns for a customer. He was in his element, the same unremarkable fixture of the commercial district that everyone simply walked past.\n\nThen, he saw her.\n\nA flash of black and white habit emerged from the press of the crowd. At first glance, she looked like any other drifting acolyte, a silent piece of the Church's machinery. But Eamon saw the tension in her stride, the way her eyes were searching for him with a hunger that was nearly palpable. His amber eyes widened a fraction—not out of surprise, for he had expected her, but out of a quiet, calculated appreciation for her courage.\n\nHe didn't let the mask slip. He finished the transaction with the customer, his voice remaining a smooth, conversational drawl, his movements efficient. He waited until the customer stepped away and the space in front of his stall cleared. For a brief, golden moment, there was nobody else in line. The window was open.\n\nEliza approached. She didn't rush; she moved with a shy, tentative hesitation, but as she stopped before the scarred wooden counter, Eamon noticed a firm, almost stubborn set to her jaw. She was trembling, yes, but she was standing her ground.\n\n\"You came back,\" Eamon said.\n\nIt wasn't a question. It was a simple observation, delivered with that same easy, half-lidded gaze. He didn't congratulate her, nor did he push her. He simply acknowledged the fact of her presence, giving her a moment to realize that she had already crossed the threshold.\n\nEliza nodded minutely, her gaze flickering to the surroundings to ensure they weren't being watched. \"Yes,\" she whispered.\n\nEamon leaned forward, the scent of old wax and linseed oil enveloping the space between them. His voice dropped an octave, becoming a low, intimate vibration. \"Then you're sure?\"\n\nThe question hung in the air, a final warning. He was giving her one last chance to retreat into the safety of her silence, to go back to the grey walls and the same predictable misery.\n\n\"Yes,\" she replied. The word came out as a desperate whisper, a fragile thread of sound that carried the weight of everything she had lost and everything she hoped to find.\n\nEamon nodded, acknowledging the pact. He shifted his weight, his gaze wandering for a moment as he mentally mapped out the logistics of the cell's current status.\n\n\"We... usually do things a little differently,\" he began, his voice trailing off as he thought. \"The approach is usually slower, more indirect. But you're a... well... an *odd* case, to say the least. A bit of a high-wire act, given where you live.\"\n\nHe refocused on her, his amber eyes locking onto hers. \"Tell me, do you think you can get away from the church for two or three hours? Without a chaperone? Without someone wondering where the same-colored robe has vanished to?\"\n\nThe a bit of resolve in Eliza's jaw vanished, replaced by a sudden, sharp spike of nervousness. She looked around the market, her breath becoming shallow. \"That's... I'm not sure. Maybe... distributing alms? That usually takes a few hours...\" She paused, her voice trembling. \"But... but I can't leave it unfinished. They'll hear if I shirk my duty.\"\n\nEamon thought for a moment, his gaze drifting toward the horizon of the city's skyline, calculating the risks. He could see the terror in her, but he could also see the fire.\n\n\"That's alright, we can work with that,\" he muttered, almost to himself. He looked back at her, his expression softening. \"That's fine,\" he repeated. \" The question is... do you know which day it will be? When the window is open?\"\n\nEliza shook her head, a small, defeated movement. \"I don't... they don't really tell me ahead of time. The orders just... come.\"\n\nEamon tilted his head, his mind already turning the problem into a puzzle. \"Can you find out? Do they write the schedules down anywhere? A ledger in the Almoner's office, a list on a wall? Give me a date, sister, and I'll give you a way out.\"",
