    "2026-05-11T17:33:01.227Z": {
      "msg_index": 42,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 2:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 2:00pm)\n\nEliza sucked in a sharp breath, the sound a small, ragged gasp that seemed too loud in the middle of the bustling square. *A way out?* The words echoed in her mind, not as a promise, but as a threat. The very idea of \"escaping\" the Cathedral was a concept she had only ever encountered in the fragmented, terrifying stories of the excommunicated—those who were cast out into the cold world to starve or be forgotten. To actively seek a way out was not just rebellion; it was an act of spiritual suicide.\n\nShe thought furiously, her mind racing back through the layout of the residential block, the dust-moted corridors, and the heavy oak doors of the administration wing. As the thought solidified, the terror grew, a cold sweat breaking out beneath the heavy fabric of her robe.\n\n\"I... suppose... the Almoner probably has a schedule,\" she said, her voice shaky and thin, the words barely escaping her throat. \"He keeps a ledger... in the office... it lists the acolytes' assignments for the coming weeks.\"\n\nEamon’s amber eyes glinted, his expression one of focused curiosity. \"See if you can get to it then,\" he replied, his tone encouraging but firm.\n\n\"But...\" she started, her voice rising in a small, nervous pitch. \"I... I cannot. The office is guarded, and the Almoner... he is very strict. To be found there without an errand... it would be... I would be...\"\n\n\"Now now, it's not that bad,\" Eamon interrupted, his voice softening into that velvet drawl that always seemed to make the world feel less precarious. He leaned closer, his posture relaxing to disarm her. \"You're not stealing anything. Just taking a little peek, right? It's no big deal.\"\n\nHe watched her face, seeing the war playing out in her light-blue eyes—the clash between a lifetime of conditioned fear and a new, raw hunger for autonomy. He realized then that she was not a seasoned rebel; she was a bird that had only just discovered the cage door was unlocked.\n\n\"Look, I'll do you one better,\" he decided, making an executive call to ease the panic he saw mounting in her. \"Either myself, or someone I trust, will... let's say... take a little *walk* around the outside of the Cathedral once an evening. If you ever have anything you need to let me know, you can do it then.\"\n\nHe bent over to look beneath his counter, the movement abrupt and practiced. He rummaged through a crate of odds and ends—old buttons, rusted nails, and fragments of glass—until he withdrew a small, grey, triangular stone. He held it out in his palm, the object looking like nothing more than a piece of rubble from a collapsed wall.\n\n\"What's that?\" she asked, her brow furrowing.\n\nEamon smirked, a flicker of genuine amusement crossing his face. With a deft flick of his thumb, he triggered a tiny, hidden latch. The stone sprang open with a soft *click*, revealing a hollow, velvet-lined interior.\n\n\"Pretty neat, eh?\" He closed the stone with a snap and pressed it firmly into Eliza's palm. The contact was brief, but it felt like a physical anchor. \"Just slip a note in there and drop it outside the fence. We'll find it, and drop it back on the other side for you.\"\n\nEliza stared down at the false stone in her hand. It was such a small thing—a pebble of grey rock—but suddenly it felt like the most important object in the city. It was a link to a world that didn't demand her submission, a line of communication to people who knew she existed and, seemingly, wanted to help her somehow.\n\nShe looked back up at the raccoon merchant, her voice small and laden with a sudden, crushing vulnerability. \"Why are you... doing all of this for me?\"\n\nEamon didn't answer with a speech or a promise. He only shook his head and smiled, a look of quiet, patient understanding. \"You'll understand soon, sister. Try to give me the benefit of the doubt. You came back, after all.\"\n\nThe logic was simple: she had already taken the risk. She had already stepped into the light.\n\nEliza looked down at the 'stone' again, her fingers curling around it. She felt a strange, fluttering sensation in her chest—not the panic of the Inquisition, but the anticipation of the unknown.\n\nShe pocketed the stone in the deep fold of her habit and nodded, her gaze steady for the first time that day. \"Alright. I'll... I'll try.\"",
