      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 6:30pm)\n\n\nThe evening air in the Cathedral was thick with the smell of burning tallow and the distant, muffled clatter of the dinner rush. It was that precarious hour before the evening meal, when the residential block became a hive of activity. Clergy and staff filtered toward the third-floor refectory, their habits swirling like black ink against the grey stone, their voices a low, collective hum of liturgical chatter.\n\nEliza, however, was currently navigating the ground floor. She had just ascended from the basement, her skin still smelling of the damp, metallic scent of the coal stores and old archives. As she stepped into the main hallway, she paused, her head tilting. The only sound was the rhythmic, distant thumping of a knife on a cutting board from the kitchen at the far end of the hall.\n\n*Who knows when I'll get another opportunity...* she thought, the words a frantic pulse in her mind. *The stone... the lifeline... I cannot let it go to waste.*\n\nShe didn't walk; she glided. She rolled her feet, a technique she'd perfected over years of avoiding the gaze of strict matrons, muffling the sound of her leather soles against the cold stone. Every shadow seemed to lean toward her, every flicker of the wall-sconces felt like a watchful eye.\n\nShe stopped outside the Almoner's office. She held her breath, leaning her ear against the heavy wood, listening for the scratch of a quill or the shift of a chair. Silence.\n\nShe slipped inside.\n\nThe office was a claustrophobic, dusty sanctuary of bureaucracy. Tall stacks of parchment threatened to collapse from the shelves, and the air was heavy with the smell of old ink and dried wax. The room felt frozen, a museum of the Church's logistical grip on the city. Eliza’s heart felt as if it might explode out of her chest, each beat a thunderclap in the oppressive silence.\n\nShe moved with a desperate, trembling haste. She didn't look at the curiosities on the desk; she went straight for the schedule book. Her fingers shook as she opened the heavy leather cover, the pages snapping with a sound that felt, to her ears, like a gunshot. She flipped through the pages rapidly, her eyes racing over the columns of names and dates, the ink blurring into a grey smudge as her panic mounted.\n\n*Please... please let it be there... please let me be alone...*\n\nFinally, she found it. A line of graphite scribbled in the margins, dated three days from now: *'Industrial Slums - 30 lbs. food, 3 lbs. medicine; Eliza, Lorraine'*\n\nShe stared at the page, a wave of crushing disappointment washing over her. *Dammit...* she thought, her breath hitching. *Couldn't they send me alone?*\n\nThe presence of 'Lorraine'—another acolyte, another set of eyes—was a barrier she couldn't easily breach. She couldn't simply vanish into the slums with a witness.\n\nHer eyes flickered around the room, her mind racing, searching for a solution in the clutter. Her gaze landed on a mangled, grey hunk of rubber—the Almoner's eraser—sitting atop a stack of requisitions.\n\n*Oh Mother, what am I doing?* she pleaded silently, her internal voice a scream of terror. *What if he notices? What if he remembers he put that there?*\n\nThe risk was astronomical. To alter a Church record was an act of forgery, a sin of deception that could lead her straight to the Examination offices. But the hunger for the \"candle\" was stronger than the fear of the flame.\n\nShe grabbed the eraser. With a precision born of absolute desperation, she leaned over the ledger and began to rub. She did it carefully, scrubbing the name 'Lorraine' from the page, her breath held so tightly her lungs burned. She felt the friction of the rubber against the paper, the same feeling as if she were erasing her own existence from the Church's records.\n\nWhen the graphite had finally faded into a ghostly smudge, she flicked away the small bits of rubber fuzz with a quick, nervous motion. If one didn't know the name had been there, the page looked untouched, merely aged.\n\nShe slammed the book shut, the sound echoing in the small room. She retreated to the door, pausing one last time to listen. Satisfied that the hallway remained empty, she took a deep, shuddering breath and slipped out, closing the door with a soft, final click.\n\nShe walked back toward the stairs, her movements steady, her face once again the mask of a humble, invisible servant.\n\nAs she entered the refectory on the third floor, the anxiety began to melt, replaced by a cold, crystalline triumph. Every minute that passed without a guard seizing her arm, every second she spent blending back into the crowd, felt like a victory.\n\nShe spotted Cecilia sitting at their usual table, the sheep-girl looking up with a warm, welcoming smile. Eliza slid into the seat beside her, her voice soft and deferential as she began to discuss the evening's prayers, but beneath the table, her hand was pressed firmly against the hollow stone in her pocket.",
