      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 7:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 7:00pm)\n\nThe refectory was a cavern of echoes and steam, the air heavy with the smell of boiled cabbage and the rhythmic clatter of wooden spoons against ceramic bowls. It was a space of enforced silence and communal discipline, where the only sounds permitted were the occasional muffled cough or the soft rustle of habits. Eliza sat beside Cecilia, her posture a study in practiced humility, but there was a lightness to her presence—a subtle, electric vibration that Cecilia hadn't missed.\n\nAs they began their meal, Cecilia didn't focus on her porridge. Instead, she kept stealing glances at Eliza. She watched the way Eliza’s eyes seemed to sparkle more than usual, the way she didn't seem quite so burdened by the weight of the ceiling above them. A slow, warm grin spread across Cecilia's face, a look of quiet satisfaction that mirrored the joy of a gardener watching a flower finally bloom after a long winter.\n\nEliza tried to maintain her mask, focusing intently on her bowl, but the sheer intensity of Cecilia's gaze was impossible to ignore. Eventually, the tension snapped, and Eliza let out a soft, genuine chuckle.\n\n\"What is it, Cecilia?\" Eliza asked, her voice a hushed, playful whisper. \"You look like the cat that drank the cream.\"\n\nCecilia giggled, the sound a small, bright, forbidden thing in the austere dining hall. She looked down at her plate, suddenly shy, and began to toy with her food, poking at a small piece of bread with the tines of her fork as if it were a fascinating archaeological find.\n\n\"It's nothing, sister,\" Cecilia replied, her voice airy and light. \"I'm just happy to see you looking so well lately. I knew getting out in the fresh air would do you good.\"\n\nEliza paused, her spoon halfway to her mouth. She tilted her head slightly, a flicker of confusion crossing her features. \"What... do you mean?\"\n\nAt this, Cecilia’s blush deepened, a soft, peony-pink hue spreading across her cheeks. She glanced away, her gaze darting toward the senior clergy at the head of the table, suddenly worried that her lack of discretion might be noted.\n\n\"Oh, well... I didn't... I shouldn't have said anything,\" she murmured, her voice trailing off into a bashful mumble. She looked back at Eliza, a barely repressed smile tugging at the corners of her mouth, her eyes shining with a mixture of affection and pride. \"I don't want to... to sound boastful, but...\"\n\nShe paused, her voice dropping to a conspiratorial whisper, the kind of tone used for the most dangerous of secrets.\n\n\"I might have... asked the Almoner if he could give you more duties outside.\"\n\nCecilia looked away again, her blush growing even pinker, her shoulders hunching slightly as if she were an embarrassed child caught in a prank. \"You just... seemed so much happier when we were outside. I thought... well, I thought it would be a kindness. I just wanted you to feel the sun on your face a little more often.\"\n\nShe looked back at Eliza, her expression one of pure, uncomplicated affection.",
