      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe world seemed to tilt on its axis. The clatter of the refectory, the smell of the bland porridge, the distant drone of the senior clergy's prayers—all of it vanished, leaving only the sound of Cecilia's voice ringing in her ears. The revelation hit her with the force of a physical blow, leaving her breathless and stunned.\n\nEliza stared dumbfounded at Cecilia. She looked at the sheep-girl—so earnest, so genuinely devoted to a friendship that Eliza was currently betraying with every fiber of her being. The kindness of it was almost unbearable. Cecilia hadn't just given her \"fresh air\"; she had unwittingly handed her the keys to her own rebellion. She had advocated for Eliza's freedom in a place where freedom was a sin.\n\nA sudden, sharp stinging flared behind Eliza's eyes. She blinked furiously, her vision blurring as the same heat she had felt in the market returned, not as a spark of rebellion, but as a wave of overwhelming guilt.\n\nIn a rare, impulsive break from her own conditioned restraint, Eliza reached out. Her hand found Cecilia’s, her fingers curling around the softer palm with a sudden, desperate intensity.\n\nCecilia let out a small, quiet gasp at the unexpected contact, her eyes widening. In the world of the Temperantia, physical touch was a leak, a transgression, a sign of instability. But here, in the middle of the refectory, it felt like the only honest thing in the room.\n\n\"You did?\" she breathed, her voice a fragile thread of sound, stripped of its ecclesiastical formality.\n\nThe word *thank you* felt too small, too insignificant for the weight of the moment. \"Cecilia... *thank you*,\" she whispered, squeezing her hand with a strength that spoke of a longing she couldn't put into words.\n\nThe contact lasted only a few seconds before the reality of their surroundings rushed back. The risk of being seen—the risk of a Warden noticing a 'leak' of affection—was too great. Eliza let go abruptly, the cold air rushing back into the space between their palms.\n\nShe quickly brushed at her eyes with the back of her hand, sniffling once, a small, wounded sound that she tried to hide behind a cough. She picked up her spoon again, staring intently at the porridge as if it held the secrets of the universe, forcing herself to return to the mask of the humble acolyte.\n\nBut inside, her heart was breaking.\n\nThe guilt was a heavy, suffocating blanket. She felt like a fraud, a parasite feeding on the genuine goodness of a girl who believed in everything Eliza had come to hate. Cecilia loved a version of her that didn't exist—a pious, struggling sister who needed a little sun on her face. The real Eliza was a liar, a thief of records, and a seeker of shadows.\n\n*I don't deserve her,* she thought, the porridge tasting like ash. *I don't deserve a single second of her kindness.*\n\nBut as she looked at Cecilia, who was still beaming with that uncomplicated, honest warmth, a different thought emerged. Cecilia deserved someone who truly cared for her. She deserved a friendship that wasn't a performance. Even if Eliza was a fraud, she decided, she would be the best fraud she could be. She would protect Cecilia, she would cherish her, and she would ensure that the kindness Cecilia had shown her was returned a thousandfold, even if the only way to do so was to keep her in the dark.",
