      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 7:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 7:00pm)\n\n(Year: 2144; Friday; September; 20\nTime: 9:00pm)\n\nThe dormitory was a sanctuary of oppressive silence, the only sound the rhythmic, distant tolling of the evening bell signaling the transition to the night's prayers. Beside her, Cecilia had already retreated into her own world of quiet piety, her movements slow and methodical as she folded her habit with the precision of a ritual. Eliza watched her for a moment, the guilt from the refectory still a cold weight in her stomach, but the fire of the secret she carried was stronger. The drive for autonomy had become a hunger that drowned out the shame.\n\nShe needed to move. Now.\n\nUnder the guise of a final nightly cleansing, Eliza slipped out of the room. She didn't head for the basins, but instead drifted toward a secluded corner of the washroom, her movements cautious and erratic. She felt as though the very walls were listening, the stone porous and eager to swallow her secrets.\n\nIn the corner of the room sat a small, rusted wastebasket, overflowing with the discarded remnants of the day—torn pieces of scrap parchment, used wax-wick trimmings, and discarded instructional leaflets. Eliza knelt beside it, her heart hammering against her ribs like a trapped bird. With a trembling hand, she reached into the bin and fished out a clean-looking scrap of paper, a fragment of a ledger page that had been ripped away. It was small, stained with a smudge of oil, but it would suffice.\n\nFrom the depths of her sleeve, she produced a tiny, jagged nubbin of broken pencil lead she had scavenged from a classroom floor earlier that afternoon. It was a pitiful thing, barely a sliver of graphite, but in her hand, it was a weapon.\n\nEliza stepped into the furthest stall and slid the bolt home. The click of the lock sounded like a thunderclap in the sterile silence of the room.\n\nShe leaned her back against the cold, damp stone wall, the smell of lye and old plumbing filling her lungs. She placed the scrap of paper on the ledge of the toilet, her breath coming in shallow, ragged sips.\n\n*I am doing this,* she told herself, her internal voice a whisper of terror and triumph. *I am actually doing this.*\n\nWith the small piece of lead, she hastily scribbled the date and the location: *Industrial Slums.* The graphite was faint, scratching the paper with a harsh, grating sound that felt dangerously loud. She didn't write her name; she didn't need to. The date was the only currency that mattered now.\n\nOnce the words were set, she began to fold the paper. She creased it once, then twice, then again, her fingers working with a frantic, obsessive energy. She pressed the edges down with her thumbnail, folding it smaller and smaller until the paper was no larger than her fingernail—a tiny, white seed of rebellion.\n\nSlowly, she withdrew the hollow stone from her pocket. The grey, unassuming pebble looked back at her, a piece of rubble that held the weight of her future. She flicked the hidden latch, the stone springing open with a soft, metallic *click*.\n\nCarefully, almost reverently, she slid the tiny scrap of paper into the velvet-lined interior. It fit perfectly, a secret tucked away in a shell of stone.\n\n*Snap.*\n\nThe stone closed. The evidence was gone.\n\nEliza stood in the dark of the stall for a long moment, her forehead resting against the cold stone. She felt a strange, dizzying sense of vertigo, as if she had just stepped off a ledge and was waiting for the fall.\n\nTomorrow, she would find a moment to drop it over the fence.",
