      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 21\nTime: 7:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 21\nTime: 7:15pm)\n\nThe city was shivering. A premature winter had descended upon the valley, bringing with it a bitter, bone-deep chill that turned the breath into crystalline plumes. The first snowflakes had begun to fall—tiny, jagged shards of white that drifted lazily through the sulfurous yellow glow of the streetlamps, blending with the soot of the industrial district to create a grey, suffocating veil.\n\nEamon walked with a slow, languid grace, his hands buried deep in his pockets. To any observer, he was merely a man of the markets winding down his day, his footsteps rhythmic and unremarkable against the hardening slush of the sidewalk. He didn't look like a man conducting a clandestine operation; he looked like a man who had long ago made peace with the boredom of his existence.\n\nBut beneath the casual facade, his eyes were searching.\n\nAs he approached the perimeter of the *Sanctae Temperantia*, he didn't slow down, nor did he look around suspiciously. He simply kept his gaze fixed ahead, his amber eyes scanning the grey concrete of the sidewalk with a practiced, predatory efficiency.\n\nAnd there it was.\n\nA small, triangular grey stone, resting innocently against the base of the iron fence. To the uninitiated, it was a piece of rubble, a stray fragment of a crumbling wall. To Eamon, it was a beacon.\n\nIn one fluid, seamless motion—a dip of the shoulder and a quick flick of the wrist—he scooped the stone up. There was no hesitation, no pause to check for witnesses. It was a gesture as natural as breathing. He stepped away from the fence, the cold wind whistling through the gaps in the ironwork, and with a deft click of his thumb, he extracted the tiny, white seed of a message.\n\nHe didn't keep the stone. With a casual, almost careless toss, he flicked the pebble back through the bars of the fence. It landed with a soft *thud* in a small clump of frozen grass, precisely where Eliza would be able to spot it during her next trip to the courtyard. He was a man of habits, and he knew that the girl was now a creature of habits—habits of secrecy and longing.\n\nEamon didn't open the note immediately. He continued his stroll, walking several blocks away from the looming silhouette of the cathedral, moving deeper into the industrial sprawl where the noise of the machinery drowned out the silence of the church. He waited until he was sure he was alone, tucked into the shadow of a soot-stained brick alleyway where the snowflakes fell thickest.\n\nHe unfolded the scrap of paper. The graphite was faint, a desperate scratching that spoke of a heart hammering in a dark room.\n\n*'Industrial Slums Mon'*\n\nEamon stared at the words for a moment, a slow, knowing smile curling the corner of his mouth. The girl had done it. She had risked the ledger, the Almoner, and the very air she breathed to send a signal. She was no longer just a scared, helpless girl; she was a collaborator in her own liberation.\n\nHe didn't keep the evidence. After a second glance to commit the location to memory, he gripped the edges of the paper and tore it. He didn't just rip it; he shredded it into a thousand unrecognizable specks, a frantic, rhythmic tearing that mirrored the destruction of the life she was leaving behind.\n\nHe opened his hand. As he resumed his walk, the flecks of white paper fluttered from his palm, blending seamlessly with the falling snow. They drifted downward, disappearing into the white-grey slush of the street, leaving no trace that the message had ever existed.\n\n\"Well now,\" he whispered to the wind, his drawl heavy with a quiet, dangerous anticipation. \"The game is afoot.\"",
