      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September 22\nTime: 10:00am)\n\nThe two days leading up to Monday were a blur of agonizing, slow-motion anticipation. Every single hour felt like a heavy stone being added to a pile, pressing down on her chest until she felt she could barely breathe. She moved through the Cathedral as if she were underwater, the sounds of the bells and the chants of the sisters reaching her as a muffled, distant hum. She spent her nights staring at the ceiling of the dormitory, her mind a whirlwind of terror and adrenaline, counting the seconds until the world changed. Every time she passed the Almoner's office, her heart would spike, a sudden jolt of electricity that left her fingers trembling. She was a prisoner counting down to an execution, yet for the first time in her life, she was the one who had set the date.\n\nWhen the summons finally came, it didn't arrive with a flourish. It was a simple, clipped order from a junior warden, a brief command that echoed through the hallway. Even though she had spent forty-eight hours preparing for this exact moment, Eliza felt a sudden, violent flip in her stomach—a cold, sickening lurch that made her feel lightheaded.\n\nShe entered the Almoner's office, the air smelling of stale ink and old parchment. The room felt smaller today, the piles of ledgers leaning in like silent witnesses to her deception.\n\n\"Yes, Father?\" she asked, her voice soft and breathless, her eyes fixed on the polished toes of her shoes. She stood before his desk in a posture of absolute humility, her hands clasped tightly in front of her, the fabric of her habit pulled taut across her knuckles.\n\nThe Almoner didn't look up immediately. He was a man of habits and checklists, his world defined by the rigid columns of his ledger. He squinted through a pair of thick, gold-rimmed glasses, his finger tracing a line of graphite. He stopped, his brow furrowing as he stared at the entry. He scratched at his coarse, grey beard, the sound of the bristles a sharp, rhythmic rasp in the silence of the office. He looked at the ledger, then at the stack of supplies waiting by the door, and then back at the ledger.\n\n\"Those supplies are earmarked for distribution in the industrial slums,\" he said, his voice a dry, papery rattle. He paused, the silence stretching out, thick with the possibility of discovery. He seemed to be searching for a memory—perhaps a memory of a name he had once written there, a name that was now only a ghostly smudge of erased graphite. \"Are you sure you can handle all of that by yourself?\"\n\nThe question was a trap, a sudden cliff edge. For a heartbeat, Eliza felt the mask slip. She could almost hear the voice of the Inquisition in her head, the same voice that had kept her in line for twenty-one years. But then she remembered the stone. She remembered the amber eyes of the raccoon.\n\nShe didn't give the old man a chance to second-guess the ledger.\n\n\"Oh yes!\" she said, her voice lifting with a forced, bright energy. She stepped forward and hefted the heavy sack of food, the coarse burlap scratching against her palms. She swung it over her shoulder with a forced casualness, a performance of strength and eagerness that felt like a costume she was wearing. \"It's... no problem. Shall I be on my way then?\"\n\nThe Almoner raised a thin, translucent eyebrow. He looked at her—really looked at her—for a moment. He saw a slender, blonde acolyte who looked as though a strong wind might blow her over, carrying a load that seemed far too heavy for her frame. He seemed to be weighing her competence against his own desire to be finished with the morning's paperwork.\n\nFinally, he waved his hand at her dismissively, a gesture of bored indifference. \"Alright then, off you go.\"\n\nHe turned his attention back to his ledgers, the scratch of his quill returning as he erased her from his immediate concern.\n\nEliza stepped back out into the corridor, the heavy door clicking shut behind her. As she walked toward the exit, the air in the hallway felt thinner, colder, and infinitely more breathable. She wasn't just a messenger anymore. She was a ghost who had learned how to haunt her own house, and the road to the industrial slums was calling.",
