      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 6:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\nThe air in the warehouse was thick, a heavy haze of musk and ancient dust, but as the Matron stood, the atmosphere seemed to shift, centering entirely around her. She rose with a fluid, predatory grace, her white scales shimmering like crushed diamonds under the dim, flickering lights. Eliza had to crane her neck back, her small frame dwarfed by the dragon-kin’s imposing height. Verali looked down, her golden eyes tracing the line of the girl's trembling throat, noticing the way Eliza swallowed hard, a visible, jagged movement of desperation and awe.\n\n\"Sister,\" Verali said, her voice a low, melodic hum that seemed to vibrate in the very marrow of Eliza's bones. It was a sound of sanctuary, quiet enough to remain a secret from the lovers entwined in the shadows around them. \"I've been waiting for you.\"\n\nVerali could see the panic beginning to claw back in. Eliza was staring at the room—the tangled limbs, the raw, unabashed pleasure of the others—with a look of sheer, overwhelming vertigo. The girl looked as if she were standing on the edge of a cliff, her breath coming in short, jagged sips.\n\nRealizing the main room was likely too much for a soul conditioned by the sterile silence of the Cathedral, Verali beckoned her toward a side door. It was a utility closet, a cramped space that had been hastily repurposed for the night. Inside, a mattress sat bare atop a stack of weathered wooden pallets, the scent of old oil and damp concrete lingering in the corners.\n\n\"Sit,\" Verali invited, her voice soft and unhurried.\n\nEliza collapsed onto the mattress, though she didn't so much sit as she did fall, her body swaying as if she were intoxicated. Her light-blue eyes were wide, darting frantically, never leaving Verali's form. She didn't just look at the Matron's face; her gaze drifted, drawn by a magnetic, forbidden curiosity to the curve of a hip, the sweep of a wing, the forbidden architecture of a body that the Church had taught her to fear.\n\n\"There's no need to be frightened, Eliza,\" Verali murmured, her presence filling the small room like a warm hearth. \"There are only friends here. Nothing that happens here will make it back to the church, and that is a promise.\"\n\nShe spoke the words with a steady conviction. In truth, the secrecy was as much for the protection of the cell as it was for the girl; a single leak could bring the Inquisition's hammer down upon every person in the warehouse. But she didn't need to burden the girl with the mechanics of their survival.\n\n\"What... what is this?\" Eliza asked, her voice a small, quavering thing that barely carried. Her fingers were tying themselves into frantic knots in her lap, her knuckles white. Her cheeks remained a vivid, burning red, the color of a shame she was beginning to outgrow. \"Is...\" she paused, her eyes widening in a flash of sudden, internalized terror, \"This is... one of the dragon cults that the High Inquisitor speaks of... isn't it?\"\n\nVerali winced. The mention of Justinian was like a cold draft in a warm room. *Don't forget where she sleeps at night,* she reminded herself. The girl's entire world was a construct of the Inquisitor's rhetoric. Beyond that, the term 'dragon cult' was distasteful, a malignant distortion of their truth.\n\n\"Those aren't... the words I would choose,\" Verali replied patiently, her voice a soothing balm. \"This isn't about belief or faith, Eliza. It's about *you*.\"\n\nEliza’s brow furrowed, a look of genuine, heartbreaking confusion crossing her face. \"Me... what... what do you mean, it's about me?\"\n\nVerali leaned forward. Slowly, with a tenderness that belied her physical power, she reached out and brushed her fingertips against Eliza's cheek. The contact was electric. Eliza made a sound—half-gasp, half-sob—a jagged release of tension that had been building for twenty-one years.\n\n\"You're... different than the other acolytes, aren't you?\" Verali asked.\n\nEliza clenched her eyes shut, her lashes wet. She didn't pull away; instead, she leaned into the touch, her head tilting ever so slightly into Verali's hand, as if she were a starving animal finally finding a crumb of warmth.\n\n\"Yes...\" she whispered, the word barely audible.\n\n\"And you've been told all your life that you're a sinner because of the things you feel?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" the word was choked out, her throat tightening, her voice thick with the weight of a thousand repressed desires.\n\n\"Listen to me, Eliza,\" Verali murmured, leaning closer, her warmth radiating through the small room. Her hand remained on the girl's cheek, a steady anchor. \"There is *nothing* wrong with you. The things you're feeling... they're natural. They're a part of almost everyone... and they were never meant to be chained up and hidden away like some shameful secret.\"\n\nVerali shifted, taking Eliza's face in both of her hands, forcing the girl to look into those golden, knowing eyes. She repeated the words slowly, each syllable a brick of truth intended to dismantle the Cathedral's walls.\n\n\"Eliza... there is *nothing* wrong with you.\"\n\nThe dam finally broke. The halting breaths turned into violent, racking sobs. The mask of the humble acolyte, the performance of piety, the years of pretending to be a ghost—it all shattered.\n\nVerali didn't say another word. She simply pulled the trembling woman into her arms, drawing her close. Eliza collapsed against her, her head pressed firmly into the Matron's ample, soft breasts. Verali held her with a steady, maternal strength, letting the girl shake and cry, providing the only thing the Church had ever denied her: a place where she was allowed to be broken.",
