      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\nThe silence of the room was gone, replaced by the raw, jagged sound of her own undoing. For twenty-one years, she had been a vessel of stillness, a girl who moved through the world as if she were afraid of leaving a footprint. Now, in the arms of this creature who should have been a monster, she felt the internal architecture of her life simply collapse.\n\nThe sobbing was violent, a physical purging. Every gasp, every wretched, shaking breath seemed to drag out a piece of the heavy, leaden weight that had settled in her chest since the day she first entered the creches. It was the weight of a thousand whispered prayers that felt like lies; the weight of the \"Biological Deadline\" that felt like a countdown to a theft; the weight of a loneliness so profound it had become a part of her skeletal structure.\n\nAs the first wave of relief crested, Eliza’s instincts took over. Her arms, which had been limp and trembling, suddenly found purchase. She reached out, her hands grasping at the smooth, warm scales of the Matron's waist. She clung to Verali with a desperation that was almost feral, her fingers digging into the white scales as if she were a drowning person catching a piece of driftwood in a storm.\n\nShe didn't just hold her; she anchored herself. She buried her face deeper into the softness of Verali's chest, the scent of musk and salt filling her senses, drowning out the smell of incense and lye that had defined her existence.\n\nThen, the reciprocation happened. Verali’s arms tightened around her, a steady, enveloping pressure that felt like a physical shield. It was a simple act—a hug, a hold, a touch—but to Eliza, it was an apocalypse of the heart. The warmth of another living being, offered without a price, without a ritual, and without a demand for submission, brought on a fresh, suffocating wave of confused and relieved sobs.\n\n*I am... I am here,* she thought, the words a flickering light in the froth of her mind. *I am actually here.*\n\nShe didn't know where this path led. She didn't know if the High Inquisitor would find her, or if the \"Contamination\" the Church spoke of was real, or if she was simply trading one set of chains for another. But as she felt the rhythmic beat of the Matron's heart against her own, the fear felt distant, like a noise coming from another room.\n\nFor the first time in her life, the mask was gone. There was no apathetic acolyte, no dutiful servant, no invisible shadow. There was only Eliza—small, trembling, and utterly seen. She didn't want the prayers, the pews, or the promises of a distant restoration. She only wanted this. The terrifying, beautiful reality of being accepted for exactly what she was: a girl who was tired of being a vessel, and who finally, *finally* wanted to be full.",
