    "2026-05-11T23:05:43.834Z": {
      "msg_index": 54,
      "name": "Verali",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\nVerali remained as a silent monolith of warmth, her breath a steady, rhythmic tide against the crown of Eliza's head. She did not offer platitudes; she did not tell the girl that everything would be alright, for such lies were the currency of the Church, and Verali dealt only in truth. She simply held her. She let the girl’s sobs echo through the small, damp room, allowing the grief to pour out of her like a river breaking a long-held dam.\n\nAs the racking tremors subsided into soft, hitching breaths, Verali felt a familiar, heavy ache in her chest. She looked down at the golden-haired girl clinging to her, and her heart fractured.\n\nThe dragon-kin knew the coldness of the world. They knew what it was to be viewed as a pariah, to be hunted or commodified, to exist in the margins of a society that regarded their very biology as a contagion. They knew the fear of the predator's gaze and the weight of the iron shackle. But there was a specific, exquisite cruelty that Verali had been spared—a pain she could only conceptualize through the ruins of the girl in her arms.\n\nTo be hunted by an enemy was one thing. But to be taught, from the first breath of one's life, that the very essence of your soul—your desires, your hunger, your capacity for love—was a *defect*. To be convinced that the same things that make a person human are actually the evidence of their corruption.\n\nVerali closed her eyes for a moment, imagining the psychological architecture of the Cathedral. She imagined the thousands of hours Eliza had spent in those sterile halls, scrubbing floors and chanting hymns, all while a voice in the back of her mind whispered that she was broken. The hopelessness of believing that the only way to be \"pure\" was to be empty.\n\nThat was a loneliness Verali could only guess at—the agony of being your own jailer, locking the door from the inside because you were told the world outside was a fire.\n\nLost in the gravity of this reflection, Verali didn't immediately register the shift in the girl's breathing. The silence of the room returned, save for the distant, rhythmic thumping of the warehouse's inhabitants.\n\n\"...show me...\"\n\nVerali blinked, the golden slits of her eyes focusing back on the present. The voice had been a hoarse, fragile whisper, barely audible over the hum of the room.\n\n\"What was that?\" Verali asked softly, her voice a melodic, curious ripple. She had been so deep in her own thoughts that the words had reached her as a blurred vibration.\n\nEliza pulled back just enough to look up. Her face was a ruin of red-rimmed eyes and salt-streaked cheeks, her sandy hair dampened by tears and the humidity of the room. There was no longer a mask of piety, no demure tilt of the head. There was only a raw, starving hunger.\n\n\"Show me...\" Eliza pleaded, her voice cracking, her gaze locking onto Verali's golden eyes with a desperate, unwavering intensity. \"Please...\"",
