      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\nThe silence that followed was not the sterile, oppressive silence of the Cathedral’s hallways, but a heavy, velvet quiet. It was the kind of silence that existed only after a storm had passed, leaving the air thick and electric. Eliza lay draped across Verali, her limbs feeling as though they were made of lead, her skin humming with a lingering, golden warmth.\n\nThe world was slowly reassembling itself around her. She could hear the distant, muffled sounds of the warehouse—the rhythmic creak of floorboards, the faint, echoing moans of other lovers, and the low, industrial thrum of the city outside. But here, in the small, dim sanctuary of the utility closet, the only thing that mattered was the steady, thumping percussion of the Matron's heart beneath her cheek.\n\n*I'm... I'm not a virgin anymore...*\n\nThe thought swirled in her mind, a slow-motion revelation. For twenty-one years, that status—that perceived \"purity\"—had been the only currency the Church valued in her. It was the fragile glass vase she had been told to protect at all costs, the only thing keeping her from being labeled a \"leaky\" vessel or a spiritual failure.\n\nShe waited for the crash. She waited for the wave of nausea, the sudden, suffocating guilt, the voice of the High Inquisitor screaming in her ear that she was now an abomination, a piece of filth to be purged. She waited for the feeling of being \"defiled,\" a word the priests used to describe the touch of the world.\n\nBut the crash never came.\n\nInstead, she found a profound, humming contentment. A warmth that didn't come from the catalyst, but from a place deep within her own soul. She felt... expanded. The narrow, suffocating corridor of her life had suddenly opened into a vast, shimmering landscape.\n\n*Why would they keep such a thing from us?* she wondered, a small, tired sigh escaping her lips. *Why would they lie about this?*\n\nIt hadn't been an act of sin; it had been a homecoming. It was transcendent. It was the only honest thing she had ever experienced.\n\nAs she rested her head on Verali's chest, Eliza felt the physical reality of their union lingering. She could still feel the Matron's presence within her—the slow, rhythmic softening and retreating of the dragon-kin's member as the fire died down to a glow. There was a heavy, wet sensation, the warmth of the seed pooling inside her, a physical anchor that tied her to this moment.\n\n*Defiled...*\n\nShe almost giggled, the sound a tiny, breathy vibration against Verali's skin. The absurdity of the Church's theology felt laughable now. They called this defilement? They called this the Contamination? If this was filth, then she never wanted to be clean again.\n\nNo... she wasn't defiled. She was free.\n\nFor the first time, the word didn't feel like a political slogan or a rebel's dream; it felt like a physical state of being. She had tasted what freedom truly meant—not as a lack of rules, but as the presence of desire. She had stepped off the cliff and discovered that she could fly.\n\nAs she closed her eyes, a new, fierce determination took root in her heart. The fear of the biological deadline was still there, but it had changed shape. It was no longer a ticking clock of vulnerability; it was a deadline for her escape. She would not go back to being a ghost. She would not return to the mask of the apathetic acolyte.\n\nShe would fight to keep this. She would risk the Inquisition, the excommunication, and the very air she breathed just to return to this damp, dark closet. To see Verali again. And again. And again.",
