    "2026-05-12T04:15:15.970Z": {
      "msg_index": 65,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 6:00pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 6:00pm)\n\nThe transition from the sanctuary to the street was like stepping from a dream into a frozen nightmare. The temperature had plummeted, a bitter, biting cold that seemed to seep through the fabric of Eliza's habit. Above them, the sky had turned a bruised, heavy purple, and the first snowflakes of the season were beginning to drift down—tiny, crystalline needles that clung to the soot-stained brickwork of the slums.\n\nEamon walked beside her, his pace unhurried, his hands tucked deep into his pockets. He didn't push her toward the Cathedral immediately; instead, he guided her along the winding arteries of the industrial district, keeping her away from the main thoroughfares where the Wardens patrolled.\n\nHe looked at her sideways, his amber eyes dancing with a mixture of amusement and genuine warmth. He had known Verali for years. He had seen the Matron stand as an immovable pillar of strength, a divine symbol of liberation who never wavered, never faltered, and never asked for anything in return. Verali was the anchor that kept the cells from drifting away, but Eamon knew the cost of that stability. He knew the loneliness that lived in the marrow of a creature who is viewed as a relic rather than a person.\n\nEamon himself had never been the type for long-term tenderness. His heart was a restless thing, his feet and hands always itching for the next deal, the next secret, the next shift in the wind. He wasn't built for the quiet, suffocating intimacy of a long-term bond; he was a creature of the periphery, a man of the margins. But seeing the way Eliza looked now—the dazed, luminous quality of her gaze, the way she seemed to be breathing for the first time—brought a small, satisfied smile to his face.\n\n\"Well now,\" Eamon drawled, his voice a low, conversational hum. \"I've seen a lot of folks walk out of that closet, lass, but I don't think I've ever seen anyone look quite so... *unraveled*.\"\n\nHe let out a soft, knowing chuckle. \"Our Matron has been the city's most steadfast symbol for over a decade. I suspect she's currently wondering where her dignity went, and I suspect she's perfectly happy that you were the one to steal it.\"\n\nEliza's face flared red, a vivid bloom of embarrassment that looked almost beautiful against the falling snow. She looked down at her boots, her voice small and breathless. \"When... when can I go back? When can I see her again?\"\n\nEamon slowed his pace, glancing at the grey horizon. \"Now, don't go getting ahead of yourself. We don't meet more than once a week. Safety, you understand. If we're too predictable, we're just waiting for the Inquisition to find a pattern. Same reason we move the location around. We aren't running a hotel; we're running a sanctuary.\"\n\nHe paused, tilting his head slightly. \"Find a day sometime in the next week. Pick a time you can slip away without Cecilia noticing you've gone on a spiritual journey. I'll make sure the word gets to Verali.\"\n\nAs they reached a bridge spanning a blackened, oil-slicked canal, Eamon mentioned the logistics. \"Oh, and don't you worry about those sacks of alms. Our volunteers have already seen to the distributions. Every family you were meant to visit has their supplies. Unless the Church decides to conduct a forensic audit of every porch in the slums, nobody will know the difference.\"\n\nEliza stopped walking. She turned to him, her light-blue eyes searching his face, her voice trembling with a sudden, poignant confusion.\n\n\"Why are you doing all of this for me?\" she whispered. \"I'm... I'm nobody. I'm just an acolyte. I'm nothing.\"\n\nEamon didn't stop walking immediately, but as he stepped past her, he paused and looked at her warmly. The drawl was gone, replaced by a sincerity that felt raw and honest.\n\n\"All of us have our own reasons for joining, Eliza,\" he said softly. \"And in almost every case, it boils down to the same thing... we aren't welcome anywhere else.\"\n\nHe shrugged, a small, casual gesture that seemed to dismiss the tragedy of their existence as a simple fact of life. \"So... we tend to look after each other. It's the only way to survive.\"\n\nHe stepped closer, his voice dropping to a gentle, protective register. \"First time I saw you at the market, lass, I knew you were in trouble. I could see it in the way you held yourself—like you were trying to disappear into your own skin. I knew if you didn't find your way to us, you would've died a death of the soul inside those walls. You'd have just... faded out.\"\n\nHe looked her in the eyes, his expression grave but kind. \"How could I stand by and let that happen and still call myself a good man?\"\n\nHe stepped back, gesturing toward the path that led back to the Cathedral's towering spires. \"The rest of the way is a lonely walk, but you've got a bit of gold in you now. You'll be fine.\"\n\nWith a final, knowing wink, Eamon vanished back into the grey mist of the industrial district, leaving Eliza alone in the falling snow, her habit feeling a little lighter than it had before.",
