      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 6:00pm)\n\n\n\"Thank you...\" she whispered, her voice a small, fragile thread of sound that was quickly snatched away by the biting wind. She watched Eamon's silhouette dissolve into the grey, soot-stained mist of the industrial district, his retreating form a reminder of the bridge she had just crossed.\n\nShe stood still for a moment, the first snowflakes of the season landing on her eyelashes and the black fabric of her habit. The cold was sharp, a clinical shock to the system, but it didn't feel oppressive. Instead, it felt like a boundary—a clear, crisp line between the world she had lived in and the world she had just discovered.\n\nAs she turned and began the long walk back toward the towering spires of the Cathedral, Eliza felt a sensation she had never known: a profound, humming vitality. She felt like a new woman, a new being altogether. It was as if a veil had been torn away, and for the first time, the world was no longer a blur of grey stone and muted prayers.\n\nThe world was sharper. More vibrant. Even the muted, monochrome landscape of the falling snow seemed to pulse with a hidden energy. She noticed the way the streetlamps flickered with a rhythmic, electronic heartbeat; the way the oil-slicked puddles reflected the bruised purple of the evening sky; the way the distant, rhythmic thumping of the industrial machinery sounded less like a dirge and more like a pulse.\n\nEvery nerve in her body was firing in remembered pleasure. The friction of the wool against her skin, the lingering warmth between her thighs, the ghost of Verali's breath against her neck—it was all there, a secret map of ecstasy written onto her skin. She walked in a daze, her footsteps light, almost floating, as her brain attempted to process these new revelations. She was no longer a vessel; she was the source.\n\nAs the silhouette of the Cathedral loomed ahead, the great, gothic structure that had been her entire universe, a wave of apprehension washed over her. Normally, the sight of the spires brought a feeling of crushing weight, a reminder of her insignificance and her duty.\n\nBut as she looked up at the stone heights, the feeling changed.\n\nThe thought of returning to the residential block, of spending days or even weeks performing her duties with the mask of piety firmly in place, no longer felt like a sentence. It no longer felt like the last thing she would ever do in her life—a slow fade into a colorless existence.\n\nNow, it felt like... a test. A test of endurance.\n\n*I can do this,* she told herself, her voice a determined whisper in the cold air. *I can play the part. I can be the quiet acolyte, the humble servant, the invisible shadow. I can walk through the Nave and scrub the floors of the Sacristy, and they will think I am the same.*\n\nBut she wouldn't be the same. She would be a spy in the house of her enemy. She would carry the gold of Verali's seed inside her like a hidden treasure, a secret flame that would keep her warm during the long, sterile hours of her service.\n\nHer heart fell full and warm at the thought of seeing the Matron again. The cruelty of the separation hit her then—the sheer, agonizing distance of the next few days. To have finally found the one thing she had been searching for, only to be forced back into the silence, felt like a physical ache in her chest.\n\nBut the ache was a reminder. The longing was a tether.\n\nEliza straightened her shoulders, adjusted the heavy cloth of her habit, and stepped toward the gates. She wasn't returning as a servant. She was returning as a survivor, counting down every second until she could be stolen away once more.",
