      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 6:00pm)\n\n\nThe transition from the biting winter air to the interior of the Cathedral was a return to a familiar, oppressive stillness. The stone walls of the ground floor were cold, radiating a damp chill that seemed to echo the sterility of the doctrine taught within. As she pushed through the heavy doors and entered the transition zone, the scent of incense and old wax filled her nostrils—the smell of the Church, a fragrance that had always felt like a shroud.\n\nShe made her way toward the Almoner's office, her footsteps echoing softly on the polished stone. She felt the weight of her habit once more, but this time, it didn't feel like a cage; it felt like a costume. She was playing a role now, a performance of piety that she could execute with chilling precision.\n\nAs she rounded the corner of the rear transept, her heart gave a sudden, sharp thud.\n\nStanding in the center of the hallway, his charcoal-grey robes smelling faintly of stale wine and old sweat, was Father Gaspard.\n\nThe sight of him usually triggered an immediate, visceral reaction in Eliza—a trembling of the knees, a shallowing of the breath, a desperate urge to shrink into the wall and disappear. For years, Gaspard had been the predator in her periphery, the man whose gaze felt like a stain.\n\nBut as she looked at him now, something had shifted. She saw the bloatedness of his face, the bulbous, red-veined nose, and the predatory glint in his beady eyes. She saw the man who had threatened to \"guide\" her into becoming a vessel, who had used the biological deadline as a weapon of intimidation.\n\nSuddenly, those threats felt small. They felt like the blustering of a child. Gaspard’s power relied on Eliza’s fear, on her belief that he was the only authority who could \"save\" or \"ruin\" her. But Eliza had already been saved—by a white-scaled Matron in a damp basement. She had already been ruined, and in that ruin, she had found a glory that Gaspard could never comprehend.\n\nHe had already spotted her. There was no way to divert her path without appearing suspicious, and the thought that she might look like she was fleeing him—which would only embolden him—was unacceptable.\n\nHer heart hammered, yes, but not with the panic of a prey animal. It was the adrenaline of a soldier. Her steps remained sure, her posture demure, her breathing steady. She approached him with the practiced grace of an acolyte, her eyes lowered just enough to maintain the illusion of submission.\n\n\"Sister,\" he said, his voice a grating, wheezing rasp that cut through the silence of the hall.\n\nEliza stopped, her hands clasping tightly in her lap, the same nervous tell she had used for years. But inside, she was cold. She looked up to meet his eyes, her expression a mask of humble obedience.\n\nGaspard didn't just stand there; he leaned in, invading her personal space with a calculated intensity. He wanted her to feel his breath, to smell the decay of his habits, to feel the crushing weight of his presence. He was attempting to squeeze the fear out of her.\n\n\"I've noticed you're going out more often, sister,\" he murmured, his voice slipping into that guttural, demanding snarl. \"I hope you're resisting the temptations of... of...\"\n\nHe stopped abruptly.\n\nAs he leaned in close, a tiny, stray waft of air entered his nostrils. It was a scent so subtle that Eliza, nose-blind to the same aroma, didn't even realize it was there. It was the scent of dragon-kin essence—the musk, the salt, the primal sweetness of the seed that still lingered between her legs, hidden beneath the thick wool of her habit.\n\nGaspard’s eyes widened. He didn't connect the scent to the small, blonde girl in front of him. To him, the scent of dragon-kin was a luxury, a black-market drug he consumed in secret to escape his own mediocrity. For a split second, he smelled the essence and his brain leaped to a singular, panicked conclusion: he smelled it on *himself*. He thought the scent of his own illicit habits was clinging to his breath, potentially betraying his secret to any passerby.\n\nHe jerked back from Eliza as if she were a hot stove, his face twisting in a look of sudden, neurotic alarm.\n\n\"Nevermind,\" he sputtered, his voice returning to a hurried, wheezing tone. \"I just remembered... something.\"\n\nWithout another word, Gaspard turned and waddled as fast as his heavy legs could carry him toward the stairwell. He disappeared into the shadows of the residential block, his robes billowing around him, desperate to reach the privacy of his room to rinse his mouth and scrub away the evidence of his own vices.\n\nEliza stood perfectly still, watching the space where he had been.\n\nAn unusual hardness settled in her gaze. It wasn't the quivering relief she had felt in the past—the feeling of *'Thank God he's gone.'* It was a cold, distant disgust. She looked at the empty hallway and felt a surge of quiet triumph. The predator had been spooked by his own reflection.\n\nShe turned silently and continued toward the Almoner's office, her steps light, her heart beating with a secret, golden rhythm.",
