      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 9:45pm)\n\nThe room felt unusually still after Eliza left, the air still humming with the ghost of that unexpected embrace. Cecilia remained on the edge of her bed, her arms still vaguely curled as if she were holding the shape of the hug. She blinked, her brown eyes wide and thoughtful, her mind processing the event with the same unhurried, methodical nature that governed her entire life.\n\n*She hugged me,* Cecilia thought, a small, puzzled smile touching her lips. *Eliza hugged me.*\n\nIt was a strange thing to contemplate. In the creches, and later in the acolytal dorms, physical affection was treated as a danger—a slippery slope toward the \"Contamination.\" Touch was for duty, for cleaning, for the mandated reproductive acts of the state. A hug was a gesture of intimacy, and intimacy was a liability. Yet, as she sat there, Cecilia didn't feel a sense of spiritual peril. She felt a warm, radiating glow of satisfaction.\n\nShe looked at the doorway where Eliza had vanished, her expression one of quiet, maternal pride. To Cecilia, this wasn't a rebellion or a descent into sin; it was a healing. She truly believed that Eliza had finally found the strength to accept the Church's grace, that the anxiety and the \"darkness\" the girl had complained about were finally being washed away by a renewed sense of faith.\n\n\"She's finally coming around,\" Cecilia whispered to the empty room, her voice a soft, satisfied hum.\n\nShe stood up and began to perform her nightly routine with her usual, meticulous care. She folded her robes, placed her shoes exactly parallel to the bedframe, and dimmed the lamp. Every action was a ritual of order, a way of maintaining the stability she craved.\n\nBut as she lay down and pulled the heavy wool blanket up to her chin, her thoughts returned to the look in Eliza's eyes. There had been something there—a spark, a secret, a flash of something that didn't look like the \"peace\" the Church taught. It looked like a victory.\n\nCecilia stared up at the dark ceiling, listening to the distant, rhythmic thumping of the Cathedral's machinery. She didn't know about the Testifiers, the honey-gold seed, or the white-scaled Matron in a basement. She didn't know that her friend was currently scrubbing the evidence of a divine transgression off her skin in a communal tub.\n\nShe only knew that for the first time in years, she didn't feel the need to pray for Eliza's soul before she slept.\n\n*I'm just glad she's happy,* Cecilia thought, closing her eyes. *I'm just glad she's finally found her balance.*\n\nAs she drifted off into a dreamless, uncomplicated sleep, she remained the only person in the building who truly believed that the world was exactly as the High Inquisitor said it was. She was the perfect citizen of Temperantia—the one who loved the cage because she had forgotten it had bars.",
