      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 8:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 8:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Monday; September; 22\nTime: 10:00pm)\n\n\nThe sanctuary of the warehouse was a place of liberation for the flock, but for Verali, it was a place of work. Her own living quarters were a stark contrast to the divine, maternal image she projected during the gatherings. It was a small, cramped attic space in a derelict tenement, a room that smelled of old rain, charcoal smoke, and the lingering, metallic tang of the industrial district.\n\nThe mattress she slept upon was a pathetic thing—a thin, stained piece of foam that had long since lost its elasticity. It was far too small for her imposing frame. Verali lay upon it now, her long, muscular body forced into a tight, unnatural curl, her knees tucked toward her chest and her wings folded awkwardly against the peeling wallpaper of the wall. She looked less like a goddess and more like a creature trapped in a box, a predator forced into a miniature cage.\n\nThe room was dark, save for the weak, flickering light of a single oil lamp that cast long, dancing shadows across the ceiling. Normally, this solitude was her solace—the place where she could shed the weight of her role and simply *be*. But tonight, the silence felt hollow. The air felt empty.\n\nVerali stared at the ceiling, her golden eyes tracking the slow crawl of a spider in the corner, but her mind was miles away, locked in a dim utility closet with a blonde girl who had a heart like a supernova.\n\n*Eliza.*\n\nEven the thought of the name sent a ripple of heat through Verali's scales. She shifted on the mattress, the friction of the coarse fabric against her skin reminding her of the softness she had held. Verali had spent decades as a provider, a catalyst, a source of spiritual medicine. She had an exhaustive library of experiences—she knew how to dismantle a person's shame, how to guide a soul toward its own desire, how to be the steady, unmoving anchor for the drowning.\n\nBut she had never felt a fire like the one that lived inside Eliza.\n\nIt wasn't just the catalyst—though the seed had certainly stripped away the girl's armor. It was the *intent*. There was a raw, desperate hunger in Eliza that hadn't been a request for healing or a plea for liberation. It had been a demand. A starving girl who had finally found food and decided to devour the entire feast.\n\nVerali closed her eyes, and the memory of the girl's mouth—the way she had sucked on Verali's finger with a feral, uninhibited greed—surfaced. She remembered the way Eliza had looked at her: not as a relic to be revered, but as a woman to be wanted.\n\nGods, Verali thought, her breath hitching in the dark, she wanted to be burned again.\n\nShe wanted to feel that desperate, clumsy energy crashing against her. She wanted the risk, the danger, and the absolute, terrifying honesty of Eliza's need. For the first time in her life, the Matron didn't want to be the one holding the light; she wanted to be consumed by the flame.\n\nShe curled tighter into herself, the mattress creaking under her weight. She was the anchor of the cell, the protector of the pariahs, the steady hand in the storm. But as she lay there in the suffocating dark of her small room, Verali realized that she was no longer the only one who had been saved. Eliza had broken the lock on a door Verali hadn't even realized was closed.\n\n*I am waiting for you,* she whispered into the shadows, the words a low, melodic hum. *And I will wait as long as I need.*",
