      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe two days that followed were a blur of mechanical repetition. The Cathedral had returned to its rhythmic, suffocating pulse—the scrubbing of the sacristy floors, the precise folding of the liturgical linens, the hushed whispers in the corridors. To any observer, Eliza was the same ghost she had always been, a compliant shadow in a black-and-white habit. But beneath the heavy fabric, her skin felt different. Every time she closed her eyes, she could still feel the ghost of Verali’s touch; every breath she took seemed to carry a hint of that musk and salt. The habit no longer felt like a garment of piety; it felt like a costume, a thin veil she wore to hide the fire she now carried in her belly.\n\nThe afternoon service had just ended, the heavy air of the nave still thick with the smell of incense and the lingering echoes of the choir's hypnotic chanting. Eliza stood in the Triforium gallery, her hands clasped tightly in front of her, looking down at the sea of bowed heads as the parishioners began to file out toward the narthex. Some lingered by the confessionals, their shoulders slumped in a performative display of guilt, while others hurried away, eager to return to their drab lives.\n\nNormally, this was Eliza's sanctuary—the time when she could discreetly zone out, letting her mind drift toward the warehouse, imagining the gold of Verali's eyes and the strength of her wings. But today, the silence in her head was replaced by a jarring, metallic ringing.\n\nHigh Inquisitor Justinian had spoken today. His voice, cold and precise, had sliced through the nave like a scalpel. He hadn't just preached temperance; he had spoken of *contamination*. He had painted a picture of the dragon-kin—not as people, but as biological hazards, as spiritual parasites that preyed upon the weak-willed. He had used words like 'abomination' and 'filth,' describing their biology as a corruption of the divine image, a mistake of nature that needed to be pruned for the species to survive.\n\nEliza felt a hot, sickening surge of nausea rise in her throat. She looked down at the crowd, and for a moment, the faces of the faithful looked like masks of stone.\n\n*You liar,* she thought, her fingers digging into the fabric of her sleeves. *You arrogant, hollow liar! The only corruption in this city is you. The only filth is the way you use fear to chain us.*\n\nShe wanted to lean over the railing and scream it. She wanted to shriek at the top of her lungs, to tell them all that the 'monstrosity' Justinian feared was the only thing that had ever made her feel human. She wanted to tell them that the 'contamination' was actually a liberation.\n\nBut the scream died in her throat, swallowed by the crushing weight of the architecture around her. She forced her expression back into one of vacant humility, pushing the anger down into a cold, hard knot of resolve. She could not fight him with words—not yet. She had to be the ghost.\n\n\"Are you alright, Eliza?\" Cecilia whispered beside her, her voice laced with genuine concern. \"You look... distant. Did the Word touch you deeply today?\"\n\nEliza blinked, her gaze returning to her roommate. She offered a small, fragile smile—the mask slipping perfectly back into place. \"Yes, Cecilia. I was just... reflecting on the gravity of the Inquisitor's warnings.\"\n\nAs they descended the stone stairs to the ground floor, the air grew cooler, the echo of their footsteps ringing against the walls. They had barely reached the transition zone between the residential block and the nave when a priest intercepted them. He was a lean man with a face like a pinched piece of parchment, his eyes narrow and suspicious.\n\n\"Sisters,\" he said, his voice a dry rasp. \"The Almoner has summoned you to the print shop in the rear courtyard.\"\n\nEliza felt a flicker of anxiety. The print shop was secluded, a place of ink and heavy machinery. The staff that worked there seemed of a different breed and didn't mingle with the acolytes much. It was also off-limits to acolytes most of the time. She exchanged a worried glance with Cecilia, her heart beginning to hammer against her ribs, though she kept her head bowed in deferential silence.",
