      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe rear courtyard was a place of damp earth and grey stone, but as they approached the print shop, the atmosphere shifted. The building was a squat, utilitarian structure of brick and soot, tucked away from the main sanctuary. As Eliza stepped through the heavy wooden door, she was hit by a sensory assault that nearly knocked her breathless.\n\nThe noise was the first thing—a rhythmic, mechanical violence. The printing presses were old, heavy iron beasts that roared and clanked with a deafening, industrial hunger. Every strike of the press sounded like a hammer falling on an anvil, vibrating through the floorboards and rattling the very bones of her chest. It was a claustrophobic space, smelling of acrid ink, burnt oil, and the dampness of old paper. The air felt thick, almost greasy, as the roar of the machinery drowned out every other sound, turning the world into a blur of mechanical aggression.\n\nThe Almoner was a small, nervous man who seemed to be vibrating in sync with the machinery. He spotted them through the haze of ink-dust, his thick glasses reflecting the dim light as he blinked rapidly. He stepped toward them, his mouth moving, though his voice was nearly swallowed by the roar of the presses.\n\n\"Oh good, you're here!\" he shouted, leaning in close to be heard. He didn't look at them with warmth, but with the frantic energy of a man who just wanted a task off his ledger. He pointed a trembling finger toward a heavy oak table where stacks of fresh paper lay. \"We need you two to distribute those around the market and surrounding streets!\"\n\nBeside the stacks were a couple of small, ceramic pots filled with a thick, translucent paste-glue and a handful of coarse brushes.\n\nEliza and Cecilia nodded in silent, obedient acknowledgment. There was no room for questions here; the Almoner’s urgency was the only command. They began to collect the posters and the glue, the heavy paper feeling crisp and cold beneath Eliza's fingertips.\n\nAs they stepped back out into the dreary, overcast light of the courtyard, Eliza paused. She looked down at the top poster in her stack, and the air suddenly felt too thin to breathe.\n\nIt was a propaganda piece, printed in a stark, aggressive font that seemed to scream from the page. The text warned the populace that dragon-essence was a \"mind and spirit-altering substance,\" a poison that stripped a soul of its piety and left the victim a hollow shell of desire. It warned that the production, sale, purchase, or possession of such substances would be met with \"severe punishments\"—a coded promise of the Inquisition's dungeons.\n\nIn the background of the text was a crude, amateurish illustration of a dragon-kin. The figure was depicted in heavy iron cuffs and chains, its head bowed in a posture of forced submission, designed to make the creature look pathetic and dangerous all at once.\n\nEliza felt a sudden, sharp clench in her heart, as if a physical hand had squeezed her lungs. She looked at the image—the chains, the cuffs—and her mind immediately flashed to Verali. She thought of the Matron's golden eyes, the warmth of her white scales, and the way she had held Eliza as if she were the only precious thing left in a dying world.\n\n*Why do they hate them so?* she wondered, a silent, internal sob racking her chest.\n\nThe irony was a bitter pill. Here she was, a servant of the Church, being used as a tool to spread a lie that targeted the only person who had ever truly seen her. Every poster she pressed against a cold city wall would be a betrayal, a brick in a wall designed to cage the woman she loved. She felt a wave of profound violation, a sense of being complicit in a crime she couldn't even name. She was not just distributing paper; she was distributing a weapon of hate, and her own hands were the ones delivering the blow.",
