    "2026-05-12T20:42:36.889Z": {
      "msg_index": 75,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe walk from the courtyard to the market was a transition from the sterile silence of the Cathedral to the grey, churning noise of the city. The wind was biting, carrying the smell of coal smoke and damp stone, whipping the edges of their habits. As they walked, Cecilia didn't just carry the stack of posters; she studied them. She read the stark warnings over and over, her eyes wide, her breath coming in shallow, nervous puffs.\n\nShe looked over at Eliza, catching a glimpse of her friend's tight expression. Cecilia misinterpreted the tension in Eliza's jaw as the same spiritual alarm she felt. *She's scared too,* Cecilia thought. *We must be vigilant. The Inquisitor's words were so clear; the contamination is everywhere.*\n\nAs the propaganda settled into her mind, Cecilia’s imagination began to warp. She didn't see the dragon-kin as the peaceful, liberated beings they were; instead, she envisioned a towering, scaled monstrosity, a creature of claws and hunger looming out of a dark, rain-slicked alley. In her mind, this beast would snatch her away in a blur of motion, dragging her into the depths of the slums to defile her—stripping away her modesty, her faith, and her very soul until there was nothing left but a hollow shell. A violent shiver racked her frame, making her clutch the posters to her chest as if they were a shield against the unseen.\n\nWhen they finally reached the outskirts of the market, the atmosphere shifted. The crowd thickened—merchants shouting over the din of steam-carts, the smell of fried grease and raw fish filling the air. Cecilia scanned the crowd, her eyes darting with a newfound, anxious intensity.\n\nThen, she saw it.\n\nStanding near a produce stall, blending poorly into the grey backdrop, was a dragon-kin. They were dressed in shabby, oversized robes of coarse brown wool, but the fabric was an insufficient mask. The bulk of their wings created a strange, humped silhouette beneath the cloth, and a long, reptilian snout protruded from the depths of the hood, nostrils flaring as they breathed in the salty air of the market. The creature looked tired, their eyes weary, perhaps just trying to buy a handful of grain to survive the day.\n\nTo Cecilia, it wasn't a tired person; it was a predator in disguise.\n\nShe recoiled violently, her body jerking back as if she had stepped on a snake. Her hand shot out, grasping Eliza’s sleeve with a grip that was almost painful, her knuckles white.\n\n\"Eliza... can... can we maybe... go another way?\" Cecilia whispered, her voice trembling, barely audible over the roar of the market.\n\nEliza looked at her, her voice maintaining that practiced, mild puzzlement. \"Hmm? Why?\"\n\nCecilia’s eyes remained fixed on the figure in the brown robes, her pupils dilated with a primal, conditioned fear. The image of the chained monster from the poster had merged with the reality of the person in front of them.\n\n\"I... I'd rather not be near a corrupting influence if... if we can help it,\" she said, a pleading, fearful note vibrating in her voice. She looked at Eliza with a desperate kind of trust, as if begging her friend to lead her away from a cliff's edge. \"Please. Let's just... go around.\"",
