    "2026-05-12T20:54:43.834Z": {
      "msg_index": 76,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nThe market continued to roar around them, a cacophony of shouting vendors and the rhythmic clatter of steam-carts, but for Eliza, the world had suddenly gone silent. She looked at Cecilia, seeing the genuine terror etched into her friend's face, and then she followed that gaze to the figure in the brown robes.\n\nThe dragon-kin was just... standing there. They were shifting their weight from foot to foot, perhaps waiting for a merchant to weigh a bag of grain, their posture one of exhausted patience. There was nothing predatory about them; there was only a profound, heavy loneliness that seemed to radiate from their very silhouette.\n\nEliza felt a sudden, sharp ache in her chest, a mixture of disappointment and a mourning sadness that threatened to overwhelm her. It was a crushing realization. Cecilia—sweet, kind, trusting Cecilia—had been so thoroughly hollowed out by the Church's teachings that she could look at a living, breathing being and see only a monster. The propaganda posters they had just spent an hour distributing weren't just paper; they were poison, and Cecilia had swallowed every drop.\n\nA hot, stinging sensation rose behind Eliza's eyes. She felt the urge to cry, not out of fear, but out of a sudden, fierce anger toward the men in the high towers of the Cathedral who had taught her friend to hate.\n\n\"Cecilia... please don't say such things,\" Eliza whispered. Her voice was husky, stripped of its usual breathy deference, sounding raw and strained.\n\nCecilia finally tore her eyes away from the dragon-kin to look at Eliza. She looked startled, her eyebrows knitting together in a mask of innocent confusion. \"W-what do you mean?\" she asked, her voice small and trembling, as if she were the one being threatened.\n\nEliza could only meet her eyes briefly. The intensity of her own emotion felt too dangerous, too revealing. She glanced away, her gaze flickering toward the grey, oppressive sky. She fumbled for the words, her mind racing. She couldn't tell her the truth—she couldn't say *'I have been held by a dragon-kin and found the only love I've ever known'*—not here, not in the middle of a market where every passerby could be an informant for the Inquisition.\n\n\"It's...\" Eliza started, her voice barely a ripple in the wind. \"It's... cruel to say things like that.\"\n\nThe reaction was instantaneous. Cecilia's face shifted from fear to a mask of distressed shock. She recoiled slightly, as if Eliza had slapped her. \"Cruel? What do you mean?\"\n\nEliza turned back to her. She felt the tears threatening to spill, but she didn't let them fall. She steadied herself, her gaze becoming firm, an unfamiliar strength anchoring her. She reached out and gently touched Cecilia's arm, her voice soft but unwavering, infused with a tenderness that the Church had spent twenty-one years trying to erase.\n\n\"Nobody chooses how they're born,\" Eliza said gently, her eyes locked onto Cecilia's. \"It's uncharitable to blame someone for something they have no control over.\"",
