    "2026-05-12T21:00:07.063Z": {
      "msg_index": 77,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nCecilia’s breath hitched, a sharp, audible intake of air that seemed to freeze in her lungs. She stared at Eliza, her eyes searching her friend's face for a sign of a joke, a mistake, or a slip of the tongue. But there was no mockery there—only a quiet, steady conviction that Cecilia had never seen in Eliza before.\n\n*Nobody chooses how they're born.*\n\nThe words echoed in the hollow of Cecilia's mind, clashing violently with the images of the posters and the booming warnings of the High Inquisitor. For years, the world had been a series of clear, binary lines: Pure or Corrupt. Worthy or Contaminated. The Church had taught her that the 'monstrosity' of the dragon-kin was a choice of spiritual failure, a biological manifestation of a corrupted soul. But as she looked at Eliza—the girl she had grown up with, the one she trusted more than anyone else in the world—the lines began to blur.\n\n*I... I hadn't thought about it that way,* she realized, her heart drumming a frantic rhythm against her ribs. *Is... is she right? Am I being unfair?*\n\nThe thought felt like a transgression, a small, treasonous seed planted in the garden of her faith. She looked back toward the figure in the brown robes. The dragon-kin hadn't moved; they were still just standing there, a solitary, slumped shape in the grey mist of the market. Suddenly, the image of the chained beast on the poster felt less like a warning and more like a cruelty. She imagined the weight of those iron cuffs, the coldness of the metal, and the sheer, suffocating loneliness of being seen as a monster by every person you passed.\n\nAn abrupt, hot wave of shame stole over her, flushing her cheeks and making her stomach churn. It wasn't the spiritual shame the Church taught—the shame of sin or desire—but a human shame. A shame born from the realization that she had allowed herself to be a tool of hatred.\n\nShe felt the weight of the posters in her arms, the heavy paper now feeling like a leaden burden. She looked down at the stark, aggressive font and the crude drawing of the captive creature, and for the first time, the image didn't evoke fear. It evoked a sudden, sickening sense of pity.\n\nCecilia’s grip on Eliza’s sleeve loosened, her fingers trembling. She didn't know how to reconcile this feeling with the sermons she had heard, and the conflict left her feeling dizzy, as if the ground beneath her feet had shifted.\n\n\"I... I didn't mean to be...\" Cecilia started, her voice small and fragile, trailing off into a whisper. She looked at the dragon-kin again, and this time, she didn't recoil. She stayed where she was, her shoulders slumping, her gaze softening. \"I just... I was told they were dangerous, Eliza. I was told they were... wrong.\"\n\nShe turned back to her friend, her expression one of profound, childlike confusion. \"Do you really believe that? That they... they don't choose it?\"",
