    "2026-05-12T21:06:15.503Z": {
      "msg_index": 78,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe noise of the market seemed to recede into a dull hum, leaving only the two of them in a small, fragile bubble of honesty. Looking at Cecilia—so small, so earnest, and so profoundly conflicted—Eliza felt a wave of protective love that nearly choked her. It was a different kind of love than the one she felt for Verali; this was the love of a sister, a kinship forged in the sterile halls of the creches, a bond of shared survival.\n\nEliza shifted the heavy stack of posters into the crook of one arm, clutching the hateful ink to her side as she reached out with her free hand. She draped her arm around Cecilia's shoulders, pulling the other girl close. The coarse fabric of their habits rubbed together, a familiar, institutional sound, but the gesture was an act of defiance. She was holding her friend not as a fellow acolyte, but as a human being.\n\n\"No more than you chose to be born a prey species... or I a human,\" Eliza murmured, her voice soft and steady, devoid of the breathy hesitation she used for the priests. \"That's how much choice they were given to be what they are.\"\n\nCecilia leaned into her, the tension in her shoulders beginning to ebb, though she still looked haunted. She was standing on the precipice of a great doubt, and Eliza knew that if she pushed too hard, she might break her; but if she didn't push at all, Cecilia would simply go back into the sleep-walking trance of the faithful.\n\nEliza pressed further, her voice taking on a gentle, guiding quality, as if she were teaching a child a secret that the world had tried to hide.\n\n\"Can you control the workings of your body, Cecilia?\" she asked, her gaze searching her friend's eyes. \"Can you stop yourself from... from defecating, or breathing? Can you tell your heart to stop beating simply because the Inquisitor says it is improper?\"\n\nCecilia blinked, a small, hesitant shake of her head.\n\n\"Exactly,\" Eliza whispered. \"The body has its own logic, its own truth. How can they control what their bodies produce? How can a person be 'corrupt' for the simple fact that their blood, their scent, or their skin is different from yours? To call a natural function a sin is not piety, Cecilia... it is just a way to make us afraid of things we don't understand.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Eliza felt a strange, shimmering clarity. For years, she had played the part of the victim, the frightened girl, the obedient vessel. But here, in the middle of the same city that sought to crush her, she felt a spark of the Matron's power within her. She wasn't just saving herself anymore; she was trying to save the only person who had ever been truly kind to her.",
