      "msg_index": 79,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nCecilia remained silent, her gaze drifting back toward the dragon-kin in the brown robes. The figure was now moving away, their slow, heavy tread disappearing into the grey mist of the market square. Every step they took seemed to pull at a string tied to Cecilia's heart, a tether of unexpected empathy that felt both terrifying and right.\n\nHer mind was reeling. The logic Eliza had presented—the simple, undeniable truth of the body—was like a stone dropped into a still pond, sending ripples that disrupted everything she thought she knew. She looked down at the posters in her arms. The stark, black ink now looked like a smudge of filth, a lie printed on expensive paper.\n\n*It's true,* she thought, and the realization felt like a physical weight in her chest. *It's actually true.*\n\nShe could hear the ghost of High Inquisitor Justinian's voice booming in her head, talking of parasites and purity, but the voice sounded distant now, like a shout from across a wide canyon. Against that booming authority, Eliza’s quiet, steady voice was the only thing that felt real. The worst part wasn't the fear of the Inquisitor; the worst part was the discovery that her own heart was disagreeing with her head. She felt a sudden, dizzying sense of humility, a feeling that she had been walking through life with a blindfold on, and Eliza had just reached out to pull it away.\n\n\"I... I think you're right,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice cracking. She didn't look up at Eliza, instead staring at the coarse fabric of her own sleeve. \"About the unkindness. It *is* unkind. To hate someone for... for the way they were born.\"\n\nShe paused, her breath hitching. For a second, it seemed as though she might lean further into the abyss, that she might ask Eliza if the entire Church was a lie. But the fear was still there—a deep, ingrained instinct for survival. She couldn't let go of the rope entirely; it was the only thing keeping her from falling into the unknown.\n\nShe squeezed Eliza's arm, her grip tight and pleading. \"But... but the Church is still the only way, right? We just... we just have to be better. We have to be more merciful than the ones who write the posters.\"\n\nCecilia looked up, her eyes brimming with tears, searching for reassurance. She had accepted the specific point of unkindness, but she was desperately trying to fold that new truth back into the old framework of her faith. She wasn't ready to leave the Cathedral, but she could no longer pretend that the Cathedral's walls were perfectly white.\n\n\"Let's just... let's just finish the work,\" she murmured, her voice regaining some of its usual even tempo, though it was fragile. \"And then let's go home. I don't want to be out here anymore. I don't...feel well.\"",
