    "2026-05-12T21:32:32.396Z": {
      "msg_index": 80,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nEliza gave Cecilia’s shoulder one last, firm squeeze before letting go, the fabric of their habits whispering against each other. She stepped back, watching her friend with a keen, observant eye. As they continued their route through the market's winding alleys, the difference in their movements became stark. Where Eliza moved with a calculated, ghostly efficiency—the mask of the perfect acolyte—Cecilia began to falter.\n\nEach time Cecilia reached a wall, her hand would linger. She would press the glue onto the stone with a hesitant, trembling touch, her eyes scanning the poster as if she were looking at a crime scene. A shadow of deep unease clouded her face, her brow furrowed, her breathing shallow. She was no longer just distributing paper; she was feeling the weight of every lie she was pinning to the city's skin.\n\nAfter they had put up only a dozen or so, the air feeling thick with the smog of the industrial district, Eliza decided she had seen enough of the struggle in Cecilia's eyes. She picked her way through the crowd, dodging a small, rattling cart of scrap metal, and reached out to tap her friend on the shoulder.\n\n\"Let's take a little break and sit down, okay?\" Eliza suggested, her voice soft and breathless, returning to that deferential tone that acted as a camouflage.\n\nCecilia hesitated, glancing nervously toward the street, perhaps fearing a passing warden or a prying eye. But the exhaustion—both physical and spiritual—was etched into her posture. She nodded slowly, a small, fragile movement.\n\nThey found a shaded corner, a narrow gap between two soot-stained brick buildings where the noise of the market became a distant, muffled roar. They sank down onto the cold, damp ground, the stone biting through their robes. They set the remaining stack of posters carefully beside them, the stark images staring up from the pavement.\n\nFor a long moment, there was only the sound of the city breathing—the distant hiss of steam and the rhythmic clatter of the industrial looms from a nearby warehouse.\n\n\"You were right, Cecilia,\" Eliza said finally, her voice barely higher than a whisper, vibrating with a hidden intensity. \"We do need to be better. Those above us... they are just people too. Even the High Inquisitor. They can make mistakes. They can be blinded by their own certainty.\"\n\nThe words were dangerous. In the eyes of the Cathedral, this was more than just a difference of opinion; it was a walk toward the edge of a cliff. To suggest that the hierarchy was fallible was to suggest that the divine order was flawed. It was heresy, plain and simple. But as Eliza looked at Cecilia, she couldn't bear the thought of her friend continuing to believe the lie—to see her spirit slowly crushed by a faith built on the fear of others.\n\nShe just had to be careful. She had to wrap the truth in a layer of gentleness so it wouldn't scare her away.\n\n\"We don't have to contribute to spreading hate, Cecilia,\" Eliza added, her voice gaining a sliver of strength.\n\nCecilia said nothing. She didn't move, didn't even seem to breathe. She simply looked at Eliza with wide, terrified eyes, her pupils dilated. It was the look of someone who had just realized the door to the cage had been left unlocked, and the vast, unknown world outside was suddenly terrifying. She already knew what Eliza was about to suggest; the implication was hanging in the air between them like a lightning bolt.\n\nEliza felt a swarm of butterflies erupt in her stomach—a cocktail of nervousness and a strange, intoxicating thrill. She was gambling with her life, and possibly Cecilia's, but the risk felt honest. For the first time, she wasn't acting out of fear, but out of a desire to protect something precious.\n\n\"We've put up enough that the Almoner will be satisfied,\" Eliza whispered, glancing toward the street to ensure they were still unseen. \"They're not going to come behind us and count every single one. They only care that the work is *done*.\"\n\nShe trailed off, leaving the silence to fill in the blanks. She was asking Cecilia to be a conspirator, to commit a small act of sabotage against the institution they served. She had no faith in the Church—the stone walls and gold crosses were hollow—but she had an absolute, unwavering faith in Cecilia's inherent goodness.",
