    "2026-05-12T21:36:53.192Z": {
      "msg_index": 81,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:45pm)\n\n\nCecilia froze. The world around her seemed to stop; the distant roar of the market became a muffled, underwater thrum. She looked at the stack of posters, then back at Eliza, her chest heaving in short, jagged bursts. The word *sabotage* wasn't spoken, but it was written in the air between them, glowing like a brand.\n\nFor a heartbeat, the old conditioning surged back. *This is wrong. This is sin. This is the path to the dungeons.* She could almost feel the cold grip of a Warden's hand on her arm, the smell of the interrogation rooms, the weight of a penance that would never end. Her stomach lurched, and for a moment, she considered pleading with Eliza to stop, to go back to the safety of the lie.\n\nBut then she looked at Eliza. She saw the strength in her friend's eyes—a strength that wasn't based on a book or a sermon, but on something raw and real. She realized that for the first time in her life, she didn't want to be the \"perfect\" acolyte. Not if it meant becoming something cruel and hateful.\n\nA strange, dizzying feeling washed over her. It was small, barely a spark, but it was *hers*.\n\n\"They won't know,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice trembling.\n\nIt was a dangerous realization. The moment she said it, she felt a shift in her own identity. She was no longer just a follower; she was a liar. And yet, for the first time, the lie felt like a shield rather than a cage.\n\nCecilia looked at the posters again. The image of the chained dragon-kin stared back at her. Slowly, with a deliberate motion that felt like her heart was beating in her fingertips, she reached out. She didn't just pick up the posters; she gathered them into a messy heap, her fingers curling around the edges of the paper.\n\nShe didn't just leave them. She looked around the alleyway, her eyes landing on a deep, stagnant puddle of rainwater and oil that had collected near a rusted drainage grate. The water was black, iridescent with grime, smelling of sulfur and old metal.\n\nWith a sudden, decisive movement, Cecilia pushed the remaining stack of propaganda into the filth.\n\nShe watched as the ink began to bleed, the stark black letters blurring into the murky water. The image of the captive dragon-kin slowly dissolved, the paper soaking through and sinking into the sludge until the hateful words were nothing more than grey smears in the dark.\n\nCecilia let out a long, shuddering breath, her shoulders slumping. She looked at her hands—they were stained with a bit of glue and grime—and then she looked at Eliza. A small, tentative smile touched her lips, though her eyes were still wide with a mixture of terror and exhilaration.\n\n\"I've never... I've never done anything like that before,\" she whispered, her voice sounding small and fragile. \"I feel like I'm floating. Is this... is this what it feels like to be bad, Eliza?\"",
