    "2026-05-12T21:46:22.058Z": {
      "msg_index": 82,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe silence of the alleyway was broken not by the roar of the city, but by a sound that had been absent from Eliza's life for years. A small, bright giggle bubbled up from her throat, escaping before she could catch it. It was a fragile, girlish sound, completely out of place amidst the soot-stained bricks and the smell of sulfur.\n\nLooking at Cecilia—standing there with glue-stained fingers and a look of bewildered terror—Eliza felt a surge of affection so potent it felt like a physical weight. Without a word, she threw her arms around the other girl, pulling her into a fierce, tight embrace. She pressed her face into the softness of Cecilia's wool, the scent of the other girl—clean, like laundry soap and something faintly sweet—filling her senses.\n\n\"Do you *feel* bad, Cecilia?\" Eliza whispered into her shoulder, her voice light, almost playful, though it trembled with the intensity of the moment.\n\nCecilia remained stiff for a second, then slowly relaxed, her own arms winding around Eliza's waist. She shook her head, the thick, white curls of her wool brushing against Eliza's cheek with a soft, tactile warmth.\n\n\"No...\" Cecilia whispered, her voice barely audible, a tiny, fragile thing. \"I... I feel like... it was the right thing to do... and... and I don't know why that scares me...\"\n\nThe admission hit Eliza like a physical blow. It was the first crack in the dam, the first time Cecilia had admitted that the inner voice of her own conscience was louder than the voice of the pulpit. It was a terrifying transition—the moment a child realizes their parents have been lying to them. Eliza squeezed her friend tighter, anchoring her, letting her know that she wasn't alone in the void.\n\n\"It's okay, Cecilia. I know,\" Eliza murmured, her voice grounding and steady. \"I know. But you're a good person.\"\n\nShe stepped back slowly, the space between them feeling charged with a new, secret understanding. Eliza held her at arm's length, her hands gently gripping Cecilia's shoulders, the heavy fabric of the habit bunching beneath her fingers. She looked deep into Cecilia's brown eyes, seeing the confusion, the fear, and the burgeoning trust.\n\nEliza's throat tightened, and her voice became husky, thick with an emotion she could no longer mask. Tears pricked at the corners of her eyes, blurring the edges of the grey world around them.\n\n\"Actually... I think you're the best person I've ever known,\" she said, her voice cracking slightly.\n\nIt was the most honest thing she had ever said within the walls of this city. No performance, no ecclesiastical phrasing, no mask. Just a girl telling her friend that she was loved.\n\n\"So...\" Eliza continued, a tear finally escaping and tracing a hot line down her cheek. \"I think as long as you follow what your heart tells you is right, the Mother will see you for the kind soul you are.\"\n\nShe used the term 'the Mother'—not the institutionalized Lady Idella of the stained-glass windows, but a concept of maternal love and grace that she had felt in Verali's arms. She was offering Cecilia a new kind of faith, one not built on submission and shame, but on the holiness of the self. For a moment, in that dirty, forgotten alley, the two of them were the only things that were truly pure.",
