      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\nCecilia stared at Eliza, her mouth slightly open. The words *'the best person I've ever known'* felt like a physical touch, warmer and more sudden than any embrace. In the Cathedral, praise was always conditional—you were \"good\" because you were obedient, \"pious\" because you were silent, \"worthy\" because you followed the rules. No one had ever called her *good* simply for being herself.\n\nThe tear on Eliza's cheek caught the dim light, and Cecilia felt a sudden, desperate urge to reach out and wipe it away. She didn't, but her hand twitched against her side. The air between them felt thick, humming with a frequency that Cecilia didn't recognize—a resonance of shared secrecy and a bond that had suddenly shifted from mere companionship to something far more dangerous.\n\n\"I... I don't know if I'm a good person,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice small, her brown eyes searching Eliza's. \"I've just... I've just always done what I was told. I thought that *was* being good.\"\n\nShe looked back at the puddle of black water where the posters had dissolved. The fear was still there, a cold knot in her stomach, but it was being crowded out by a strange, blossoming sense of autonomy. For the first time, the thought of the High Inquisitor didn't make her feel like a small, insignificant insect. It made her feel like a witness to a crime.\n\n\"You are, Cecilia,\" Eliza insisted in a husky whisper, \"You saw a living being and decided they shouldn't be hated for things outside of their control. Tell me where the evil is in that?\"\n\nCecilia glanced away, shaking her head minutely. \"I...I don't know...Eliza.\"\n\nEliza took her hand, tugging slightly, away from the mound of inky pulp dissolving in the alley. \"Come on, Cecilia. Let's...let's go for a walk and get away from the crowds for a little while.\"\n\nSniffling, Cecilia nodded, and allowed herself to be led. As they walked, she found herself leaning closer to Eliza than usual, their shoulders rubbing together with every step. The world felt different now—the grey buildings were the same, the soot was the same, but the silence between them was no longer a void; it was a bridge.\n\n\"Eliza?\" Cecilia asked, her voice regaining that unhurried, plain quality, though it was tinged with a newfound curiosity. \"Why did you say the Mother would see me? You...you didn't say 'The Church' or 'The High Inquisitor.' You just said... the Mother.\"\n\nShe stopped walking for a second, looking up at her friend with a puzzled expression. \"Since when do you believe the Mother listens to us without the priests?\"",
