      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe question hung in the air, a fragile, shimmering thread of curiosity. Cecilia was looking at her with such earnestness, such a genuine desire to understand the architecture of Eliza's faith, that it almost felt like a trap. It was a reminder of how delicate this moment was; one wrong word, one too-bold a claim, and the bridge they had built in that alleyway could collapse into a chasm of fear.\n\nEliza paused. She looked at the skyline of the city—the jagged silhouettes of the industrial chimneys belching thick, greasy smoke into the grey sky, the distant, towering spire of the Cathedral that dominated everything like a watchful eye. She thought of the rituals, the scripted prayers, and the way the clergy acted as the only gatekeepers to the divine. She thought of the *Supplicatio*—the idea that a woman's plea for mercy or life had to be bought with coin or labor, filtered through a man who decided if her soul was \"worthy\" enough to be heard.\n\nAnd then, she thought of Father Gaspard.\n\nThe memory of him—his predatory gaze, the way he looked at her as if she were a piece of fruit to be peeled, the suffocating atmosphere of his office—surfaced in her mind like a piece of cold iron. The disgust she felt for him wasn't just a flicker anymore; it was a steady, freezing current.\n\nWhen she turned back to Cecilia, her expression had shifted. The tenderness was still there, but it was now edged with a hardness, a crystalline sharpness that had been forged in the secret heat of the warehouse.\n\nThe answer, when it came, was colder than she had intended it to be. It didn't come from the soft, breathless acolyte; it came from a woman who had seen the truth behind the curtain.\n\n\"I can't believe the Mother would listen to the prayers of someone like Father Gaspard,\" Eliza said, her voice cutting through the damp air with a sudden, jarring precision, \"and not those of you or I.\"\n\nThe words felt heavy, like stones dropped into a still pool. The sheer audacity of the statement—the claim that a simple acolyte's voice was more precious to the divine than that of a high-ranking Examiner—was an act of spiritual rebellion.\n\nEliza realized the coldness of her tone a moment later and softened her gaze, but she didn't take the words back. She couldn't. Not now. Not after the way Gaspard had looked at her. Not after she had felt the warmth of Verali's truth.\n\n\"He speaks of purity, Cecilia,\" Eliza added, her voice dropping back into a conspiratorial whisper, \"but he is the one who is stained. He uses the Mother's name to hide his own hunger. Why would she listen to a man who only prays for his own appetites?\"",
