      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:45pm)\n\n\nCecilia stopped dead in her tracks. She didn't just stop; she recoiled, her body jerking back as if she had touched a hot stove. Her eyes went wide, and for a moment, she looked as though she couldn't breathe. The words *'stained'* and *'hunger'* regarding a Father of the Church were not just heresy—they were a rupture in the fabric of her reality.\n\nIn Cecilia's world, the clergy were the bedrock. They were the only ones who knew the way. To suggest that Father Gaspard—a man who held the keys to her spiritual standing—was actually the one who was corrupt was like saying the sun was actually a black hole. It was a thought so dangerous it felt physically violent.\n\n\"Eliza!\" she hissed, her voice a sharp, panicked whisper. She looked around frantically, her eyes darting to the nearby rooftops and the passing strangers, terrified that someone had overheard. \"Don't... don't say that. You can't say that! If an Inquisitor heard you... if *he* heard you...\"\n\nThe fear returned, but this time it was a different kind of fear. It wasn't the fear of a monster in the alley; it was the fear of the system. The fear of the machine that could erase a person from the records, turn them into a pariah, or lock them in a room until they forgot their own name.\n\nBut as she looked at Eliza, she saw that her friend wasn't trembling. Eliza looked... calm. She looked certain. And that certainty was a magnet, pulling Cecilia toward a conclusion she didn't want to reach.\n\nCecilia's mind flashed back to the way Gaspard spoke to the acolytes—the way he leaned in too close, the way his voice had a grating, wheezing quality that always made her skin crawl, though she'd always told herself it was just 'clerical gravity.' She remembered the way he looked at Eliza, that predatory tilt that had haunted her since the start of the week.\n\nShe had called it 'discipline.' She had called it 'guidance.'\n\nNow, looking at Eliza's hardened expression, a new word entered Cecilia's vocabulary. *Predator.*\n\nThe word felt wrong in her mouth, a jagged piece of glass. But as she looked at Eliza, the piece of glass began to fit. If Eliza—the most demure, the most obedient of them all—could look at Father Gaspard and see a man of hunger, then perhaps the mask wasn't on Eliza. Perhaps the mask was on him.\n\n\"Is... is he really like that?\" Cecilia whispered, her voice trembling, the fear now mixing with a sudden, sickening curiosity. \"I mean... I've always felt... uneasy... when he's near. I thought it was just because I was nervous. I thought I was the one who was failing.\"\n\nShe looked at Eliza, her brown eyes brimming with a mixture of terror and a desperate need for confirmation. \"Does he... does he do things? To the others? To you?\"",
