      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe world around them seemed to shrink, the grey streets and the smog-choked sky fading into a blur. For the first time in years, Eliza felt as if she were deflating—the rigid, breathless posture of the perfect acolyte simply collapsing. The mask she had worn since she was a child in the creches, a mask crafted from a thousand repetitions of *\"Yes, Father\"* and *\"I am but a vessel,\"* was finally slipping. It didn't just slip; it shattered.\n\nShe looked at Cecilia and felt a dizzying sense of vertigo. She was pouring out truths that no Inquisitor, no matter how skilled in the art of the break, could have dragged out of her. The secret was a physical weight leaving her chest, a long, shuddering exhale that felt like the first real breath she had taken in a decade.\n\nA sudden, sharp instinct flared in the back of her mind—a warning. *The Testifiers.* She caught herself just as she was about to mention the sanctuary, the white-scaled Matron, the gold of the seed. She clamped her jaw shut for a fraction of a second. *No. Not yet. Not this.* If her tongue became too loose, if Cecilia’s own fear outweighed her newfound empathy, a single slip could send them both to the dungeons. Some secrets were too holy, too dangerous, to be shared until the other person was truly ready to step off the ledge.\n\nBut Gaspard? Gaspard was a monster, and monsters deserved to be named.\n\n\"He doesn't just... 'do things,' Cecilia,\" Eliza whispered, her voice trembling, the ecclesiastical formality gone, replaced by a raw, jagged edge. \"He's been tormenting me. For years. Every time I walk past his office, every time he catches me in the hall, it's the same. He doesn't see me as a sister, or even as a human. I am a countdown to him.\"\n\nShe gripped Cecilia's hand, her fingers digging into the other girl's skin.\n\n\"He holds my birthday over my head like a blade,\" Eliza continued, her eyes wide and shimmering with a mixture of old fear and new disgust. \"He tells me that when I reach the deadline, he will be the one to 'guide' me. He talks about it as if it's a gift he's granting me, as if my body is a prize he's already claimed.\"\n\nShe let out a short, bitter laugh that sounded almost like a sob.\n\n\"And the lots... the system we were told protects us, the one that ensures fairness and piety in the reproduction process... he brags about it, Cecilia. He *brags* that he can rig it. That the system is a formality for the masses, but for him, it's a toy. He’s told me—to my face—that no matter whose name is drawn, he can make sure it's *him*.\"\n\nEliza looked up at the towering spire of the Cathedral, the stone reaching up to a god that felt silent and distant.\n\n\"And the most terrifying part?\" Eliza whispered, her voice barely audible over the wind. \"Nobody stops him. Nobody holds him accountable. He is the Examiner. He is the one who decides who is sinful. How can you fight a man who owns the definition of sin?\"",
