      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:45pm)\n\n\nCecilia’s hand went limp in Eliza’s grip, her fingers trembling. She looked at the Cathedral spire, and for the first time, it didn't look like a beacon of hope or a stairway to heaven. It looked like a giant, grey finger pointing down at them, pinning them to the earth.\n\nThe concept of \"the lots\" had been a fundamental truth for Cecilia. The lottery was the divine mechanism—the fair, unbiased way that Lady Idella's will was manifested. It was the only thing that made the biological deadline bearable; the idea that it was a random act of grace, a lottery of survival. To hear that it was a \"toy\" for a man like Gaspard was like hearing that the air she breathed was actually a poisonous gas.\n\nShe felt a sudden, violent wave of nausea. She thought of the other girls in the creches—the ones who had already reached the deadline, the ones who had been \"guided\" and returned with vacant eyes and a shattered spirit. She had always viewed their trauma as a necessary sacrifice for the species. Now, the thought that their suffering might have been the result of a man's whim, rather than a divine plan, made her feel sick.\n\n\"He... he can't,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice sounding hollow, as if she were speaking from the bottom of a well. \"He can't just... change the names. That's... that's impossible. The records are sealed. The Almoner oversees the boxes.\"\n\nBut the doubt was already there, a black ink spreading through the water. She looked at Eliza, seeing the raw, jagged pain in her friend's eyes, and realized that the \"impossible\" was happening every single day.\n\nA strange, protective instinct flared up inside Cecilia. She had spent her life wanting to be the perfect vessel, but seeing the predator's shadow over Eliza made her feel a sudden, sharp spark of anger. It was a small, flickering thing, but it was the first time in her life she had felt anger that wasn't directed at herself for a mistake.\n\n\"Why didn't you tell me?\" she asked, her voice regaining some of its plain, unhurried strength, though it was now laced with a trembling urgency. \"Why did you keep it inside? I... I would have... I would have...\"\n\nShe stopped, realizing she didn't know what she would have done. She was a girl who followed rules. She didn't know how to fight a Father of the Church. But as she looked at Eliza, she realized that the secret they now shared—the dissolved posters, the hatred for Gaspard—was a bond stronger than any ritual.\n\n\"We can't go back to the Cathedral and just... pretend,\" Cecilia said, her brown eyes filling with tears. \"How can we go back and look at him? How can I look at him and not see... a monster?\"\n\nShe clutched Eliza's hand back, her grip tight and desperate. \"What do we do, Eliza? Please... tell me what we do. I don't want you to be a prize. I don't want any of us to be prizes.\"",
