      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\nThe silence that followed was heavy, punctuated only by the distant, rhythmic clatter of the city's industrial lungs. The air felt colder now, the grey mist of the afternoon settling into the gaps between the buildings like a shroud. Looking at Cecilia—whose face was a map of terror and newfound empathy—Eliza felt a surge of profound tenderness. Cecilia was standing on the edge of a cliff, her eyes wide and searching, waiting for Eliza to give her a map, a weapon, or a plan of escape.\n\nBut Eliza knew that there was no simple plan. There was no secret army waiting in the wings to storm the Cathedral, and no magic word that could strip Gaspard of his power. The power he held was structural; it was woven into the very stone of the city.\n\nEliza’s expression softened, the hardness in her eyes melting back into a look of deep, soulful affection. She reached out and took Cecilia's hand again, not with the desperate grip of a conspirator, but with a gentle, grounding squeeze. Her thumb stroked the back of Cecilia's hand, a small, rhythmic motion intended to soothe the trembling that still plagued the other girl.\n\n\"We... we can't *do* anything, Cecilia,\" Eliza whispered, her voice returning to that soft, breathless quality, though it carried a weight of maturity that hadn't been there before. \"Not with the world. Not with the Church. We cannot tear down the walls or change the hearts of men who enjoy the shadow they cast.\"\n\nShe paused, her gaze intensifying, locking onto Cecilia's brown eyes.\n\n\"Except be true to ourselves,\" she added, the words a quiet vow. \"That is the only rebellion that matters. To look at the lie and call it a lie. To hold onto the part of us that they couldn't scrub away in the creches. If we can do that, then they haven't actually won.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Eliza felt a shift in her internal landscape. She looked away from Cecilia, her gaze drifting toward the horizon where the soot-stained skyline met the oppressive grey of the sky. A dark cloud of complex emotions washed over her face—a mixture of mourning for the girl she used to be and a fierce, secret pride in the woman she was becoming. She thought of the warehouse, the white scales, and the way the air had tasted of salt and freedom.\n\nShe realized that the fear which had once defined her relationship with Gaspard had undergone a chemical change. It had been a cold, paralyzing dread; now, it was a cool, distant disgust. He could threaten her, he could manipulate the lots, and he could claim her body as a prize, but he could never touch the same thing that Verali had. He could not take from her what she had already given freely and joyfully to another.\n\nShe turned back to Cecilia, a small, enigmatic smile touching the corners of her lips—a look of hidden knowledge that the priests could never decode.\n\n\"Anyway... don't worry about me, Cecilia,\" Eliza said, her tone light, almost airy. \"I've... made my peace with Father Gaspard. He doesn't scare me anymore.\"\n\nThe statement was a paradox—a lie wrapped in a truth. She was still in danger, but she was no longer a victim. The power dynamic had shifted in a way Gaspard couldn't possibly perceive.\n\n\"I'll do my duty as I must,\" she finished, the phrase sounding like a scripted line from a prayer, but the meaning behind it was entirely her own. She was going back into the cage, but this time, she knew where the key was hidden.",
