    "2026-05-12T22:49:57.087Z": {
      "msg_index": 90,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\nThe question hung in the air, a fragile, shimmering thing. Cecilia’s gaze was so searching, so hungry for a truth that could finally bridge the gap between them, that Eliza felt a sudden, sharp pang of vertigo. She looked at her friend—the girl who had just learned how to hate a lie, the girl who had just discovered the concept of mercy—and for a fleeting second, she wanted to tell her everything.\n\nShe wanted to tell her about the warehouse. She wanted to describe the way Verali’s scales felt beneath her fingertips, the intoxicating scent of musk and salt, and the way the world had vanished when she had finally, *finally* been touched by someone who saw her as a woman rather than a vessel. She wanted to tell her that the \"contamination\" was actually the only cure for the sickness of the soul.\n\nBut as the words formed in her throat, a cold, visceral warning flared in her chest.\n\n*Verali.*\n\nThe Matron was the anchor for so many. She was the sanctuary for the broken, the provider for the pariahs. If Eliza spoke her name, if she described the location, she wasn't just sharing a secret—she was painting a target on the back of the only person who had ever truly loved her. Even with Cecilia's newfound empathy, the risk was too great. The Inquisition didn't just hunt sinners; they hunted the *source* of the sin. If Cecilia’s curiosity turned into a confession, or if a slip of the tongue happened in the dormitory, the result would be a raid. It would be chains, screams, and the extinguishing of the only light in this city.\n\nEliza looked away, her gaze drifting back to the oppressive grey of the skyline, her eyes tracing the jagged line where the soot-stained chimneys met the leaden clouds. The silence stretched between them, no longer a bridge, but a boundary.\n\n\"I just... I had my eyes opened, Cecilia,\" she said, her voice barely a whisper, a soft and breathless sound that seemed to vibrate with a hidden, aching weight.\n\nShe didn't look back at her friend yet. She kept her eyes on the distance, imagining the gold of the Matron's eyes and the strength of those wings. She felt the phantom heat of the catalyst seed still humming in her veins, a secret fire that kept her warm even in the damp, biting wind of the market.\n\n\"And I found something that... that I thought would never be allowed to me,\" she continued, her voice trembling slightly. It wasn't just about the physical act; it was about the autonomy, the sudden, shocking realization that her body belonged to *her*, not to the Church, not to the lots, and certainly not to Gaspard.\n\nSlowly, Eliza turned back to Cecilia. She didn't use the mask this time. She let the vulnerability show, the raw, exposed nerves of a woman who had been stripped bare and found herself beautiful for the first time. She reached out, her fingers grazing Cecilia's cheek with a tenderness that felt like a prayer.\n\n\"Try not to judge me too harshly, Cecilia,\" she whispered, her voice thick with a mixture of grief and triumph, her eyes searching her friend's face for any sign of the old conditioning. \"I was dying.\"\n\nThe word *dying* wasn't a metaphor. It was the truth. For twenty-one years, she had been a ghost in a black-and-white robe, a spirit fading into the grey stone of the Cathedral. She had been a candle burning in a vacuum, waiting for the oxygen to run out. She had been a vessel with nothing inside but a hollow, echoing void.\n\nIn that moment, Eliza wasn't an acolyte, a rebel, or a sinner. She was simply a survivor, reaching out to another survivor, hoping that the truth of her own salvation wouldn't be seen as a betrayal.",
