    "2026-05-12T22:53:33.015Z": {
      "msg_index": 91,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 1:45pm)\n\n\nThe touch of Eliza's fingers on her cheek was light, but it felt like a brand. Cecilia froze, her breath hitching in her throat, her eyes locking onto Eliza's. The word *dying* echoed in her head, a heavy, singular note that vibrated through her entire being.\n\nIn the creches, death was a biological fact—the plague, the famine, the failure of the body. But the way Eliza said it... she wasn't talking about the heart stopping or the lungs failing. She was talking about a death of the spirit. A slow, grey erasure of the self.\n\nCecilia looked at the girl before her, and for the first time, she truly *saw* her. She saw the tension in the set of Eliza's shoulders, the way her eyes seemed to hold a light that the Cathedral's lamps could never produce, and the raw, exposed vulnerability of her expression.\n\n*Dying.*\n\nSuddenly, the mirrored life Cecilia had led—the habits, the prayers, the same-colored robes, the same-colored thoughts—felt like a shroud. She had thought she was living, but she was only existing. She had been a piece of furniture in the house of the Mother, a dutiful servant in a museum of silence.\n\nA single tear escaped Cecilia's eye, trailing down her cheek where Eliza's fingers had just been. She didn't wipe it away. She didn't feel the need to be \"tidy\" or \"proper.\" The image of the dragon-kin in the brown robes flashed in her mind again, and the memory of the posters dissolving in the filth.\n\n\"I... I don't judge you,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice thick, the plainness of her speech now infused with a sudden, desperate tenderness. \"I couldn't... I couldn't possibly judge you for wanting to live.\"\n\nShe leaned forward, pressing her forehead against Eliza's. It was a gesture of intimacy they had never shared—a breaking of the physical boundaries that the Church encouraged to prevent \"undue attachment.\" In the sterile corridors of the Cathedral, touch was for ritual or for discipline. This was something else. This was a sanctuary.\n\n\"I don't know what you found,\" Cecilia murmured, her eyes closing, \"and I don't know where you went. But if it made you... if it made you feel like you're not dying... then I'm glad. I'm so glad.\"\n\nShe pulled back just enough to look into Eliza's eyes, her expression a mixture of profound sadness and a flickering, newborn hope.\n\n\"I'm still scared, Eliza. I'm still so scared that we'll be caught. That we're... that we're broken.\" She paused, her lip trembling. \"But I think I'd rather be broken with you than be perfect and alone.\"",
