      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\nThe walk back to the Cathedral was a journey through a landscape that had been fundamentally rewritten. The city remained the same—the same oppressive smog, the same rattling steam-carts, the same grey, weeping walls—but for Cecilia, the air felt different. It was thick with a new, electric tension. They walked in a shared, uneasy silence, their shoulders occasionally brushing, but the closeness no longer felt like simple companionship. It felt like a conspiracy.\n\nThe uneasiness didn't lie between them; the bond they had forged in the alleyway was the only thing keeping them upright. Instead, the discomfort came from the weight of the truths they had finally given voice to. Every step they took toward the great stone walls of the sanctuary felt like a step back into a cage.\n\nAs they passed through the heavy outer gates and stepped onto the cobblestones of the Cathedral grounds, Cecilia felt a sudden, violent wave of vertigo. The world tilted. The high walls that had once felt like a protective fortress now looked like the ramparts of a prison.\n\n*How is this possible?* she wondered, her breath coming in short, shallow puffs. *How can the world look exactly the same, but feel so completely different?*\n\nShe felt a spike of genuine panic as they entered the main corridor. Every priest they passed, every warden standing guard, every acolyte gliding by in their same black-and-white habits, suddenly seemed like a potential threat. She found herself clutching her habit, her heart hammering a frantic rhythm against her ribs.\n\n*Do they know?* she thought, her eyes darting from face to face. *Can they smell the ink on my hands? Can they see the sin written in my eyes?*\n\nBut as she looked at the serene, vacant expressions of the clergy, the fear shifted. It was no longer just a fear of being caught; it was a sudden, chilling realization. If Eliza was right—if the shepherds were not kind to the flock—then the masks they all wore were not signs of holiness, but camouflage. She looked at a passing priest, a man she had known for years, and for the first time, she didn't see a holy man. She saw a stranger. She saw the possibility of a predator.\n\nA small, petulant part of her—the part of her that was still terrified, the part that loved the safety of the rules—wanted to be angry. She wanted to turn to Eliza and scream at her for this. Why had she done this? Why had she pulled the veil back? It would have been so much easier to stay in the dark, to believe the lies, to be a \"perfect\" vessel in a world of clear lines and simple answers. Why couldn't things have stayed simple?\n\nBut as they reached the stairs leading up to the fourth floor, Cecilia knew that the door had been slammed shut behind them. The knowledge in her heart was an indelible stain, a permanent mark that could never be erased.\n\nShe realized with a heavy, sinking feeling that she could never truly hear a priest pronounce someone a \"sinner\" or a \"contaminated\" without a sickening sense of doubt. She could no longer trust the Word because the Word had been revealed as a tool of control. She was no longer a blind follower; she was a witness. And in the house of the Mother, witnessing the truth was the most dangerous sin of all.",
