      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Wednesday; September; 24\nTime: 5:00pm)\n\n\nThe ascent to the fourth floor felt like a climb through a tomb. Every echoing footstep on the stone stairs seemed to ring out a warning, a rhythmic reminder of the surveillance that permeated the Cathedral. Eliza kept a close, watchful eye on Cecilia. She didn't do so out of a lack of trust—quite the opposite. She did it because she knew that Cecilia's internal compass, so long calibrated to the north pole of the Church's doctrine, was now spinning wildly.\n\nCecilia’s instincts were under-developed; she had lived her life as a passenger in her own existence, allowing the current of the Temperantia to carry her. Now that she had finally stepped off the boat, she was drowning in the sudden depth of her own autonomy. Eliza could sense the shift in her friend's posture—the way her gait had become tentative, her shoulders hunched as if expecting a blow from the very air. Her eyes darted nervously toward every passing acolyte, her gaze flickering with a suspicion that was almost a physical weight.\n\nWhen they finally reached their room, the heavy oak door clicked shut, sealing out the oppressive silence of the corridor. The room was sparse, smelling of old wax and the faint, sterile scent of the linens.\n\nEliza didn't let her go. She gently pulled Cecilia aside, leading her toward the twin beds that defined their shared space. She guided Cecilia down onto the mattress, the springs giving a soft, metallic groan beneath them. Eliza sat down beside her, their shoulders brushing—a point of contact that felt like the only solid thing in a world made of ghosts.\n\n\"Cecilia,\" she said softly, her voice returning to that tender, grounded hue. \"I know... I know it's been a rough day for you. And that we've... talked about a lot of uncomfortable things.\"\n\nEliza felt the tremors still running through Cecilia’s frame, the lingering aftershocks of her spiritual awakening. She reached out, her hand seeking out Cecilia's, and squeezed it gently, the warmth of their skin a silent anchor.\n\n\"But you're as safe today as you were yesterday, and the day before that,\" Eliza continued, her gaze steady and sure. \"And you'll be safe tomorrow, too.\"\n\nIt was a promise, a shield she was offering to hold over her friend. She knew the risk—she knew that safety in the Cathedral was an illusion—but she also knew that Cecilia needed a place to land. She needed to believe that the world hadn't completely dissolved beneath her feet.\n\nEliza searched Cecilia's eyes, looking past the fear to the core of the girl she had grown up with. There was a question she had been wondering since the market, a question born from her own experience with the predators in the priesthood.\n\n\"Has anyone been... *bothering* you?\"\n\nThe word *bothering* was a carefully chosen euphemism. It was a bridge. It was an invitation for Cecilia to name the things that had been whispered in the dark, the subtle touches, the intrusive gazes, the small, daily violations that the Church dismissed as 'discipline.'\n\nCecilia hesitated. Her eyes flickered, a momentary conflict playing out behind her pupils. For a second, Eliza thought she might finally speak a name, a memory, a grievance. Then, Cecilia slowly shook her head.\n\nEliza nodded, a small, relieved exhale escaping her. As if that settled it, as if the absence of a predator was a victory in itself, she leaned in. She shifted her weight, moving closer until their habits overlapped, and lowered her voice into a playful, conspiratorial whisper—the kind of tone used by children sharing secrets in the creche gardens.\n\n\"Then they have no reason to,\" Eliza whispered, a glimmer of a smile dancing in her eyes. \"No matter what they might want you to believe, Cecilia... they can't read your mind. I should know.\"",
