      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\nThe intervening forty-eight hours had been an exercise in agonizing endurance. For Eliza, the Cathedral had transformed from a sanctuary of hiding into a claustrophobic waiting room. Every hour spent scrubbing the sacristy or polishing the nave's pews was a chore performed in a trance of longing. The memory of Verali—the scent of honey and musk, the weight of a gaze that actually *saw* her—had become a fever in her blood. The physical void in her belly, a pulsing, hollow ache that no amount of liturgical humming could soothe, grew more demanding with every passing hour. She felt like a starving woman watching a banquet through a glass pane; she knew the feast existed, and she knew she had been tasted, but the hunger was now a living thing, clawing at her insides.\n\nShe had spent the last two days playing a dangerous game of observation. She had lingered in the corridors, her eyes tracking the Almoner's movements with the intensity of a predator, searching for a gap in the bureaucracy—a misplaced ledger, a distracted clerk, a moment of negligence—that would allow her to glimpse the assignment schedules. She needed to know when she would be sent out on alms or errands, for those were the only windows of time she could carve out to return to Verali's embrace. But the Almoner had been uncharacteristically vigilant, and the opportunities had been nonexistent.\n\nBy Saturday afternoon, the desperation had reached a boiling point.\n\n\"Sister Eliza, Sister Cecilia. In here. Now,\" the Almoner’s voice droned, echoing through the residential block's ground floor.\n\nEliza followed Cecilia into the office, her heart hammering against her ribs. The room smelled of old parchment, ink, and the stale air of a space that saw too many petitioners and too little ventilation. The Almoner sat behind a heavy mahogany desk, his expression one of perpetual, weary irritation. He slid two small purses of coin across the polished wood and a handwritten list of supplies needed for the coming week.\n\n\"The market is bustling this weekend. Ensure the quality of the grains is high; the kitchens have complained about the last shipment,\" the Almoner stated, his tone flat.\n\nAs Cecilia stepped forward, leaning in to clarify a specific item on the list—something about the grade of tallow for the sanctuary candles—the Almoner’s attention shifted. He began a tedious explanation about the purity of the wax, his finger tracing the line on the list, his focus entirely absorbed by the sheep-girl's earnest questions.\n\nEliza saw the opening. It was a sliver of a chance, a momentary lapse in the man's peripheral vision.\n\nWith a practiced, fluid motion, Eliza leaned over the desk, pretending to reach for her purse. Her movements were slow, calculated, her breath held tight in her chest. She shifted her weight, her habit brushing against the mahogany, and her eyes darted instantly to the large, open ledger resting beside the Almoner's elbow.\n\nThe pages were filled with the rigid, ink-black script of the Church's logistics—names, dates, assignments, and destinations. Her vision blurred for a second, the columns of text swimming, but she forced her focus. She didn't need the whole book; she only needed to find her own name. She scanned the entries for the coming week, her eyes hunting for any 'alms' or 'outreach' designations.\n\n*Please,* she whispered internally, her pupils dilating as she searched the lines. *Just one gap. One excuse to leave this place.*",
