    "2026-05-13T02:41:10.550Z": {
      "msg_index": 97,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\nThe air in the Almoner's office felt thick, tasting of old ink and the metallic tang of the coal heaters. Cecilia was focused on the tallow—the mundane, rhythmic detail of the Church's logistics—until she felt a shift in the air beside her. Out of the corner of her eye, she saw Eliza move. It wasn't the movement of a girl reaching for a purse; it was a calculated lean, a predatory tilt of the head.\n\nCecilia’s gaze darted past the Almoner’s shoulder. She saw the ledger. She saw the way Eliza’s eyes were scanning the pages with a hunger that looked almost physical, her pupils darting back and forth across the ink-black lines of the assignment logs.\n\nHer heart leaped into her throat.\n\n*Eliza! What are you doing!?*\n\nA surge of cold panic washed over her. The ledger was the heart of the Cathedral's order; to tamper with it, or even to sneak a glance at unauthorized schedules, was a breach of discipline that could lead to 'spiritual re-education.' The thought of Eliza being dragged to an examination room by Father Gaspard made Cecilia’s stomach churn.\n\nShe felt the Almoner begin to shift. He was finishing his sentence, his torso rotating back toward the center of the desk. If he looked up now, he would catch Eliza in the act of espionage.\n\nCecilia didn't think. She didn't have time to calculate. She simply reacted with the desperation of a girl trying to save her friend from a precipice.\n\nShe lunged forward slightly, her finger stabbing at a line of text further down the list with an urgency that bordered on the frantic.\n\n\"Wait—forgive me, Father!\" she blurted out, her voice a pitch higher than usual, breaking the steady, unhurried cadence she usually maintained. \"The... the oil for the lamps! Is this for the common areas or the nave? I want to make sure I don't accidentally request the refined grade for the hallways. It would be a terrible waste of the Mother's resources, wouldn't it?\"\n\nIt was a nonsense question—she already knew the oil grades—but she leaned in closer, practically crowding the Almoner's space, her fluffy white wool brushing against the desk. She focused her brown eyes intently on the man's bespectacled gaze, her expression a mask of exaggerated, earnest concern. She could feel the heat of the man's irritation, the way he blinked in confusion at her sudden intensity, but she kept her gaze locked on him, acting as a human shield between the Almoner and the ledger.\n\nShe prayed, silently and fervently, that Eliza had found what she needed and would pull back before the Almoner realized he was being played.",
