    "2026-05-13T02:55:10.358Z": {
      "msg_index": 98,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n*There!*\n\nHer eyes locked onto the entry for the following week. *Tuesday. Alms in the Tannery quarter.*\n\nThe words were like a lifeline thrown into a stormy sea. The Tannery quarter was a wretched, stinking place, but it was far enough from the Cathedral's center to allow for a detour—or a complete disappearance for a few hours. In an instant, the suffocating pressure in her chest eased, replaced by a jolt of electric anticipation.\n\nShe pulled back from the desk with a smoothness that was almost ghostly, her face instantly resetting into the mask of the humble, vacant acolyte. The transition was seamless; the hunger vanished from her eyes, replaced by that practiced, distant placidity. She stepped back, folding her hands into her sleeves, her breathing shallow and controlled.\n\nAs they turned to leave, Eliza caught Cecilia’s gaze. The sheep-girl wasn't looking at her with the usual warmth; her brown eyes were wide, flickering with a mixture of genuine fear and a stunned, silent disbelief. It was as if Cecilia had just seen Eliza commit a crime in broad daylight and was waiting for the hammer to fall.\n\nThey took the purses and the list, stepping out of the Almoner's office and navigating the corridors of the residential block. The air grew colder as they passed through the rear transept and stepped out into the courtyard, exiting the heavy doors into the crisp Saturday air. The sky was a pale, washed-out grey, and the wind bit at their exposed skin.\n\nOnce they were well away from the walls of the Cathedral, moving toward the market streets where the crowds provided a layer of acoustic cover, Cecilia stopped abruptly. She turned to Eliza, her voice a sharp, urgent hiss that cut through the ambient noise of the city.\n\n\"Are you crazy? What were you doing back there?\"\n\nThe tone was almost angry—a rare, jarring departure from Cecilia's usual softness—but the anger was a thin veil. Underneath it, the look in her eyes was one of raw, trembling concern. She looked like a sister who had just watched her sibling dance on the edge of a cliff.\n\nEliza felt a sudden, hot flicker of guilt. She looked at Cecilia—the girl who had just acted as her shield, who had risked her own standing to cover Eliza's transgression—and she felt a pang of shame. She couldn't tell her. She couldn't tell her about Verali, about the honeyed seed, or the raw, physical craving that was currently making her tremble.\n\nShe glanced away, her cheeks flushing a deep, betraying red.\n\n\"I'm... I'm sorry, Cecilia,\" she said, her voice soft, breathless, and returning to that cautious, deferential cadence. \"I just... I needed to know my schedule. It's... important.\"\n\nCecilia blinked, her annoyance shifting into a confused curiosity. She stepped closer, her fluffy white wool brushing against Eliza's sleeve. \"Your schedule? Why? We're acolytes, Eliza. We don't *choose* our schedules. We just follow them.\"\n\nEliza looked at her friend, her expression pained. The guilt was a weight in her throat, making it hard to swallow. She wanted to tell her. She wanted to tell Cecilia that she had found something—someone—who made the world feel alive, and that the thought of being trapped in the Cathedral without a way out was becoming an unbearable torture.\n\nBut the secret was too precious, and too dangerous, to share.\n\n\"I... I just do,\" Eliza whispered, her voice trailing off into a fragile, guilty sigh. \"I'm sorry, Cecilia... but it's not my place to say any more.\"",
