    "2026-05-13T03:02:15.889Z": {
      "msg_index": 99,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\nThe wind whipped through the market square, carrying the scent of ozone and burnt coal, but for Cecilia, the air suddenly felt freezing. The silence that followed Eliza's admission was louder than the roar of the crowds around them. *'Not my place to say any more.'*\n\nThe words echoed in Cecilia's mind, each syllable a small, sharp stone. A few days ago, those words would have sounded like the caution of a devout acolyte, perhaps a secret prayer or a private devotion. But now, in the wake of their shared rebellion, they sounded like a wall. A boundary.\n\n*Not her place to say?* Cecilia wondered, her brown eyes searching Eliza's face. *Does that mean... there are others? People she's... protecting?*\n\nThe thought bloomed in her mind like a dark flower. She began to imagine a world where Eliza had a secret life—friends in the slums, perhaps a hidden cell of Testifiers, or a love she had kept tucked away in the shadows of the Cathedral. The idea that Eliza had a whole architecture of secrets that Cecilia wasn't allowed to enter made the world feel tilted.\n\nAnd then, the realization hit her with the force of a physical blow.\n\n*Protecting them from me?*\n\nCecilia felt a stab of pain lance through her heart, a sharp, cold ache that made her breath hitch. She had spent years believing they were the same—two halves of a whole, two girls drifting through the same sterile hallways, sharing the same fears. She had just risked her own safety, her very soul, to cover for Eliza in the Almoner's office. She had stepped into the line of fire for her, and in return, she was being told that there were things Eliza couldn't—or wouldn't—share.\n\nThe betrayal wasn't about the act of lying; it was about the lack of trust. It was the realization that while she had opened her heart and her mind to Eliza, Eliza was still keeping a door locked.\n\nCecilia stopped walking. She turned fully toward Eliza, her shoulders slumping, her expression crumbling from confusion into a raw, fragile vulnerability. The bustle of the market faded into a blur of grey noise; there was only the two of them, standing in the middle of the street like two ghosts.\n\n\"Eliza?\" her voice was small, trembling, stripped of its usual steady composure. It sounded like a child's voice, fragile and thin.\n\nShe reached out, her hand hovering near Eliza's sleeve but not quite touching, as if she were afraid she might be pushed away. Her eyes were pleading, shimmering with a layer of unshed tears that reflected the dull grey of the sky.\n\n\"You... you know I wouldn't report you, right?\" she whispered, the words catching in her throat. \"Not after everything... not after what we've said. Not after I... I've seen the truth too.\"\n\nShe looked at Eliza with a desperation that was almost tactile, a silent plea for the wall to come down. She didn't want the secret—she just wanted the knowledge that they were still, truly, together.",
