    "2026-05-13T03:17:43.063Z": {
      "msg_index": 100,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\nThe sight of Cecilia’s crumbling expression was a blade to Eliza's heart. Seeing that raw, shimmering vulnerability in the sheep-girl's eyes—the look of someone who had finally let their guard down only to find a closed door—was worse than any threat from the Inquisition. Eliza had known this moment was coming; she had been dreading it since the first time she had felt the catalyst's heat in her veins. She had wanted to keep Cecilia in the light, safe and untainted, while she herself waded into the depths. But the cost of that protection was this: a wall of silence that felt like a betrayal.\n\n\"Oh, Cecilia. Please don't,\" Eliza whispered, her voice breaking.\n\nShe stepped forward, closing the distance between them, and reached out to seize Cecilia's hesitant hand. Her grip was firm, an anchor intended to pull Cecilia back from the edge of that cold, lonely feeling. She felt the softness of Cecilia's skin, the slight tremor in her fingers, and it made Eliza’s chest ache with a fierce, protective love.\n\nShe tried to inject a bit of lightness into the air, though her own heart was heavy. She forced a small, flickering smirk—a ghostly remnant of the mischief they had shared just hours before.\n\n\"It isn't that I don't trust you, Cecilia,\" Eliza murmured, her tone shifting back to that soft, breathless quality. \"You know enough now to send me to the dungeons ten times over. If you wanted me gone, you'd only have to whisper a few words to the Confessor.\"\n\nThe smirk faded as the gravity of their situation returned. Eliza’s eyes darted around the market, scanning the crowd for any familiar faces, any Wardens who might be listening. The danger wasn't just to her; it was to a network of people who were risking everything to dismantle the machine they both lived within.\n\n\"It's just... it's not just me that's at risk from the Inquisitors,\" she explained, her voice dropping to a barely audible level, a secret shared in the wind. \"There are others. People who... who have helped me. And I... I'm only trying to be careful. I couldn't live with myself if... if something I did... or said...\"\n\nShe bit her lower lip, her gaze falling to the cobblestones. She couldn't finish the sentence. She couldn't say *'if I accidentally exposed the sanctuary'* or *'if my recklessness led the Inquisition to Verali's door.'* The thought of the white-scaled Matron being captured, her divinity replaced by a cell and a chain, was a horror Eliza couldn't even articulate.\n\nShe looked back up at Cecilia, her blue eyes swimming with a mixture of longing and resolve. She realized that Cecilia’s spirit was now as fragile as a piece of spun glass; she couldn't leave her hanging in the void of uncertainty.\n\n\"Will you just... be patient with me, a little longer, Cecilia?\" Eliza pleaded, her voice trembling. \"I want to tell you everything. I *do*. I want us to have no more secrets. But please... let me get... permission... first.\"\n\nIt was a strange request—permission from a shadow, from a ghost in the slums—but it was the only way Eliza could bridge the gap. She squeezed Cecilia's hand one last time, a silent promise that the door would eventually open, if only she could ensure the path was safe.",
