      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:45pm)\n\n\nThe word *'permission'* hung in the air between them, a strange, heavy thing. For a girl raised in the Cathedral, permission was the only currency that mattered. You asked permission to speak, permission to eat, permission to exist. For Eliza to ask for permission from someone *outside* the Church was a conceptual earthquake. It implied that there was an authority—a law, a logic—that existed beyond the stone walls of the sanctuary.\n\nCecilia felt the tension in her chest ease, replaced by a slow, warming sense of relief. The wall hadn't disappeared, but Eliza had left a window open. She wasn't being excluded because she wasn't trusted; she was being excluded because the stakes were higher than a simple roommate's confidence.\n\nThe idea that Eliza was protecting others—people who had *helped* her—sparked a new kind of curiosity in Cecilia. It was a quiet, humming interest, a realization that the world was far larger and more complex than the three-pillar theology of the Temperantia.\n\n\"Permission,\" Cecilia repeated softly, her voice regaining its plain, unhurried quality. \"I... I understand. I can be patient.\"\n\nShe looked at their joined hands, the contrast of their skin and the shared grip of their fingers. For the first time, the habit she wore didn't feel like a uniform of submission; it felt like a disguise. They were two girls playing a part in a play, and the play was the only thing keeping them safe.\n\n\"Just... don't be too long,\" she added, a small, tentative smile returning to her face. \"I'm not very good at waiting when I'm thinking about things. My head starts to fill up with questions, and if I don't have answers, I start to... well, I start to imagine things. And I think I'm starting to like imagining things.\"\n\nShe looked up at the grey sky, the bruised purple of the evening beginning to creep back into the horizon. The fear was still there—the fear of Gaspard, the fear of the Inquisition—but it was now tempered by a sense of solidarity. She wasn't just a flock member anymore. She was a co-conspirator.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said, gently tugging Eliza's hand to lead her back toward the market stalls. \"We still have to buy the grains and the tallow. If we're late getting back, the Almoner will start wondering why we're taking so long, and I'd rather not give him any reason to look at the ledger again.\"\n\nAs they walked, Cecilia didn't let go of Eliza's hand. She held it tight, a small, private rebellion against the rules of modesty and temperance. In the middle of the crowded square, under the watchful eyes of a city built on shame, two acolytes walked hand-in-hand, their secrets tucked away like smuggled jewels.",
