    "2026-05-13T04:24:33.533Z": {
      "msg_index": 102,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:45pm)\n\nFrom the sanctuary of his stall, Eamon watched the ebb and flow of the market with the practiced eye of a man who made his living reading the spaces between people. His stall was a calculated mess—a curated chaos of brass fittings, coarse twine, and mismatched jars of salted preserves—designed to make him look like a man too preoccupied with the minutiae of commerce to be a threat. In reality, he was the most attentive man in the square.\n\nHe spotted them almost immediately.\n\nTwo figures in the heavy black and white habits of the Cathedral, moving through the crowd like two ghosts adrift in a sea of brown and grey. He recognized the posture first—the slight, nervous tension of the one, and the more grounded, sturdy gait of the other.\n\n*Eliza.*\n\nA small, knowing smile touched the corners of his mouth. He watched her from a distance, his dark amber eyes tracking her movements as she navigated the stalls. He saw the way she moved—there was a newness to her, a certain electric current running beneath the surface of her piety. She didn't just look like an acolyte anymore; she looked like a woman who had discovered a secret door and was currently imagining all the ways she might unlock it.\n\nBetween the intervals of serving a customer—selling a handful of tallow candles to a nervous laborer or a spool of thread to a seamstress—Eamon kept his gaze fixed on them. He expected the usual. He expected Eliza to eventually find a way to shake her companion, to slip into an alleyway or linger behind a pillar, carving out a moment of solitude to send a signal or a message.\n\nBut as they moved deeper into the market, Eamon's eyebrows rose, and he tilted his head slightly, his interest piqued.\n\nThey weren't drifting apart. In fact, they were doing the opposite.\n\nHe saw it then—a small, defiant point of contact. Their hands were locked together, fingers entwined, a tight grip that spoke of a bond far more intimate than the institutional sisterhood the Church preached.\n\n*How curious,* he thought, his tongue clicking against the roof of his mouth.\n\nHe didn't know Cecilia—not really—but he knew the type. The devout, the steady, the ones who followed the map without questioning the terrain. To see such a girl clinging to Eliza with such desperate intensity was a detail he hadn't anticipated. It wasn't just companionship; it was a lifeline.\n\nEamon leaned back against a crate of rusted nails, his ringed tail flicking once, thoughtfully, behind him. He didn't move to intercept them; he wasn't a man of sudden gestures. He preferred the slow burn.\n\nThe sight of them holding hands in the middle of the square, beneath the gaze of a thousand potential informers and the looming shadow of the Cathedral, was a piece of poetry he couldn't ignore. It was a leak. A beautiful, dangerous leak in the social fabric of the city.\n\n\"Well now,\" he murmured to himself, his voice a low, easy drawl that barely rose above the noise of the market. \"Looks like the flock is starting to realize the shepherd's crook is just a stick.\"\n\nHe watched them disappear toward the grain merchant, the sight of their joined hands lingering in his mind. He had a feeling the math of this little arrangement was going to get a lot more interesting.",
