    "2026-05-13T04:43:00.155Z": {
      "msg_index": 103,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\nThe market was a blur of sensory overload—the smell of roasting chestnuts fighting with the stench of open sewers, the cacophony of vendors shouting prices, and the rhythmic clatter of iron-rimmed wheels on cobblestones. They were nearly finished with the Almoner's list, their purses growing lighter as they checked off the tallow and the grains. For a while, the silence between them was comfortable, a shared secret held in the clasp of their hands.\n\nBut as they turned the corner toward the final stall, the atmosphere shifted. The noise of the main square dimmed, replaced by the damp, heavy silence of a narrow side-street.\n\nCecilia stopped. Her grip on Eliza's hand tightened, not out of fear, but out of a sudden, jarring realization.\n\nIn the deep shadow of a recessed alleyway, slumped against a wall of grimy, salt-stained brick, sat a man. He was a ghost of a person, wrapped in rags that had long ago lost their original color, now a uniform shade of city-grey. His head was hung low, chin resting on a bony chest, his breathing shallow and ragged. Beside him sat a battered tin cup, scarred with dents and containing nothing but a few copper scraps and a single, sodden cigarette butt.\n\n*An excommunicant,* the thought flashed through Cecilia's mind, accompanied by the echoes of a dozen different sermons.\n\nThe Church's instructions were absolute: *The casted-out are the mirrors of our own failures. To look upon them is to invite the contamination of their despair. Pass them as you would pass a stone in the road—without acknowledgement, without pity, for their state is the result of their own spiritual negligence.*\n\nFor twenty-one years, Cecilia had followed that instruction with the precision of a clock. She had walked past a hundred men like this, keeping her gaze fixed forward, her heart a locked vault of dutiful indifference.\n\nBut today, the vault was open. Today, she didn't see a 'spiritual failure.' She saw a man who was cold. She saw a man who was hungry. She saw a human being who had been erased from the world while he was still breathing.\n\n\"Eliza... wait...\" she said softly, her voice barely a whisper.\n\nCecilia looked down at the heavy leather purse in her hand. The coins inside were not hers; they were the property of the Cathedral, a trust given to her by the Almoner to be spent on specific, approved goods. To divert those funds was, by definition, theft from the Church. It was a breach of the most basic trust.\n\n*It isn't my money... the Almoner gave it to us for supplies...*\n\nShe felt the internal tug-of-war—the old, conditioned fear of the law versus the new, burgeoning warmth of her own conscience. She looked at the beggar again. He looked so small, so utterly defeated, that the thought of returning to the Cathedral with an extra few coins in the purse felt like a different kind of crime.\n\nBefore the fear could reclaim her, Cecilia reached into the purse. Her fingers brushed the cold metal of the coins. She withdrew a handful, weighing them in her palm, her mind quickly calculating the cost of the remaining grains. She banked on the fact that the Almoner, in his weary arrogance, wouldn't count every single copper if the quality of the tallow was sufficient.\n\nShe stepped away from Eliza, her movements shy and hesitant, as if she were stepping into a forbidden zone. She approached the man and, with a soft *clink*, dropped the coins into the tin can.\n\nThe sound was a thunderclap in the silence of the alley. The man started, his shoulders jerking as he craned his head up. His eyes were bloodshot and sunken, but as they focused on the black and white habit of the acolyte standing above him, his mouth went slack. The disbelief was total; he looked as if he had seen a statue come to life and offer him a piece of its heart.\n\nA flush of heat rose to Cecilia's cheeks, a deep, embarrassed crimson. The weight of his gaze was too much, too intense. She couldn't bear the gratitude—it felt too heavy, too real.\n\n\"I'm... I'm sorry. I hope this helps,\" she whispered, her voice trembling.\n\nShe didn't wait for a response. She turned quickly, almost running, back to Eliza's side. As she slid her hand back into Eliza's, she felt the other girl's grip tighten.\n\nEliza didn't say a word, but she was looking at Cecilia with an expression that wasn't just friendship. It was a look of affectionate, shimmering pride. It was the look of someone who had seen a seed break through concrete and was watching it reach for the sun.",
