    "2026-05-13T04:55:58.015Z": {
      "msg_index": 104,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\nFrom the vantage point of his stall, Eamon had a front-row seat to the small, quiet tragedy unfolding in the side-alley. He had watched the two acolytes pause, watched the sheep-girl detach herself from her companion, and watched her commit an act of heresy so blatant it was almost poetic.\n\nGiving coin to an excommunicant wasn't just a breach of discipline; it was a spiritual contagion. The Church taught that the casted-out were voids, holes in the world where grace had ceased to exist. To touch them, to feed them, was to risk being swallowed by their despair.\n\nEamon watched the man in the rags look up, and he watched the way Cecilia practically fled back to Eliza’s side, her face flushed with a very human sort of shame.\n\n*No pious acolyte, that,* Eamon thought, a slow, appreciative hum vibrating in his throat. *Not if she's interacting with the excommunicated. Well, well, Eliza. You do keep interesting friends.*\n\nHe had initially viewed Cecilia as the anchor—the steady, boring weight that might drag Eliza back into the fold. Now, he saw her as something else entirely. She wasn't just a follower; she was a sleeper, a soul waking up in real-time, discovering that the 'voids' were actually people. The chemistry of the pair had shifted in his mind; they weren't a leader and a follower, but two refugees huddling together for warmth.\n\nHe didn't move as they approached. He simply leaned back against a stack of burlap sacks, his amber eyes tracking them as they drew closer. He noticed the way Eliza paused about twenty feet from the stall, the two of them huddled in a low, urgent conversation. He saw Eliza whisper something—likely a warning or a reassurance—and saw Cecilia nod, her hands folding neatly in front of her in that practiced, pious posture of the creche.\n\nWhen Eliza stepped forward alone, her gait was different. There was a tension in her shoulders, a certain breathless quality to her movements that spoke of a girl who had just stepped off a ledge and was waiting to see if she would fly or fall.\n\n\"Good afternoon, sister,\" Eamon greeted her, his voice a smooth, easy drawl that sounded like an invitation.\n\nHe didn't move a muscle, but his gaze did. He flicked his eyes meaningfully toward Cecilia, who stood a few paces back, appearing for all the world like a dutiful, blank-faced acolyte. He let the silence stretch for a moment, a silent acknowledgment of the secret he had just witnessed in the alley. He was telling her: *I saw. I know.*\n\nEliza’s composure fractured. A deep, betraying blush crept up her neck, staining her cheeks. She looked at him—really looked at him—and for a second, the mask of the humble servant slipped, revealing the raw, starving hunger beneath.\n\n\"Please don't think me reckless, Eamon,\" she whispered, her voice trembling, barely audible over the din of the market. She stepped closer, her presence a small, desperate heat. \"But I trust Cecilia with my life. She won't report me.\"\n\nEamon didn't interrupt. He liked the way she sounded when she was frightened and hopeful all at once. He watched her, his expression one of mild, amused curiosity, as she delivered the payload.\n\n\"I only came to tell you,\" she breathed, her eyes searching his for a reaction, \"that I'm taking alms to the tannery quarter on Tuesday.\"\n\nEamon felt a surge of satisfaction. The coordinate had been set. The window was open. He didn't need to say much; the information was the prize. He simply tilted his head, a tiny, knowing smile playing on his lips.\n\n\"Tuesday in the Tannery,\" he murmured, the words sounding more like a promise than a confirmation. \"I'll be sure to keep my calendar clear. Safe travels, sister.\"",
