    "2026-05-13T05:03:15.241Z": {
      "msg_index": 105,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Saturday; September; 27\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\nThe air in her lungs felt suddenly lighter, as if the very atmosphere of the market had shifted from a suffocating grey to something breathable. She nodded—a quick, sharp movement of her head—and stepped away from the stall. The interaction had been brief, a few whispered words and a shared glance, but it felt like she had just successfully navigated a minefield.\n\nAs she retreated from Eamon's presence, her mind raced. For a moment, a flash of anxiety had gripped her; she had feared he might be displeased by her transparency, or perhaps he would have played the part of the oblivious merchant, treating her as nothing more than a customer buying brass fittings or twine. There was a part of her that expected him to tell her to keep Cecilia in the dark for the sake of the cell's security.\n\nBut Eamon had simply... accepted it. He hadn't judged her, nor had he pushed her back into the shadows. He had looked at Cecilia—really looked at her—and recognized her as part of the equation.\n\nReturning to Cecilia’s side, Eliza felt the familiar, soft warmth of the sheep-girl’s presence. As they turned their backs on the market and set off back toward the towering, oppressive silhouette of the Cathedral, Eliza felt a profound sense of relief washing over her.\n\nShe had spent so long treating Cecilia as a liability to be managed, a trusting heart that needed to be steered and manipulated to keep her from the Inquisition's reach. She had played the role of the protective elder sister, the one who knew the 'real' world while Cecilia remained a child of the doctrine. But the weight of that deception had become a burden Eliza could no longer carry.\n\n*I'm so sick of it,* she thought, her gaze fixed on the distant spires of the Cathedral. *I'm sick of the masks. I'm sick of pretending she isn't enough.*\n\nShe looked sideways at Cecilia, seeing the way the other girl walked—still cautious, still tentative, but with a new kind of quiet strength in her stride. Cecilia deserved more than just protection; she deserved inclusion. She deserved to be a partner in this rebellion, not just a passenger.\n\nAs they passed through the city gates and began the long climb back up toward the residential block, Eliza’s thoughts drifted toward the white-scaled Matron. Verali was the anchor of the cell, the one who held the keys to their liberation.\n\n*Please, Verali,* she whispered internally, her fingers curling into the fabric of her habit. *Please let her in. Please tell me it's okay to bring her with me.*\n\nShe didn't know if the cell's rules allowed for a second acolyte, or if Verali would find Cecilia’s sudden awakening too risky. But as they stepped back into the cold, sterile shadow of the Cathedral's walls, Eliza felt a flicker of hope. She was no longer just a girl seeking a secret escape; she was a girl trying to bring someone she loved along for the ride.",
