    "2026-05-13T21:52:17.270Z": {
      "msg_index": 107,
      "name": "Verali",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Sunday; September; 28\nTime: 7:30pm)\n\n\nThe air had turned sharp, a sudden, biting cold snap that tasted of iron and coming snow. It was the kind of weather that seeped through the bones, making the city feel smaller, more brittle. Verali walked with a steady, rhythmic grace, her heavy cloak wrapped tightly around her frame to conceal the breadth of her wings. Even in the poorer districts, the cold was a reminder of the city's precariousness—the soot from the crude-oil lamps clung to the damp air, creating a low-hanging haze that blurred the edges of the tenements.\n\nShe was on her way to a laying-out call—a somber, quiet task. In a city where the Church controlled the narrative of life and death, there were those who preferred their dead to be handled by someone who didn't report back to the Almoner's office. It was a living that required discretion, a quality Verali possessed in abundance.\n\nAs she navigated the winding streets, the skyline shifted. The massive, dark-stone silhouette of the Cathedral of Sanctae Temperantia loomed over the district like a silent sentinel. It was a fortress of faith, a mountain of cold masonry designed to make the individual feel insignificant.\n\nVerali slowed her pace. She stopped at the edge of the perimeter, the boundary where the cobblestones transitioned from the chaotic grit of the slums to the sterile, swept precision of the Church's grounds. She did not cross the line. To do so would be to invite the gaze of the Wardens; for a dragon-kin, stepping onto Cathedral soil without an invitation was an invitation to a cell or a leash.\n\nShe paused, her golden eyes tilting upward. Her gaze climbed the towering walls, past the heavy oak doors of the narthex, up to the fourth floor.\n\nThere, behind the narrow, arched windows of the acolyte dormitories, was a world of rules, silence, and repressed breath. Verali stared at the glass, the reflection of the grey sky shimmering on the surface. She wondered if, at this very moment, Eliza was standing there. She imagined her—the slender silhouette of a girl in a heavy black habit, perhaps leaning her forehead against the cold pane, looking out at the same grey horizon.\n\nThe knot of anticipation Verali had been carrying for a week—a tight, pulsing thrum in her chest—grew a fraction larger. It was a strange, aching sensation. She had spent the decades of her life navigating the world as a pariah, a thing to be feared or commodified, yet here she was, feeling a tender, human pull toward a girl who had only just begun to wake up.\n\n*So close,* Verali thought, her tongue flicking instinctively to taste the salt and soot of the wind. *Just a few hundred feet of stone and dogma.*\n\nIt felt like a cruel joke. They were separated by a distance that could be walked in a minute, yet they might as well have been separated by a thousand miles of wasteland. Between them lay the Inquisition, the Biological Deadline, and a hierarchy that viewed their connection as a contamination.\n\nVerali let out a slow, shimmering breath that misted in the freezing air. She didn't linger; longing was a dangerous luxury in a city governed by the Temperantia. With one last, lingering glance at the heights of the dormitory, she turned away, melting back into the haze of the industrial district, the ghost of Eliza's touch still humming beneath her scales.",
