      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "(Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30\nTime: 3:30pm)\n\n\nThe three days following her encounter with Eamon had been a masterclass in performance. To the world, she remained the ghost of the Cathedral—a silent, drifting shadow in black and white robes who moved through the corridors with her gaze cast downward. She had spent those hours refining the mask, leaning into the role of the dutiful acolyte with such precision that it felt like a second skin. But beneath the heavy wool of her habit, her skin felt electric. Every hour that passed was not merely time, but a countdown. She found herself staring at the clock in the refectory, her mind tracing the route to the Tannery quarter, her heartbeat accelerating whenever she caught Cecilia’s eye. The shared secret between the two girls had become a silent, glowing cord connecting them, a rebellion of two hearts beating in a synchronized rhythm of anticipation.\n\nTuesday arrived with a crisp, biting wind that rattled the stained-glass windows of the nave. Eliza moved through the morning rituals with a mechanical efficiency, her prayers a hollow recitation, her mind already miles away. When she finally descended to the ground floor, the air in the residential block felt thick and stale, smelling of old incense and floor wax.\n\nThe Almoner’s office was a cavern of ledger books and stacked crates, smelling of dry parchment and the metallic tang of ink. The Almoner, a man whose soul seemed as dried-out as his records, didn't look up as Eliza entered. He simply pointed a withered finger toward a heavy wooden crate containing the week's alms: coarse grain, salted fish, and a few vials of basic medicinal tinctures for the sick of the district.\n\n\"Sign the ledger, Acolyte,\" he droned, his voice like grinding gravel.\n\nEliza stepped forward, her movements slow and deliberate. As she pressed the quill to the paper, her hand trembled slightly. It wasn't fear—not anymore. It was a raw, humming excitement that threatened to spill over. She felt as though she were vibrating, a high-frequency energy that made the sterile air of the office feel suddenly too small, too restrictive.\n\nShe hoisted the crates with a strength born of adrenaline, her muscles straining against the fabric of her robes. Each step she took away from the Almoner’s desk felt like a step away from a grave. When she passed through the rear transept arms and stepped out into the open air, she didn't stop. She didn't look back. She walked with a purpose that would have been seen as an abnormality by any watching Warden, but she didn't care. The moment the heavy gates of the Cathedral grounds clicked shut behind her, the mask didn't slip—it evaporated.\n\nThe journey to the Tannery quarter was a descent into a different kind of world. As she left the polished stones of the center, the architecture began to decay, the buildings leaning inward as if whispering secrets to one another. The air grew heavy, the scent of ozone and coal smoke replaced by the pungent, cloying stench of tannins and curing hides—a sharp, acidic smell that stung the nostrils and clung to the throat.\n\nEliza walked through the narrow alleys, her boots clicking on wet cobblestones. She passed the \"gray\" people—the laborers with skin stained by chemicals, the hollow-eyed men leaning against soot-stained walls, the children with smudge-streaked faces who watched her habit with a mixture of curiosity and innate distrust. She was a splash of stark black and white against a backdrop of rust and grime.\n\nShe felt a strange sense of kinship with the filth. The Cathedral was a place of artificial purity, a place where the dirt was hidden beneath rugs and scrubbed from the skin. Here, the filth was honest. The stink of the tanning pits was the smell of work, of survival, of a life lived outside the scripted prayers of the Temperantia.\n\nAs she rounded a corner into the heart of the district, where the runoff from the vats pooled in iridescent, oily slicks on the road, a familiar silhouette materialized from the haze of a nearby alley.\n\n\"Right on time,\" a voice drawled, weaving through the noise of the hammers and the shouting of the foremen. \"I was starting to think you'd gotten lost, sister.\"\n\nEliza stopped, the crates settling heavily in her arms. Eamon stood there, leaning against a rusted iron railing, his amber eyes gleaming with a quiet, knowing amusement. He looked as though he had been waiting for her since the moment she left the gates.",
