    "2026-05-14T03:21:26.044Z": {
      "msg_index": 109,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n\"I... I am here,\" she breathed, the words coming out in a soft, shaky exhale. She looked up at Eamon, her light-blue eyes wide and searching. \"I have brought the offerings. Please... is she here? Is she waiting?\"\n\nEamon didn't answer with words. Instead, he stepped forward and reached for the crates. He didn't just take them; he handled them with a practiced, respectful efficiency, as if the grain and salt were sacred relics rather than meager handouts. He gave her a small, reassuring wink, his amber eyes reflecting the dim light of the Tannery.\n\n\"The alms will find their way to the hungry, sister,\" Eamon murmured, his voice a comforting drawl. \"And you'll find your way to the Matron. Follow me. Keep your head down and your steps light.\"\n\nHe turned on his heel, leading her away from the main thoroughfare and deeper into the labyrinth of the district. They slipped into a narrow alleyway where the walls were so close they nearly touched, blotting out the sky and leaving them in a world of charcoal shadows. Eliza followed him closely, the hem of her heavy black robe brushing against the damp stone. Every sound—the distant clang of a hammer, the shout of a laborer—felt amplified, a reminder that she was now far outside the protective, suffocating bubble of the Cathedral.\n\nThey stopped before a non-descript shop, its facade weathered and grey, blending seamlessly into the surrounding decay. Eamon didn't knock; he simply pushed open a heavy back door that groaned on rusted hinges.\n\nInside, the shop smelled of old oil and dust. Eamon guided her toward a narrow, spiraling staircase that clung to the interior wall. As Eliza climbed, the scent began to change. The acidic sting of the tannery faded, replaced by the rich, heavy aroma of finished leather and the sweet, dry scent of sawdust.\n\nWhen they reached the top, Eamon pushed open a final door, and Eliza stepped into the loft.\n\nThe room was vast and dim, lit only by a few flickering lamps and the pale light filtering through high, grime-streaked windows. The space was filled with towering bales of finished hides, stacked high like soft, leather mountains. The pelts created a landscape of natural nooks and crannies, a sanctuary of velvet and grain that felt luxuriously out of place in the heart of the slums. It was a place of tactile comfort, a hidden world where one could disappear into the folds of the leather and be forgotten by the world.\n\nEliza paused, her breath catching in her throat. Her eyes searched the gloom, scanning the shadows between the leather stacks. And then, she saw her.\n\nVerali was there, a vision of pristine, ivory white against the deep browns and tans of the loft. The Matron sat amidst the hides, her golden eyes glowing in the half-light, her presence an anchor of absolute stillness in the chaotic city.\n\nAn involuntary whimper escaped Eliza's lips—a small, broken sound of longing and recognition. It was the sound of a child finding a home after a lifetime of wandering.\n\nShe didn't walk toward Verali; she moved as if pulled by an invisible tide, her feet barely feeling the sawdust on the floor. She felt the mask of the acolyte—the poise, the deference, the carefully maintained piety—simply dissolve. There was no Father Gaspard here. There was no High Inquisitor. There was only the white-scaled goddess who had claimed her soul.\n\nBehind her, Eamon watched the reunion with a quiet, satisfied smile. He shook his head slightly, a silent acknowledgment of the raw intensity of the connection, before he turned and wandered off into the shadows of the loft, leaving the girl and the Matron to their own private world.",
