      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "<T/D>\n\n\n(Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30\nTime: 4:00pm)\n\n\nVerali felt the heat of her own fury pulsing in her veins, a golden, electric current that demanded action. Her mind was already racing, calculating risks, imagining the ways a predator could be dismantled, the ways a cell could be moved, the ways a girl could be vanished from the records of the Cathedral. She was prepared to fight a war for Eliza—to become the monster the Church already accused her of being if it meant keeping this girl safe.\n\nBut the reaction she expected—trembling, fear, a desperate plea for rescue—did not come.\n\nInstead, Eliza shifted. The tension in her shoulders vanished, replaced by a strange, quiet serenity. She looked up at Verali, and for the first time, the fear in her blue eyes had been replaced by something else: a shimmering, steel-edged resolve. She reached up, her small, soft hand grazing the ivory scales of Verali's cheek, her touch light and grounding.\n\nA sad smile touched her lips—not a smile of defeat, but one of acceptance.\n\n\"It's... it's alright, Verali,\" Eliza whispered, her voice steady now, devoid of the previous tremors. \"I've... made my peace with it.\"\n\nVerali blinked, her pupils narrowing. \"Made your peace? Eliza, he is a predator. He intends to—\"\n\n\"I used to be scared,\" Eliza interrupted softly, her hand lingering on Verali's skin. \"Terrified. I spent every night imagining the door opening, the sound of his boots in the hall. I thought the world ended at the edge of his reach.\"\n\nSuddenly, Eliza surged forward, wrapping her arms around Verali with a sudden, possessive intensity. She pulled the Matron down, dragging her deep into the leather hollow, her grip so tight and desperate that Verali actually worried for a second that she might smother the poor girl. The embrace was a frantic, clinging thing, a claim of ownership that mirrored the way Verali had held her moments before.\n\nEliza buried her face against the soft, heavy globes of Verali's breasts, her voice coming out muffled, filtered through the heat and scent of their joined bodies.\n\n\"But...\" she whispered, her breath warm against Verali's skin. \"Because of you, he won't be able to take what he wants. He can't hurt me like he thinks he can.\"\n\nVerali went still. The roar of her anger faded, replaced by a profound, echoing confusion. She felt the girl's heart beating against her own—fast, but rhythmic. Eliza was speaking of a psychological fortress, a sanctuary of the self that the Church's laws could not penetrate. She was talking about the 'Holiness of the Self'—the very core of the Testifiers' philosophy—but she had reached it not through a lesson, but through the visceral experience of being known and desired.\n\nTo Eliza, the violation was no longer a total theft because she had already given herself away to someone she loved. The 'first'—the most precious thing the Church tried to commodify—was gone. Gaspard would be taking a body, but he would be finding an empty house; the spirit had already moved out.\n\nEliza pulled back just enough to look Verali in the eye, her expression suddenly cautious, almost maternal in its concern.\n\n\"Don't do anything foolish, okay?\" she whispered, her voice regaining that soft, breathy quality. \"I can handle Gaspard.\"\n\nVerali stared at her, her gold eyes wide. She looked at this frail, sandy-haired girl—a girl who lived in a costume of piety, who trembled at the sound of a bell, who had spent her life in a state of managed submission. And yet, here she was, talking about 'handling' a predatory Examiner as if it were a minor chore, a smudge of dirt to be wiped away, a mundane Tuesday afternoon in the Tannery.\n\nVerali didn't know whether to be frightened for her or in awe of her. She felt a strange, dizzying sense of vertigo. She had come here to save a lamb from a wolf, only to find that the lamb had developed teeth.\n\n\"You... you can handle him,\" Verali repeated, the words feeling heavy and strange in her mouth. She let out a soft, huffing breath, a half-laugh of disbelief. \"Eliza, you are a tiny thing. He is a man of the cloth with the keys to the city.\"\n\nBut as she looked at the resolve in Eliza's eyes, Verali realized that the girl was no longer just a devotee. She was a co-conspirator. The mask was no longer a cage; it was armor.",
