      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 4:00pm]\n\n\n<T/D>\n\n\nThe warmth of the leather nest and the weight of Verali’s embrace had created a bubble of timelessness, a space where the Cathedral felt like a distant, fading memory. For a few stolen minutes, Eliza didn't feel like an acolyte, a vessel, or a target; she felt like a woman. She lay draped across the Matron's chest, listening to the rhythmic, low-frequency thrum of Verali's heart, her eyes half-closed in a state of absolute, shimmering contentment.\n\nBut the bubble burst with a single, sharp sound.\n\nFrom the far end of the loft, near the stairs, a throat was cleared. It was a polite sound, a measured signal of time regained.\n\nEliza stiffened, her head snapping toward the sound. Eamon stood a respectful distance away, leaning against a stack of hides with his arms crossed. He didn't look impatient—he was far too smooth for that—but his amber eyes held a quiet, knowing look. He was the clock, the reminder that the window of sanctuary was closing.\n\n\"Time's up, sister,\" Eamon called out, his voice a soft drawl that cut through the silence. \"The Almoner doesn't like his ledgers waiting, and the Wardens are starting their evening rounds.\"\n\nEliza felt a sudden, sharp pang of disappointment. It was a physical ache, a longing that made her want to curl back into Verali's scales and never leave. She didn't want to move. She didn't want to go back to the grey stones and the whispered prayers.\n\n\"I don't want to go,\" she whispered, her voice small and fragile.\n\nVerali’s response was a gentle, grounding pressure. The Matron kissed her temple, a lingering touch that felt like a promise. \"You must. The mask is your shield, Eliza. Wear it well, and it will lead you back to me.\"\n\nRegretfully, Eliza began the process of reassembly. She reached for her habit, the heavy black and white cloth that felt colder and coarser than it had hours ago. As she pulled the robes over her head, she felt the transformation taking place. Each fold of the fabric was a layer of the persona she had to inhabit. The habit didn't just cover her body; it muted her spirit, dampening the fire that Verali had ignited.\n\nShe stepped into her shoes, her movements slow and reluctant. But as she fastened the head-covering, concealing everything but her face, she noticed a change. The fear that usually accompanied the habit was gone. The anxiety that had once made her heart race in the presence of the priesthood had been replaced by a secret, humming power.\n\nShe turned back to Verali one last time, lunging forward to cling to the Matron for a few final, stolen moments. She pressed her face into Verali's neck, inhaling the scent of scales and old leather, trying to memorize the feeling of the Matron's heat so she could recall it when she was shivering in the dormitory.\n\n\"I'll see you soon,\" she whispered, her voice a desperate vow.\n\nAs she walked away from the loft and followed Eamon back down the spiraling stairs, Eliza felt an unexpected lightness. She was returning to the Cathedral, yes, but she was returning as a spy in her own life. The grey corridors, the sterile rooms, and the suffocating rules were no longer her world—they were simply the stage.\n\nHer time at the Cathedral was no longer her life; it was merely the space in-between. Every hour spent scrubbing the sacristy or reciting the litany was just a countdown. She was living for the moments of rebellion, for the gold of Verali's eyes, and for the knowledge that she was no longer alone in the dark.",
