    "2026-05-14T19:30:00.857Z": {
      "msg_index": 122,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe evening had been a blur of routine, but for Cecilia, the routine felt hollow. She had moved through her assigned duties—polishing the brass candlesticks in the nave and organizing the linens in the creche—with a mechanical precision, but her mind was miles away, wandering through the soot-choked alleys of the Tannery quarter.\n\nShe knew Eliza’s schedule. She knew the alms distribution was the official reason for the absence. But the way Eliza had spoken of it—the urgency, the subtle calculation in her eyes when she checked the Almoner’s ledger—had planted a seed of curiosity that wouldn't stop growing. Cecilia had spent the better part of the afternoon imagining what could possibly be out there that was worth the risk of a Warden's gaze. Was it a person? A place? A secret society? The imagination of a sheep-girl raised in a creche was limited, but the hunger for truth was an appetite that once woken, could not be sated.\n\nWhen Eliza finally returned, she didn't look like a girl who had spent her afternoon hauling crates of salted fish and grain through the acidic stench of the tanneries.\n\nCecilia had watched her from across the room during the evening meal, noting the way Eliza barely touched her porridge, her gaze distant and dreamy. There was a glow about her—a literal, shimmering radiance that seemed to emanate from her skin. It was the same expression Eliza had worn after that strange, emotional breakthrough a week prior, but amplified. She looked restored, as if she had stepped out of a storm and into a warm bath. The tension that usually lived in Eliza's shoulders—the constant, bracingness of a girl expecting a blow—had vanished.\n\nNow, the heavy oak door of their dormitory room clicked shut, sealing them away from the prying eyes of the senior acolytes. The room was dim, lit only by a single oil lamp on the shared nightstand, dancing shadows across their neatly made beds. The silence of the fourth floor settled around them, heavy and expectant.\n\nCecilia stood by her bed, slowly unbuttoning her habit, her movements unhurried. She looked at Eliza, who was humming a tune that didn't belong to any hymn in the Church's hymnal.\n\nCecilia hesitated. She knew the rules of their new, fragile pact: patience, discretion, and the understanding that some things are told only when the time is right. She didn't want to push, didn't want to seem greedy for secrets, but the air between them felt thick with an unspoken story. The curiosity became a physical itch, a pressure in her chest that she couldn't ignore.\n\nShe stopped her movements, biting her lower lip, her brown eyes wide and searching. She looked at Eliza—really looked at her—and saw the girl who was no longer just a ghost in the halls.\n\n\"So...\" Cecilia began, her voice soft, lacking any of the performative piety usually required of them. It was just the plain, honest voice of a friend. \"Did... did everything go alright today?\"",
