    "2026-05-14T19:47:20.774Z": {
      "msg_index": 123,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe room felt smaller than usual, the walls pressing in as if the Cathedral itself were eavesdropping on their intimacy. The air was heavy with the scent of old wool and the dying oil lamp, but between the two girls, there was a current of electricity. Eliza looked at Cecilia, and for a moment, she felt a wave of vertigo. This was the point of no return. Once these words were spoken, they could never be unsaid; they were a bridge burned, a threshold crossed.\n\nShe let out a breathless laugh—a sound that was less of a joke and more of a sigh of relief, as if she were finally setting down a heavy trunk she had been carrying for miles.\n\n\"Yes, Cecilia,\" she murmured, her voice floating in the dim light.\n\nShe began to wring her hands in front of her, a nervous, rhythmic motion that spoke of the anxiety still clinging to her. Her mind raced, weighing the danger against the desire. She looked at Cecilia—the soft, trusting expression on the sheep-girl's face, the genuine curiosity in those brown eyes—and the decision made itself. Her head gave a small, involuntary nod.\n\n\"Sit,\" she whispered, her voice barely a thread of sound. \"Sit, and I'll tell you.\"\n\nCecilia obeyed instantly, sitting primly on the edge of her bed, her back straight, her hands folded in her lap in that practiced, dutiful posture of the creche. Eliza sat down beside her, their shoulders brushing together. The physical contact felt like a seal of solidarity, a shared warmth against the cold masonry of the dorms.\n\nEliza spoke in a low, conspiratorial tone, the words carefully modulated so that no passing acolyte in the hall or neighbor in the next room could catch a single syllable. As she spoke, a creeping blush stained her cheeks, a heat that had nothing to do with shame and everything to do with the thrill of the forbidden.\n\nShe began from the beginning, the story coming out in halting, fragile bits, like a puzzle she was assembling in real-time. She spoke of the market, of the raccoon merchant Eamon, and the way he had spotted her—not as an acolyte, but as a person. She described the way he had cracked a door open for her, offering a sliver of light that didn't come from a candle-stand.\n\n\"He offered me something... something that felt like hope,\" Eliza whispered, her eyes distant. \"He spoke of an understanding I would never get inside these walls. He... he knew, Cecilia. He knew I was drowning in my own self-loathing.\"\n\nShe paused, her voice trembling. \"Because of the things I think. The things I feel. The things I was taught were sins—things that the Church says make me a monster or a failure.\"\n\nThen came the difficult part. The part that could get them both excommunicated, or worse, sent to the 're-education' cells. Eliza took a deep breath, her gaze locking onto Cecilia's.\n\n\"I've been seeing someone,\" she whispered. \"A dragon-kin. A Matron of a cell of Testifiers.\"\n\nThe word *Testifier* hung in the air, a spark of heresy in the sterile room. Eliza described Verali not as a 'biological hazard' or an 'abomination,' but with an aching, visceral awe. She spoke of the Matron's ivory scales, the golden light of her eyes, and the way her presence felt like a sanctuary. It was as if she were describing a glimpse of divinity—not the distant, silent Lady Idella, but a living, breathing goddess who actually listened.\n\n\"She told me I wasn't broken,\" Eliza whispered, her voice breaking. \"She told me that *we* weren't broken for the natural things we feel. The things we have no control over... and that were never meant to be controlled in the first place.\"\n\nThe blush deepened, her voice becoming a fragile, honest confession. \"I am... I am a member of one of those 'dragon cults' the High Inquisitor speaks so vehemently about. I don't care what he calls it.\"\n\nShe leaned closer to Cecilia, her shoulder pressing firmly into the other girl's. \"For the first time in my life, Cecilia... I feel whole. I feel loved. I feel wanted. And I feel accepted for exactly who I am, by people who want nothing from me... except that I be true to myself.\"",
