    "2026-05-14T20:06:51.260Z": {
      "msg_index": 124,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe silence that followed Eliza’s confession was not a void, but a suspension. Cecilia sat perfectly still, her hands still folded in her lap, but her grip had tightened until her knuckles were a stark, bloodless white. The word *Testifier* echoed in her mind, colliding with the sermons she had heard since she was a child. The Church had described them as the 'Contamination'—a spiritual disease, a rot that stripped a person of their duty and left them a hollow shell of desire.\n\nShe looked at Eliza—the girl she had shared a room with, the girl she had trusted with her own small rebellions—and saw someone who looked more alive than any person she had ever known. Eliza wasn't a hollow shell; she was a bonfire.\n\nFor a moment, the internal conflict played out on Cecilia's face. She was a creature of habit and structure; the idea of a 'cult' was a jagged, frightening thing. It felt like a cliff edge. But then she remembered the man in the alley—the excommunicant she had given coins to. She remembered the way he had looked at her, not with the eyes of a monster, but with the eyes of a human being who had been forgotten by the world.\n\nIf the Church was the truth, then that man was a void. But he hadn't felt like a void. He had felt like a person.\n\nCecilia let out a long, shaky breath, her shoulders finally slumping. The rigid posture of the acolyte crumbled, and she leaned her head onto Eliza’s shoulder, closing her eyes.\n\n\"I... I don't know how to be a heretic,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice plain and devoid of the formality she usually used. \"I don't know if I'm brave enough to be a 'Testifier.' I'm just a sheep, Eliza. I just want things to be... right.\"\n\nShe paused, a look of confusion crossing her face. \"But...if what you were looking for was...*love*,\" she blushed and fidgeted as she whispered the word, \"then...why didn't you just look for a husband? I know we...we don't have a lot of free time, but...the church would allow it.\"\n\nCecilia watched as Eliza's cheeks reddened fiercely and she glanced at the floor. \"Cecilia...\" she whispered, \"I'm not...\" she glanced back at her friend with a vulnerable expression, \"I'm not...*into* men, Cecilia.\"\n\nThe gears in Cecilia's head spun as she tried to process this. Finally it clicked and her eyes widened, \"Oh...Oh!\"\n\nShe blushed fiercely, \"You mean...this whole time...\" she trailed off, suddenly aware of how close they were. A sudden, forbidden thought flashed through her mind that made her cheeks catch fire, *I wonder if she...thinks I'm attractive?*\n\nShe shook her head and looked back at Eliza. \"I...I never knew,\" she said shyly. \"I...I suppose...it makes sense that...\"\n\nThen her expression changed to one of worry and distress, \"You mean that...you've been...been living with this all this time?\" The sermons, the teachings, the warnings and reprimands, the doctrine that had been drilled into them since they were children, and the whole time, Eliza had felt each of those warnings and lessons and sermons as a personal attack. A finger pointed at her.\n\nCecilia felt tears prick at her eyes. \"I'm sorry. I'm so sorry I didn't see it. I thought...I thought we were just...the same.\"",
