      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe tension in the room, which had been a thick, suffocating blanket of heresy and fear, suddenly shifted. Seeing Cecilia’s wide-eyed curiosity—the way she was trying to reconcile the \"scandal\" of the loft with the reality of her friendship—made Eliza feel a sudden, bubbling lightness in her chest. The shock was there, yes, but it was intertwined with a genuine, innocent wonder that made the whole situation feel almost playful.\n\nEliza giggled, a soft, melodic sound that felt entirely too loud for the sterile silence of the dormitory. It was a sound of liberation, a small, secret victory over the gravity of the Church.\n\n\"No, it's... I don't think it's like what you're imagining,\" she said, her voice trailing off into another little laugh. She leaned closer to Cecilia, her eyes sparkling with a mischief that would have horrified a Confessor. \"It's not a... a carnival of sin, Cecilia. It's not chaotic. Actually, it's very quiet. Very intimate.\"\n\nShe paused, her gaze drifting toward the ceiling, though she wasn't seeing the plaster. She was seeing the dim, golden light of the loft, the scent of curing leather, and the way the shadows danced across Verali's ivory scales. Her voice dropped an octave, becoming a breathless, distant murmur.\n\n\"There are some... noises, sure,\" Eliza admitted, a deep, crimson flush returning to her cheeks as she remembered the guttural growls and the rhythmic, wet sounds of their union. \"But...\"\n\nHer eyes took on a far-off quality, a glazed look of longing that made her seem as if she were halfway between two worlds. She was no longer in the fourth-floor dormitory; she was back in the sanctuary of the leather bales, feeling the heat of a dragon-kin's heart beating against her own.\n\n\"When I'm with Verali... it's like... like the whole world just melts away,\" she whispered, her expression one of absolute, shimmering peace. \"The Cathedral, the rules, the fear... they just stop existing. I think... I think it must be like that for them, too. It's not about the act, Cecilia. It's about the... the stillness. The knowing that you are finally, truly seen.\"",
