    "2026-05-14T20:41:13.946Z": {
      "msg_index": 130,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe way Eliza spoke of it—the \"stillness,\" the \"melting away\" of the world—made the whole thing sound almost romantic. It was a far cry from the visceral, frightening images Cecilia had conjured up just moments ago. The idea of the \"stillness\" resonated with her; after all, the only stillness she had ever known was the oppressive, forced silence of the Cathedral, a quiet born of fear and discipline. To imagine a stillness born of peace and acceptance was... well, it was an intoxicating thought.\n\nAnd yet, the core of the act still felt like a leap into a void. The thought of baring oneself, truly and completely, in front of others... it was crazy, wasn't it? It was an act of vulnerability that felt almost obscene in a world where a single misplaced glance could lead to an Inquisition. The idea of stepping out of her habit and standing naked before a room of strangers was still a barrier that felt too high to climb. She wasn't sure she was ready for that—maybe she never would be.\n\nBut then, Cecilia looked at Eliza.\n\nShe looked at the way her friend’s eyes seemed to hold a light that hadn't been there before, the way she carried herself with a newfound strength that bordered on the divine. Since the raccoon merchant had entered their lives, Eliza had undergone a transformation that was impossible to ignore. She wasn't a ghost anymore; she was a flame.\n\nIf this was \"corruption,\" Cecilia thought, then the corruption looked a lot like honesty. If this was \"contamination,\" then it looked like the only thing that had ever made Eliza truly happy. It was hard to label it as \"wrong\" when the result was a girl who finally seemed to love herself. Eliza wasn't evil. She wasn't a monster. She was, to Cecilia's eyes, the most vibrant, honest person in the entire Cathedral—perhaps the only one.\n\nBut as the conversation drifted, a small, cold knot tightened in Cecilia's chest. It wasn't the fear of the Church, nor was it the shock of the heresy. It was something simpler, something more human.\n\nFor years, she and Eliza had been each other's entire world. They were the only two people who truly understood the suffocating weight of the creche and the sterile boredom of the dorms. They were a pair, a unit, two souls drifting together through a grey sea. Now, there was this *other* person. A Matron. A dragon-kin. Someone who could give Eliza a kind of love and validation that Cecilia, in all her sheepish devotion, didn't know how to provide.\n\nIt wasn't the jealousy of a lover—Cecilia didn't look at Eliza that way—but it was the jealousy of a best friend. She felt a sudden, sharp pang of loss, a fear that she was being replaced by someone who could offer Eliza the world, while she could only offer a shared room and a small piece of bread from supper.\n\nShe shifted slightly on the bed, her voice taking on a fragile, tentative quality.\n\n\"I... I think I'm still too scared to... you know... *do* those things,\" Cecilia admitted, her gaze dropping to her lap. \"I don't think I'm ready to be... exposed. Not yet.\"\n\nShe looked up, her brown eyes searching Eliza's face, the jealousy flickering behind a mask of curiosity. \"But I still want to meet her. Not because I want to join... but because I want to see who this person is. I want to know who has... who has made you so happy.\"",
