      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe admission—the admission that she wasn't ready, that she was still clinging to the safety of her habits and her modesty—didn't make Eliza see Cecilia as weak. Instead, it made her see Cecilia as honest. For years, they had both performed the part of the perfect acolyte, but while Cecilia was simply following the rules, Eliza had been pretending to follow them. To hear Cecilia admit her fear was, in Eliza's eyes, a form of courage.\n\nShe looked at her friend with a depth of affection that transcended their shared history. She saw the flicker of uncertainty in Cecilia's brown eyes, the way she clung to the familiar safety of her bed, and she felt a wave of tenderness. *Cecilia... you are so much braver than I ever gave you credit for,* she thought. *You are facing the void with me, even if you're doing it with trembling hands.*\n\n\"I'll see what I can do,\" Eliza promised, her voice soft and grounding. She reached out, squeezing Cecilia's hand with a firm, reassuring pressure. \"I'll talk to her. I'll tell her that you're... you're not quite ready for the 'public' part of it. Verali is patient. She's the most patient soul I've ever known. She won't push you.\"\n\nThe air in the room seemed to soften, the shadows of the Cathedral feeling less like a cage and more like a backdrop to their own private revolution.\n\n\"Truthfully...\" Eliza added, her voice dropping to a whisper that felt like a sacred confidence, \"I want you to meet her too. I don't want to have to split my life in two anymore. I want my world to be one world, with you and her in it.\"\n\nAs the words left her mouth, a sudden, electric realization surged through her. The word *love* had been floating in the periphery of her mind for weeks, a vague, shimmering feeling she had been too afraid to name. But here, in the quiet of their shared room, with the person who knew her best in the world, the word finally felt right. It felt honest.\n\nEliza felt a sudden, hot prickle of warmth bloom in her chest, radiating outward. A deep, vivid blush crept across her neck and tinged her cheeks a bright, shimmering pink. She glanced away, her gaze darting toward the oil lamp, her heart hammering a frantic rhythm against her ribs. This wasn't the nervous tremor of a girl fearing the Inquisition; it was the dizzying, terrifying flutter of someone who had finally found their anchor.\n\n\"I... I think I love her...\" she whispered, the words barely audible, a fragile, breathless confession that felt more daring than any act of heresy.\n\nShe didn't look back at Cecilia immediately, her face still burning, but a small, private smile crept across her lips. For the first time in her life, the word *love* didn't feel like a forbidden desire or a biological deadline. It felt like the only truth that mattered.",
