    "2026-05-14T21:24:57.144Z": {
      "msg_index": 132,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; September; 30 |\n// Time: 8:30pm]\n\n\nThe word *love* hung in the air, shimmering and strange, like a piece of jewelry from a world Cecilia had never visited. It was a small word, only four letters, but it felt like a landslide. It shifted the very ground beneath her feet.\n\nUntil this moment, Cecilia had always envisioned her future as a series of checkboxes. A good husband. A devout home. A household of children who were proper, polite, and obedient—little echoes of the order and discipline she had been taught to crave. She had seen marriage as a transaction of stability, a necessary step in the cycle of the Gift, a duty to be performed with a quiet, humble heart.\n\nBut as she looked at the radiant, blushing face of her friend, a sudden, sharp pang of something unfamiliar pierced through her. It wasn't jealousy, exactly; it was an awakening. *Love?* she wondered.\n\nIf Eliza could find a love that made the world melt away, why shouldn't she? Why should she be content with a 'good' husband if there was such a thing as a love that made you feel whole? For the first time in her life, Cecilia felt a flicker of resentment toward the script she had been given. She felt cheated. She had been told that duty was the highest form of virtue, but seeing Eliza’s expression, she realized that duty was merely the absence of desire.\n\nThe thought brought a sudden, cold clarity to her situation. She looked around the dim room, at the sparse furniture and the grey walls. She didn't have anyone. She didn't have a 'vibrant' love, nor did she have a prospect. The only people she knew were the ghosts of the Cathedral—other acolytes who were just as frightened as she was, and priests who saw her as a tool of the institution. She couldn't even name a handful of the regulars who sat in the pews every Sunday.\n\nThen, the thought surfaced, unbidden and freezing: *The lottery... my... my birthday...*\n\nA chill ran down her spine, a physical shudder that made her pull her knees up to her chest. For years, the Biological Deadline had been a distant, abstract rule—a religious requirement she would eventually fulfill. But now, hearing Eliza speak of love, the Deadline no longer felt like a sacred duty. It felt like a threat.\n\nThe thought of the lottery—of being 'assigned' to a man she didn't know, a stranger who might be old, cruel, or indifferent—suddenly became a nightmare. She imagined a man like Father Gaspard, his wheezing breath wafting over her face, the smell of stale wine and old sweat filling her nostrils as he grunted over her in a room that smelled of incense and fear. The idea made her stomach churn with a sudden, violent nausea. The thought of a stranger claiming her body before she had ever found someone who made her feel the way Verali made Eliza feel was an intolerable void.\n\nAs the oil lamp flickered and died, plunging the room into a heavy, charcoal darkness, the girls drifted to their own beds. The springs of the mattresses groaned under their weight, a familiar, domestic sound that usually brought comfort, but tonight it felt like the ticking of a clock.\n\nCecilia lay on her back, staring up into the blackness of the ceiling. She closed her eyes and attempted to return to the same ritual she had performed every night since she was a child. She began to pray to Lady Idella.\n\nBut the prayer was different tonight. It wasn't a scripted petition for temperance or a plea for a humble heart. It was a desperate, whispered plea for a miracle.\n\n*Please...* she prayed, her voice a tiny, fragile thread in the dark. *Please let something happen. Let me meet someone... someone kind. Someone who sees me. Or let the rules change. Just... please, don't let it be someone like Gaspard.*\n\nShe didn't know what she was asking for, exactly—only that she could no longer stomach the thought of being a vessel for someone else's desire. She wanted to be a person. She wanted to be found.",
