    "2026-05-14T23:36:25.958Z": {
      "msg_index": 133,
      "name": "Narrator",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe week following Eliza's confession was a blur of strategic silence and heightened tension. For seven days, the two girls moved through the Cathedral like ghosts, their shared secret a heavy, invisible cord binding them together. Every glance from Father Gaspard felt like a probe; every summons to the sacristy felt like a trap. They spoke in whispers and coded looks, the air between them thick with the knowledge that they were no longer just roommates, but co-conspirators in a heresy that could cost them everything.\n\nCecilia spent those days in a state of quiet, vibrating anxiety. The scripts of her faith, once a comforting blanket, now felt like a shroud. Every time she looked at the altar, she didn't see the serenity of Lady Idella; she saw the looming shadow of the Biological Deadline. The thought of the lottery, once a distant formality, had become a predatory countdown. She found herself watching the other acolytes, wondering which of them were performing a mask as meticulously as Eliza, and which were truly as hollow as the doctrine demanded.\n\nEliza, meanwhile, navigated the week with a cool, focused intensity. The fear that had once defined her had been replaced by a shimmering, defiant purpose. She spent her hours of service counting down the seconds until she could shed her habit once more and return to the arms of the Matron. Her performance of piety became a polished art form—a weaponized version of the \"perfect servant\"—used to keep the hierarchy at a distance while she coordinated her underground life with Eamon.\n\nThen came Founding Day.\n\nIt is the one day of the year when the city of Temperantia breathes a ragged, uneven sigh of relief. Officially, it is a celebration of the city's survival and the wisdom of the council. In reality, it is a rare, precarious loosening of the social reins. The mood in the streets is a strange cocktail of forced cheer and desperate indulgence. Vendors set up stalls of sugared nuts and cheap trinkets, lanterns are strung across the narrow alleys to ward off the encroaching winter chill, and the Church grants a small window of \"mercy\" by allowing the acolytes a few hours of free time to wander the city.\n\nThe air is biting—a sudden, sharp cold snap that has dropped the temperature to 29°F, turning breath into white plumes of steam. The city is dressed in its holiday finery, but the grit of the industrial baseline remains: the smell of crude oil burning in the street lamps, the soot clinging to the stone walls, and the underlying current of suspicion that never truly vanishes.\n\nEliza and Cecilia stepped out from the Cathedral gates together, their heavy black and white robes wrapped tight against the wind. For a few hours, they were not just servants of the Temperantia; they were two young women stepping into a world that felt larger and more dangerous than it ever had before.",
