    "2026-05-15T05:59:51.585Z": {
      "msg_index": 135,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe image on the wall was not just a drawing; it was a scream of warning. Cecilia stared at the ink-etched monstrosity, her brown eyes scanning the jagged lines of the fangs and the manic, white-rimmed eyes of the creature. The artist had captured a specific kind of predatory madness, a depiction of a beast that didn't just eat, but *destroyed*.\n\nFor a moment, the warmth of Eliza's descriptions—the talk of stillness and melting—felt dangerously naive. The mental image she had been constructing of Verali was a fragile thing, built on the testimonies of a girl in the throes of a new love. Now, looking at the poster, Cecilia felt a sudden, instinctive tremor. She looked at the talons, then looked at Eliza, and took a half-step back, her woolly ears twitching. A small, quiet part of her—the part that had spent twenty-one years being told that the Church was the only source of truth—whispered that perhaps Eliza was simply blinded by a spell, a glamour cast by a monster.\n\nBut then she looked at Eliza's face.\n\nEliza wasn't frightened. She wasn't scanning the street for danger. Her jaw was clenched tight, her mouth set in a firm, hard line that Cecilia had rarely seen. There was a simmering fury behind her light-blue eyes, a spark of genuine hatred directed not at the monster on the wall, but at the hand that had printed it.\n\nThe silence between them stretched, heavy and cold. Eliza stared at the poster for a few more seconds, her chest rising and falling in a sharp, rhythmic cadence. Then, as if a balloon had suddenly lost its air, she let out a long, shaking sigh. The tension drained from her shoulders, leaving her looking smaller, more tired.\n\nShe turned away from the wall, facing back toward the main thoroughfare where the festive crowds continued to churn.\n\n\"Let's go...\" Eliza said. Her voice was small, sounding defeated in a way that made Cecilia's heart ache.\n\nCecilia followed her, but the image of the monster remained burned into her retinas. As they walked, the contrast became a battle in her mind. She had only ever seen dragon-kin from a distance—shuffling figures in heavy cloaks, heads bowed, avoiding eye contact. They had always seemed like shadows, peripheral and diminished. The Church taught that they were dangerous, but it also taught that they were pathetic.\n\n*Lies,* she thought. *These are lies.*\n\nThe realization felt like a heavy stone settling in her stomach. The Church wasn't just protecting the people; it was poisoning their perception. It was taking a living, breathing being and turning it into a caricature of a nightmare to keep the population in a state of perpetual, low-grade fear.\n\n*Remember, Cecilia... be gracious,* she told herself, her inner voice echoing the softness she had always cultivated. *They cannot help how they were born. They are just... people.*\n\nShe glanced at the side of Eliza's head, noting the way her friend's sandy blonde hair caught the dim winter light. The Church was being unfair—cruelly so. The thought made Cecilia's ears droop, a physical manifestation of her sudden, profound sadness. She felt a wave of pity, not for herself, but for the unseen person who had to live under the weight of those posters, knowing that the world saw them as a monster before they ever spoke a word.",
