    "2026-05-15T06:09:15.811Z": {
      "msg_index": 136,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe anger from the posters still simmered beneath her skin, a low-voltage hum that made her fingers twitch inside the heavy folds of her sleeves. She didn't want to talk about the imagery—to acknowledge the monsters on the wall was to give them power. Instead, she focused on the rhythm of her boots against the cobblestones, steering Cecilia through the throng of holiday revelers.\n\nAs they moved deeper into the market, Eliza’s mind began to shift toward the practical. Her internal clock was already ticking toward Friday. She had seen the ledger; her next alms delivery was scheduled for the 10th, destined for the Rookery of the Old Quarter. It was a grim part of the city, a labyrinth of leaning tenements and open sewers where the air always tasted of damp ash, but it was also the perfect cover. It was the window she needed to see Verali again, which was becoming a steady ache in the center of her chest.\n\nShe glanced at her friend. Cecilia looked small, her white wool stark against the grey backdrop of the city, her expression still haunted by the ink-and-paper nightmare they had left behind. Eliza felt a protective surge of affection. She knew Cecilia was terrified—not of the dragon-kin, but of the *idea* of them. The Church had spent twenty-one years building a wall of fear around the \"monstrosities,\" and Eliza realized that simply telling Cecilia that Verali was kind wasn't enough. Cecilia needed to see. She needed a visual truth to overwrite the lie.\n\n*But not at a meeting,* Eliza thought, her brow furrowing. *Not in the loft, not with the Seed, not with the nudity and the intensity of the other Testifiers. That would be too much. It would be a shock that might break her instead of wake her.*\n\nShe needed something mundane. Something human. She needed Cecilia to see a dragon-kin simply *existing*—working, breathing, moving through the world without the performative terror of the propaganda.\n\n\"Cecilia,\" Eliza whispered, her voice regaining that breathless, ecclesiastical softness, though there was a hidden edge of determination to it. \"I... I want to help you. I know what those posters do to the mind. They make the world feel like a place of predators and prey. But it isn't. Not really.\"\n\nShe paused, her eyes searching for a familiar silhouette among the stalls.\n\n\"I want you to meet her,\" Eliza continued, her heart beginning to race. \"But not in the way I did. Not in the secret places. I think... I think if you could just see her—really see her—as a person, the fear would go away. I want to see if there is a way for you to meet her where it feels safe. Where she is just... Verali.\"\n\nShe led Cecilia toward a specific cluster of stalls where the scent of old parchment and ozone lingered. They were heading toward Eamon. The raccoon merchant was the only one with the tradecraft to make such an introduction possible without alerting the Wardens or overwhelming Cecilia's fragile nerves.\n\n\"I'm going to ask Eamon,\" Eliza murmured, her voice dropping to a barely audible thread. \"I'm going to see if he can show us where she works. Just for a moment. Just so you can see that the monsters on the walls... they don't actually exist.\"",
