    "2026-05-15T06:25:29.612Z": {
      "msg_index": 137,
      "name": "Eamon",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe market was a cacophony of sound—the shrill cries of fishmongers, the rhythmic clatter of iron wheels on cobbles, and the distant, haunting chime of a cathedral bell. In the center of it all, Eamon stood behind his stall, a sprawling, curated chaos of brass fittings, tallow candles, and salvaged trinkets. He was currently rearranging a stack of rusted gears, though he wasn't really looking at them. He was watching the crowd. He was always watching the crowd.\n\nWhen he spotted the two acolytes approaching, a small, knowing smile graced his lips. He recognized the gait—Eliza’s stride was different now, less of a ghost and more of a person, while the sheep girl, Cecilia, seemed to be vibrating with a mixture of curiosity and sheer, unadulterated terror.\n\n\"Good day, sisters,\" Eamon said pleasantly, his voice a smooth, low drawl that seemed to settle the air around them. He didn't look up immediately, continuing to fiddle with a gear, making his presence feel incidental rather than expectant. \"Fancy seeing you out and about on Founding Day. I hope the Cathedral hasn't worked you too hard for the festivities.\"\n\nEliza greeted him with a warmth that was almost tactile, her voice light and hurried. Cecilia, meanwhile, offered a shy, hesitant smile, her gaze darting around the market as if expecting a Warden to materialize from the steam of a nearby soup stall. She looked nervous, her shoulders hunched, as if she were standing on a thin sheet of ice that might crack at any moment.\n\nThe girls stepped closer, huddling into the shadow of the stall's canopy.\n\n\"Eamon,\" Eliza began, her voice dropping to a hushed, urgent whisper. \"Verali said it was... okay to let Cecilia in. I want Cecilia to meet her, but not...\"\n\nA vivid, shimmering blush crept up Eliza's neck, staining her cheeks. She looked away, her voice trailing off into a fragile, breathless confession. \"Not... *that* way. Is that... possible?\"\n\nEamon paused. He stopped moving the gears and tilted his head slightly, his amber eyes shifting from Eliza to Cecilia. He didn't answer immediately. He let the silence stretch, a calculated beat of hesitation designed to maintain the facade of a wise man weighing a complex problem—a performance he greatly enjoyed. It gave the girls a moment to feel the gravity of the request, to realize that this wasn't just a stroll; it was an operation.\n\nFinally, he gave a slow, deliberate nod.\n\n\"I think we can do that,\" he murmured, his tone conversational yet cautious. \"But we should be careful. The crowds are a bit thin today, and the Inquisition's eyes are wider than usual during the holidays.\" He glanced at Cecilia again, noting the way her hands had come up in front of her chest, clutching the fabric of her habit. She looked as though she were preparing for a spiritual interrogation.\n\n\"Give me a few minutes to settle a few things,\" Eamon instructed, his voice returning to that easy, sidelong drawl. \"Then follow me at a distance. Don't walk *with* me—walk *behind* me. Pretend you two are just out enjoying the day, drifting where the wind takes you, and not heading anywhere in particular. Alright?\"\n\nEliza nodded, her expression suddenly serious, the weight of her protective instinct for Cecilia taking over. Cecilia's eyes widened, a flicker of alarm crossing her face, her breath hitching.\n\nEamon let out a soft, amused chuckle and shooed them away with a flick of his wrist, a gesture of lighthearted dismissal to cover the secret nature of the arrangement.\n\n\"Off you go,\" he added, a playful glint in his eyes. \"You'll have to ask the Almoner to buy a few more things from my shop to make up for the business you're losing me by stealing my time. Consider it a holiday tax.\"",
