    "2026-05-15T06:38:54.293Z": {
      "msg_index": 138,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe world felt tilted, a strange, dizzying blur of motion and muffled noise. They drifted away from the stall, moving through the crowd like two drops of ink in a river. They played the part—stopping briefly at a vendor selling hand-carved wooden trinkets, pretending to admire a small, clumsily made bird, though Cecilia could barely focus on the object. Her mind was a humming wire of anticipation and dread.\n\n*Am I a criminal?* she wondered. *Is this what it feels like to be a heretic?*\n\nThe air was still biting, the cold nipping at her nose, but she felt a strange, internal heat. It was the thrill of the forbidden. For twenty-one years, her life had been a straight line, a narrow path paved with expectations and prayers. Now, for the first time, she was stepping off the path into the tall grass.\n\nAbout ten minutes passed—ten minutes of careful, calculated wandering. They circled a fountain that had long since stopped flowing, its basin filled with frozen slush and discarded leaflets. Suddenly, Eliza’s hand reached out, a gentle but firm tug on Cecilia's sleeve that brought her back to the present. Eliza didn't say a word; she simply tilted her head, gesturing subtly toward the market square.\n\nCecilia looked. There was Eamon.\n\nThe raccoon merchant was moving with a purposeful, unhurried grace, closing up his stall. He draped a small, hand-written sign—something about being closed for the afternoon—across the front of his display. He pulled his cap low over his brow, shoved his hands deep into his pockets, and began to walk away, blending seamlessly into the flow of the crowd. He looked like any other citizen of the industrial district, an unremarkable man in an unremarkable coat.\n\nThey began to follow.\n\nA sudden, unexpected surge of excitement bubbled up in Cecilia's chest. It was a feeling she had only ever read about in the banned stories the creche minders used to confiscate. She felt as if she had been drafted into a spy novel, a secret agent in a heavy black robe. *Do they have passwords?* she wondered, her eyes darting left and right. *Do they have secret handshakes? Or maybe a code of blinking?*\n\nShe looked around at the faces passing them—the tired laborers, the smug merchants, the stern Wardens. She began to imagine a hidden army of Testifiers, a network of invisible eyes and ears hidden in every alleyway, watching over them, guiding them toward the light.\n\nAs they walked, her imagination began to run wild, painting a picture of where Eamon was leading them. She pictured a dingy, subterranean basement, lit by a single flickering bulb, filled with revolutionaries wearing red armbands and clutching illegal pamphlets. She imagined walls plastered with blasphemous slogans and diagrams of a world without the Temperantia, a place of loud arguments and forbidden laughter.\n\n*A secret society,* she thought, her heart hammering against her ribs. *A real, living rebellion.*\n\nShe was so lost in the cinematic grandeur of her own mental movie that she didn't notice the change in the terrain or the slowing of Eliza's pace. She walked right into Eliza's back with a soft *thump*.\n\n\"Oof,\" Cecilia murmured, blinking as she snapped back to reality.\n\nShe looked up. The grand, revolutionary basement was nowhere to be found. Instead, they were standing in a damp, grey alleyway, the air smelling of lye, wet stone, and old steam. Before them stood a utilitarian building with a small, steaming window and a heavy wooden door.\n\nThe city's washhouse.\n\nEamon was there, leaning against the doorframe with a small, knowing smile. He held the door open for them, the warmth of the interior spilling out into the cold air, smelling of soap and hard labor.\n\n\"Mind the floor; it's a bit slippery.\" Eamon said, his voice a playful, low drawl.",
