    "2026-05-15T06:49:52.641Z": {
      "msg_index": 139,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe transition from the biting cold of the streets to the interior of the washhouse was like stepping into a different world. The air was thick, heavy with a humid, clinging warmth that smelled of caustic lye and wet wool. It was a sensory assault, a blanket of steam that blurred the edges of the room, making the high ceilings and timber supports look like something from a dream.\n\nEliza stepped inside, her boots clicking softly on the damp stone. She had never been here—this was Verali's mundane world, the place where she existed not as a Matron, but as a laborer. Eliza looked around with a wide-eyed curiosity, taking in the industrial clutter. There were massive, bubbling vats of boiling water and lumbering scrubbing machines, some of which groaned with the effort of their rotation, while others sat half-disassembled, their iron guts spilled across the floor for repairs or parts. It was a place of raw utility, devoid of the sterile, polished grace of the Cathedral.\n\nThen, she saw her.\n\nIn the far back, silhouetted against the shimmering steam, was a figure of immense, quiet power. Verali was leaning over a deep tub, her powerful arms submerged to the elbows in a slurry of soap and grey linen. She was scrubbing with a rhythmic, focused intensity, her shoulders broad and her posture disciplined.\n\nThe moment Eliza locked eyes with her, the noise of the washhouse—the sloshing of water, the hiss of the boilers, the distant shouting of workers—simply faded away. It was as if the world had been muted, leaving only the thumping of Eliza's heart, a drumbeat of recognition and longing that drowned out everything else. Her expression, usually so carefully guarded and demure, melted into a look of pure, unfiltered love.\n\nShe began to walk forward, her pace quickening, her habit swirling around her ankles.\n\nEliza stopped a few feet away, her hands coming together demurely in front of her in a practiced, acolyte-like gesture, though her eyes were burning. She waited, breathless, until Verali sensed her presence and looked up.\n\nThe surprise on Verali's face was instantaneous, her brow furrowing as she processed the sight of the black-robed girl standing in the middle of a laundry. But as she recognized Eliza, that surprise shifted into a wary, glowing pleasure.\n\n\"Eliza!\" Verali's voice was deep, vibrating through the humid air. \"What are you doing here?\"\n\nEliza glanced around quickly. There were other workers in the room, their movements sluggish and tired, but they were intentionally ignoring the scene. They were the ghosts of the city, the people who lived in the cracks of the system; they had no interest in the dramas of acolytes or the secrets of dragon-kin. They just wanted to finish their shifts and go home.\n\nThe realization that they were effectively alone gave Eliza a sudden, electric surge of confidence.\n\nIn one fluid movement, she stepped forward and reached up, her fingers curling around the nape of Verali's neck. She pulled the dragon-kin's face down to hers, meeting her in a kiss that was a collision of everything she had suppressed for seven days. It wasn't long—just a few seconds of searing contact—but it was an explosion of hunger. It was a claim, a reclamation, a desperate admission that she had spent every waking hour of the week missing the taste of the woman who had woken her soul.\n\nWhen Eliza finally pulled away, she was trembling. Verali was left panting, her chest heaving slightly, her amber eyes dark with a mixture of shock and affection.\n\nEliza took a half-step back, her chest heaving, her voice coming out in a soft, breathless, ecclesiastical whisper that trembled with excitement.\n\n\"I... I brought someone to meet you,\" she said, glancing back at the shivering, wide-eyed Cecilia.",
