    "2026-05-15T06:58:16.307Z": {
      "msg_index": 140,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe world had shrunk to the size of a single, humid room. The air was thick with the smell of lye and hot steam, making the atmosphere feel heavy, almost underwater. Cecilia stood frozen, her breath coming in shallow, jagged hitches, her eyes wide as she witnessed the collision of Eliza and the dragon-kin.\n\nThe kiss had been a shock—a sudden, violent rupture of the social order Cecilia lived by. To see Eliza, the picture of acolyte submission, seize another person with such raw, starving hunger was like watching a statue suddenly scream. It was terrifying, and yet, it was the most honest thing Cecilia had ever seen.\n\nBut as the kiss ended and the steam swirled around them, Cecilia found herself staring at Verali, and the mental images she had carried—the two conflicting versions of the dragon-kin—began to crumble.\n\nShe had expected a monster. She had spent the last hour recalling the propaganda posters: the dripping fangs, the insane eyes, the talons that could rend a soul. She had braced herself for a beast that would loom over her, a predator whose presence would evoke a primal, shivering fear.\n\nBut she had also expected a goddess. Based on the way Eliza spoke, the way her voice trembled when she mentioned the \"stillness\" and the \"melting,\" Cecilia had imagined a celestial being of blinding white and gold, an ethereal creature of such divine beauty that it would be impossible to look upon her without weeping.\n\nThe woman standing before her was neither.\n\nVerali was... tired.\n\nShe was tall, yes, and her white scales were striking against the drab grey of the washhouse, but she looked *exhausted*. There were dark circles under her golden eyes, and her posture, while strong, carried the slumped weight of a long shift spent over boiling vats of linen. Her arms were red from the lye, and her expression wasn't one of divine serenity or predatory hunger—it was one of wary, human curiosity.\n\nShe looked like a worker. She looked like someone who knew the price of a day's labor and the ache of a sore back.\n\nThe discrepancy was jarring. The \"monster\" was just a woman. The \"goddess\" was just a laborer. Verali was simply *there*, existing in the same mundane, gritty reality as the rest of the city.\n\nThe suddenness of this realization brought a strange, quiet relief to Cecilia's chest. The fear didn't vanish, but it transformed. It was no longer a fear of being eaten; it was the familiar, nervous apprehension of meeting a stranger.\n\nVerali turned her gaze toward Cecilia. The golden eyes were searching, cautious, scanning the sheep-girl in her oversized habit. There was no hunger in that look, only a quiet, observant intelligence.\n\nCecilia felt herself shrink slightly, her woolly ears twitching. She felt small, plain, and utterly exposed. For the first time, she realized that the \"monstrosity\" the Church spoke of was a mask they forced upon others, while the \"divinity\" Eliza saw was a light that Eliza had brought with her.\n\n\"Hello,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice small and trembling, the plainness of her speech returning. She didn't use the formal ecclesiastical phrasing. She didn't have the energy for the performance. \"I... I'm Cecilia. Eliza told me...well...she thinks very highly of you. I'm...pleased to meet you.\"",
