    "2026-05-15T07:06:47.670Z": {
      "msg_index": 141,
      "name": "Verali",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nVerali stood still, the water from the tub dripping from her elbows, sending small, rhythmic splashes back into the soapy slurry. She didn't move toward Cecilia—not yet. She knew the weight of her own presence, the way her size could be oppressive to someone as small and fragile as the girl before her. She remained anchored to her workstation, giving Cecilia the space to breathe, the space to decide if she wanted to stay.\n\nShe looked at Cecilia—really looked at her. She saw the tremor in the girl's hands, the way her woolly ears twitched with a nervous energy, and the profound, wide-eyed uncertainty that mirrored the look of a creature that had spent its whole life being told it was prey. Verali felt a familiar, sharp pang of empathy. She recognized that look. It was the look of someone who had been taught to apologize for their own existence.\n\nA small, wry smile touched Verali's lips. She saw the way the girl was staring at her, the way her gaze flickered from Verali's scales to her eyes, as if Cecilia were trying to find the monster the posters had promised.\n\n\"Pleased to meet you, Cecilia,\" Verali replied. Her voice was a low, melodic hum, vibrating softly in the humid air of the washhouse. It wasn't a command; it was an invitation. \"Eliza has told me much about you. She says you are the most loyal heart in the Cathedral.\"\n\nVerali slowly wiped her hands on a coarse burlap cloth, the movement deliberate and unhurried. She stepped away from the tub, moving with a grace that was subtle, a fluidity that spoke of a strength she didn't feel the need to flaunt. As she approached, she didn't loom; instead, she tilted her head slightly, a gesture of curiosity and openness.\n\n\"You look as though you are expecting me to bite,\" Verali noted, her golden eyes shimmering with a gentle, knowing amusement. \"I assure you, the lye in these vats is far more dangerous than I am. And I find that I am much more interested in a conversation than in a meal.\"\n\nShe stopped a respectful distance away, her scent—a mix of ozone, old stone, and the sharp tang of soap—filling the space between them. She didn't reach out to touch the girl, knowing that a touch from a dragon-kin could be a trigger for a frightened soul. Instead, she simply existed in Cecilia's space, a calm, steady presence in the midst of the steam.\n\n\"It takes a great deal of courage to walk through a door like this,\" Verali continued, her voice softening, becoming a warm, maternal anchor. \"To step away from the only map you've ever been given. That is not a small thing, Cecilia. In fact, I think it is the bravest thing a person can do.\"\n\nShe glanced over at Eliza, who was watching Cecilia with a look of fierce, protective hope. Verali felt a surge of warmth for the pair of them—two girls, bound by a shared terror and a shared rebellion, standing in a laundry room while the world outside continued to demand their submission.\n\n\"Tell me,\" Verali asked, her gaze returning to Cecilia, her tone curious and unpressured. \"What is the most frightening thing about me? Be honest. I find that honesty is the only thing that truly cleanses the soul.\"",
