      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe steam of the washhouse continued to swirl around them, the rhythmic *slosh-thump* of the heavy laundry vats providing a heartbeat to the room. Cecilia felt as though she were suspended in a vacuum. Her mind was a chaotic jumble, a frantic library where the books were being rearranged in real-time. She was trying to reconcile the \"Monster\" from the posters, the \"Goddess\" from Eliza's whispers, and the *woman* standing in front of her—a woman with red, lye-burned arms who looked just as tired as any other soul in this soot-stained city.\n\nIt took a monumental amount of brainpower just to process the physical reality of Verali. She looked at the white scales, the golden eyes, the sheer scale of the dragon-kin's frame. The first thought that popped into her head was a simple, blunt observation: *Well, you're rather large...*\n\nBut then Verali's words sank in. *Be honest.*\n\nThe request for honesty acted like a key turning in a lock. Honesty was the only thing Cecilia had ever been taught to value—even if that honesty had previously been directed toward a confessional booth.\n\nCecilia glanced between Eliza and Verali. Her woolly ears drooped, a physical manifestation of the guilt now pooling in her chest. She felt a sudden, sharp pang of shame; she had spent years living side-by-side with Eliza, sharing a room, sharing bread, sharing secrets, and yet she had been blind. She looked at Verali, unable to meet those golden eyes for more than a heartbeat at a time, her gaze flickering down to the damp stone floor.\n\n\"I... I think, i-if I were... to be honest...\" she stalled. Her voice was a fragile thread, trembling in the humid air. She wanted to tell the truth, but the truth felt dangerous. It felt like admitting a failure of friendship.\n\n\"I'm frightened by... by how much you've changed Eliza...\" she said, her voice small, almost swallowed by the hiss of the boilers.\n\nBefore either of them could interrupt—before Eliza could offer a reassurance or Verali could offer a platitude—Cecilia pressed on, the words tumbling out now, driven by a need to be known.\n\n\"It's only that... I had thought I knew who she was,\" she whispered, her voice cracking. \"And it turns out she was hurting, and scared. And I never saw it. I was right there, and I never saw it.\"\n\nShe looked at Eliza, her brown eyes stinging with unshed tears, the moisture mixing with the steam of the room. The realization that her friend had been drowning in a void of faith while she had been comfortably floating in her own devotion felt like a betrayal.\n\nThen, she looked back at Verali, her expression a mixture of awe and genuine terror. \"But you brought her to life. Like a flower blooming where I had only seen the leaves. And it's opened my eyes to so much. And that's... what's terrifying.\"\n\nCecilia finished the sentence quietly, her shoulders trembling. The truth was out. The fear wasn't about fangs or talons; it was the fear of the unknown. It was the terror of realizing that the world was infinitely larger, more complex, and more dangerous than the sterile hallways of the Cathedral had led her to believe. She was no longer a safe, sleeping lamb; she was awake, and the world was cold.\n\nEliza stood there, gawping at Cecilia, her mouth slightly open. She looked as if she had just seen Cecilia perform a miracle. The shock on her face quickly dissolved into a deep, aching affection—a look of such profound love that it seemed to radiate heat.\n\nWithout a word, Eliza stepped forward. She didn't use a formal greeting or a rehearsed phrase. She simply reached out and wrapped her arms around Cecilia's shaking shoulders, pulling her close into a tight, grounding embrace. She held her as if she were the most precious thing in the washhouse, her cheek resting against Cecilia's woolly head, anchoring her to the present.",
