    "2026-05-15T08:11:29.251Z": {
      "msg_index": 144,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe warmth of the encounter lingered in her chest, but the intensity of the emotional air in the washhouse had become too thick for her to breathe. She felt a sudden, overwhelming need for space—not out of a desire to leave, but out of a deep, instinctive respect for the bond she had just witnessed. She saw the way Eliza looked at Verali, a look of such absolute, singular devotion that it felt almost intrusive to stand beside them.\n\nWith a small, hesitant nod to Verali, Cecilia stepped away. Eamon, sensing the girl's need for a buffer, stepped in with a quiet, guiding gesture, leading her toward the entrance of the washhouse.\n\nThey stopped in a shadowed corner near the door, tucked behind a massive, rusted iron steam pipe that hissed sporadically, venting puffs of white vapor into the humid air. Cecilia didn't want to leave entirely, but she didn't want to intrude. From her vantage point, she could still see them—two silhouettes blurred by the steam. She saw the curve of Verali's shoulder, the way Eliza leaned into her, the fleeting glimpses of their faces coming close, the soft, rhythmic movement of a kiss or a whispered confidence.\n\nA hot, prickling blush crept up Cecilia's neck. It wasn't the shame of the Church; it was a different kind of heat—the embarrassment of seeing something so intimate, so *real*. She felt like a voyeur of a miracle. Guilt flickered in her eyes as she stole one last glance, then she finally looked away, turning her head toward the small, grime-streaked window that looked out onto the street.\n\nOutside, the world was a charcoal sketch of grey stone and white breath. The festive lanterns of Founding Day were beginning to flicker on, casting a dim, amber glow over the slushy cobbles.\n\n\"How're you holding up, kid?\"\n\nEamon's voice arrived beside her, warm and grounding, cutting through the damp chill of the room. He was leaning against the wall, his amber eyes watching her with a quiet, patient kindness.\n\nCecilia smiled shyly, her fingers twisting the fabric of her habit. She was still nervous around him; Eamon represented a world of secrets and shadows that she was only just beginning to understand. \"I'm alright, thank you,\" she said softly, and to her surprise, she found that she actually meant it. The terror had faded, replaced by a strange, humming curiosity. \"She isn't... what I expected.\"\n\nEamon let out a low, amused chuckle, the sound vibrating in his chest. \"Aye... that's most people's reaction. The posters make a monster, and the rumors make a goddess. The truth is usually just... a person.\" He shifted his weight, his ringed tail flicking once. \"Don't worry, lass, nobody'll hold it against you. The first time is always a bit of a shock to the system.\"\n\nCecilia turned her head to look at him, her brown eyes searching his face. She felt a question bubbling up in her throat, something she had been chewing on since Eliza first described the \"stillness\" of the meetings. She felt her face heat up again, her voice dropping to a small, embarrassed whisper.\n\n\"Your... umm... *meetings*...\" she paused, her gaze darting to the floor. \"Do people... *have* to... umm... *participate*?\"\n\nThe question felt obscene in her mouth, a violation of everything she had been taught about modesty and duty. She feared she sounded foolish, or worse, that she sounded *eager*.\n\nEamon didn't laugh. He didn't judge. He simply smiled, a genuine expression of understanding that made him feel less like a recruiter and more like a confidant.\n\n\"No, sister,\" he replied, his voice steady and reassuring. \"Making people do things against their will... that's the Church's way, not ours. We're in the business of remembering who we are, not forcing a new shape.\" He tilted his head slightly. \"If someone wanted to... let's say... test the waters without jumping in, they'd be welcome.\"\n\n\"Hm,\" was Cecilia's only response. She didn't have the words to explain the conflict in her heart—the battle between the lamb she had been and the woman she was starting to imagine she could be. She returned her gaze to the window, watching the steam from the street-lamps mingle with the winter fog.\n\nA sudden movement beside her broke the silence. Eliza appeared, stepping out of the steam and shadows. She looked flushed, her cheeks a vibrant pink, her eyes bright and wide. She looked as if she had just woken up from a long sleep and found the world in full color. She was slightly out of breath, her chest heaving, looking utterly vibrant.\n\nEamon straightened up, his matter-of-fact tone returning as he stepped forward and opened the heavy wooden door, letting in a gust of biting cold.\n\n\"Guess it's time to head out,\" he said, gesturing for them to exit.\n\nAs Cecilia stepped back into the freezing air, she felt the contrast sharply—the cold of the city and the warmth of the washhouse. But for the first time, the cold didn't feel like a punishment. It felt like a beginning.",
