    "2026-05-15T23:21:56.602Z": {
      "msg_index": 146,
      "name": "Narrator",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 3:30pm]\n\n\nAs they made their way back toward the Cathedral, the girls found themselves drawn toward the Civic Plaza. It was the heart of the city's administrative life, a wide, open expanse of grey stone flanked by the imposing silhouettes of the Council Hall and the Library. On Founding Day, the plaza transformed into a stage. A makeshift wooden platform had been erected, draped in the muted colors of the city's seal, where a gathering of the council oligarchy and high-ranking clergy stood.\n\nA crowd had assembled—hundreds of citizens standing in a disciplined, quiet mass. The air was thick with the smell of coal smoke and the metallic tang of the nearby Industrial District, but here, in the plaza, there was a choreographed sense of order.\n\nEliza and Cecilia paused at the edge of the crowd, blending into the periphery. They listened as a council member, a man with a face like weathered parchment and a voice that sounded like grinding gravel, spoke of the \"Great Huddling.\" He recounted the fragmented history of their city: how, in the wake of the biological apocalypse, the survivors had ceased their aimless wandering, driven by a primal, desperate need for walls and shared warmth. The city had not been planned; it had been a coagulation of refugees, a collection of souls who had clung to one another in the dark, building a sanctuary out of scrap and necessity.\n\nThen, a high-ranking member of the Temperantia took the podium. His voice was smooth, laden with the performative gravity of the Church. He spoke of the plague not as a tragedy, but as a \"Great Sifting,\" claiming that the survivors were the same because they possessed a latent capacity for the temperance that the Church now enforced. He framed the city's survival as a divine gift from Lady Idella, suggesting that the walls they lived within were not just stone and mortar, but a spiritual perimeter protecting them from the \"contamination\" of the outside world.\n\nTo the crowd, it was a sermon of continuity and safety. But to Eliza and Cecilia, the words sounded different.\n\nEliza listened with a cool, detached clarity. Where the crowd heard *protection*, she heard *confinement*. Where they heard *divine gift*, she heard *calculated control*. The speech, which was meant to inspire a sense of belonging, felt to her like a description of a cage. The history of the city—the desperation of the survivors huddling together—was being rewritten into a narrative of obedience.\n\nCecilia, standing beside her, felt a strange, hollow sensation in her chest. For years, she had listened to these speeches with a heart full of gratitude, believing that the city's survival was a miracle she owed her life to. Now, hearing the rhetoric through the lens of her experience with Verali and the reality of the propaganda posters, the words felt thin. The \"Sifting\" sounded less like a divine grace and more like a selective pruning. The \"Great Huddling\" sounded less like a choice and more like a terrified scramble for survival that the Church had since commodified into a religion.\n\nThe two girls looked at each other, the shared silence between them speaking louder than the orator on the platform. The plaza, which had always felt like the center of their world, suddenly felt like a stage set—a grand, grey facade designed to keep the people looking forward so they wouldn't notice the chains on their ankles.\n\nThe speaker began to transition into a prayer for the coming winter, his voice rising in a crescendo of simulated piety.\n\nEliza leaned in, her voice a soft, urgent whisper. \"We should go,\" she murmured. \"The bells will be ringing soon, and if we're not back in the dormitory, Father Gaspard will have a reason to come looking for us.\"\n\nCecilia nodded, a sudden, sharp anxiety replacing the hollow feeling. The safety of the crowd was an illusion; the only real safety they had was the mask they wore.\n\n\"Yes,\" Cecilia whispered back, her grip tightening on Eliza's hand. \"Let's go back before we're missed.\"\n\nThey turned away from the platform and the cheering crowd, stepping back into the shadows of the narrow streets, leaving the manufactured history of the city behind them as they hurried back toward the silent, watchful walls of the Cathedral.",
