    "2026-05-16T00:39:11.261Z": {
      "msg_index": 147,
      "name": "Cecilia",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Tuesday; October; 7\nTime: 9:30pm]\n\n\nThe darkness of the dormitory was absolute, save for the thin, ghostly slivers of moonlight that managed to pierce through the heavy curtains. The room was quiet, filled only with the rhythmic, soft breathing of Eliza in the opposite bed. For a long time, Cecilia lay perfectly still, her body curled into a protective ball, her knees tucked up against her chest. The sheets felt coarse against her skin, and the air in the room had grown chilly, though the lingering scent of the washhouse—soap and damp stone—seemed to cling to her habit, which she had folded neatly on the chair.\n\nAs she stared into the void of the ceiling, her mind became a projector, replaying the day in a loop of vivid, haunting imagery.\n\nShe thought of the posters first. Those jagged, hateful lines of ink that had tried to tell her who Verali was. Then, she remembered the reality: the warmth of a hand on her shoulder, and the quiet, steady hum of a voice that didn't demand submission, but offered recognition. *'You're a good-hearted person.'* The words echoed in her mind, a small, golden seed of validation that felt more precious than any prayer she had ever recited.\n\nThen, the memory shifted to the corner of the washhouse. Cecilia’s breath hitched as she recalled the sight of Eliza—not the demure acolyte, but the *woman*. The way she had reached out and taken what she wanted, kissing Verali with a brazen, starving hunger that left no room for apology. It was a sight that should have been scandalous, yet it had been the most beautiful thing Cecilia had ever seen. She remembered the look on Eliza's face when she returned to them—flushed, shimmering, and so profoundly *alive* that she seemed to be floating just an inch above the grime of the floor.\n\nA sharp, hollow pang throbbed in Cecilia's chest. It was a longing she didn't know how to name—a hunger for a connection that wasn't based on duty, a desire to be seen and cherished with the same intensity that Verali saw Eliza.\n\n*I want that,* she thought, her eyes closing tightly. *Mother... please... if I am worthy... grant me a fraction of the love Eliza has found.*\n\nThe prayer was a whisper, a fragile thread of hope cast into the dark. But as the silence settled back in, the hope was eclipsed by a shadow.\n\nThe thought of her birthday crept into her mind, cold and suffocating. She didn't have to see the calendar to feel the date approaching; it was a ticking clock in her blood. The image materialized: the sterile, echoing hall of the Cathedral, the scent of old incense and wax, and the sight of the priests. She imagined them gathered around a table, their faces devoid of warmth, their fingers sliding across parchment as they drew lots.\n\nShe could almost hear the dry, papery sound of the lottery. She imagined a man—someone like Father Gaspard, with his wheezing breath and his entitlement—claiming her as his \"assigned\" vessel. The thought of it felt like a mantle of rotting hide being draped over her shoulders, heavy and foul, smelling of decay and forced obedience. The \"divine reproductive mandate\" wasn't a gift; it was a sentence.\n\nThe thought of being a vessel for a stranger's desire, of being a piece of property passed from the Church to a man she might fear, made her stomach churn. She felt a sudden, visceral sense of vulnerability, as if the very walls of the dormitory had turned into glass.\n\nCecilia squeezed her eyes shut, clutching her own arms tightly, trying to push the images away, but they clung to her, suffocating and relentless. She pulled her knees tighter to her chest, shrinking herself down, trying to occupy as little space as possible in a world that viewed her only as a resource to be managed.\n\nEventually, exhaustion overcame the terror, and she drifted into a fitful sleep, dreaming of white scales and grey stone, curled into a ball of wool and fear.",
