      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 10\nTime: 3:30pm]\n\n\nThe walk back to the Cathedral felt like a dream, as if the gravity of the city had ceased to apply to her. She didn't just walk; she floated, her steps light and airy, her mind still swirling with the golden afterglow of Verali's touch. Every few paces, she felt the rhythmic, sliding sensation of the Matron's seed—now cooling and sticky—clinging to her inner thighs. It was a secret, physical brand, a lingering mark of her own reclamation. Each time the fabric of her habit brushed against her skin, the sensation sent a fresh wave of warmth through her, a proud, defiant flush that colored her cheeks.\n\n*I am a heretic,* she thought, a small, secret smile playing on her lips. *I am a fraud, a liar, a sinner... and I have never felt more holy in my entire life.*\n\nAs she crossed the threshold into the Cathedral's rear courtyard, the usual stillness of the sanctuary was replaced by a frantic, industrial energy. The courtyard was a swarm of activity. Men in rough laborers' clothes were hauling heavy crates and boxes through the back entrance, their voices low and urgent, the air smelling of grease and moldy cardboard.\n\nEliza paused, stepping aside to let a worker pass. He was struggling with a large box, his arms laden with materials that looked utterly foreign to her. Sticking out from the top of the cardboard were tangles of thick, insulated cable and heavy brackets of galvanized metal. Other workers followed, carrying sleek, black-and-glass boxes that caught the dying light of the evening, their surfaces reflecting the grey stone of the walls.\n\n*What is this?* she wondered, her brow furrowing. *New liturgical equipment? Or something for the Inquisition?*\n\nIntrigued, she followed the stream of workers inside. She walked silently behind them, her habit blending into the shadows of the corridors. She watched them navigate the stone halls, their heavy boots thumping against the floor, the black boxes held with a careful, almost fearful precision.\n\nAs they reached the stairwells, Eliza followed them up. She climbed the stairs, her heart beating with a curious rhythm. When she reached the fourth floor—the level of the female acolyte dorms—she stopped, expecting the workers to enter the residential block. But the men didn't stop. They kept moving, their heavy loads ascending toward the higher, restricted floors.\n\n*The fifth? The sixth?* Eliza wondered, her gaze following the ceiling. *The upper floors are for the senior clergy and the High Inquisitor. Why is there so much... machinery... going up there?*\n\nThe curiosity lingered, but the biological clock of the Cathedral—the rigid schedule of the evening—pulled her forward. She turned away from the stairwell and slipped toward her room.\n\nWhen she opened the door, the familiar, sterile scent of the dormitory greeted her. Cecilia was there, sitting upright on her bed, a heavy, leather-bound volume from the church library open in her lap. The room was dim, the only light coming from a single candle that cast long, flickering shadows across the walls.\n\nCecilia looked up as Eliza stepped in. She didn't say anything at first, but her gaze lingered on Eliza’s face—the flushed cheeks, the bright, wide eyes, the slightly disheveled state of her veil. A playful, knowing smile tugged at the corner of Cecilia's mouth, and a soft, pink blush crept up the sheep-girl's neck.\n\n\"You look like you had a good time...\" Cecilia murmured under her breath, her voice a gentle, teasing whisper.\n\nEliza froze for a second, her hand still on the doorknob. She felt the stickiness between her legs, the lingering scent of Verali, and the sudden, electric realization that Cecilia *knew*. The mask of the demure acolyte flickered, but this time, Eliza didn't put it back on. Instead, she let out a soft, breathless giggle, leaning against the doorframe with a look of absolute, unabashed satisfaction.",
