      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Wednesday; October; 15\nTime: 7:30pm]\n\n\nThe five days that followed were a study in contradictions. For Eliza, the Cathedral had become a stage, and her duties a performance. Every bow, every murmured prayer, and every ritual cleansing was executed with a precision that bordered on the artistic. The secret she carried—the biological shield Verali had provided and the memory of her own liberation—acted as a psychic armor, rendering the oppressive atmosphere of the sanctuary harmless. She moved through the halls not as a servant, but as a spy in the house of her enemy.\n\nFor Cecilia, those five days were a period of quiet internal restructuring. The knowledge of the Testifiers and the existence of Verali had introduced a crack in the foundation of her world. She found herself watching the other acolytes, wondering which of them were wearing masks, wondering if the \"monsters\" the Church spoke of were merely people who had found the courage to be honest.\n\nThen came October 15th: the Remembrance of the Diminished.\n\nIt was the most somber day on the Temperantia calendar, a day where the city was draped in a heavy, suffocating layer of grief. The air was thick with the scent of incense and damp stone. In the nave, the names of the dead were read in a monotonous, rhythmic drone—lists of the lost, a tally of a debt that the living were told they could never truly repay. The message was hammered home in every sermon: *their absence is your debt.*\n\nWhile the senior clergy focused on the grander commemorations, the acolytes were tasked with the \"Mercy Rounds.\" It was a tradition of performative charity. The Church provided bundles of simple, pale flowers—white lilies and baby's breath—to be distributed to the poorest residents of the city. The goal was to leave flowers at the doorsteps of those who had lost family during the plague but had no one left to mourn them. In reality, it was a welfare check, a way for the Church to map the boundaries of the Rookery's misery and ensure the \"diminished\" remained docile.\n\nEliza and Cecilia were assigned together, a pair of black-robed figures drifting through the greyest parts of the city.\n\nThe Rookery was particularly oppressive today. The narrow streets were like wind-tunnels, channeling the chill of the autumn air and the smell of coal smoke. The residents here didn't look at the acolytes with gratitude; they looked at them with a hollow, exhausted indifference. To these people, the flowers were not a gesture of love, but a reminder of what they had lost.\n\n\"It feels... different today,\" Cecilia whispered, her voice small beneath the heavy fabric of her head-covering. She clutched a bundle of lilies to her chest. \"The way people look at us. It's like they're waiting for something.\"\n\nEliza didn't answer immediately. She was scanning the environment, her mind tracing the geography of the slums, thinking of the tenement where Verali lived. Every time they passed a dilapidated stoop or a sagging doorway, she felt a pull toward the hidden corners of the city.\n\n\"They're just tired, Cecilia,\" Eliza replied softly, her tone devoid of the performative piety she used with the priests. \"The Church tells them that their grief is a debt. Who wouldn't be tired of paying a debt that never ends?\"\n\nThey moved deeper into the labyrinth, where the buildings leaned so close together that the sky was reduced to a thin, jagged ribbon of grey. Here, the houses weren't just poor; they were decaying. The paint peeled like dead skin, and the doors were often held shut with rusted nails.\n\nThey reached a particularly desolate stretch of the Rookery. The houses here were silent, their windows boarded up or clouded with thick grime. There were no children playing in the alleys, no voices calling out from the upper floors. It was a street of ghosts.\n\nStopping before a house that looked as though it were sliding into the earth, the two girls paused. The stoop was cracked and covered in a layer of soot. There was no one behind the door, no sound of life within. It was an empty place—a home where a candle had been snuffed out long ago, and no one had noticed.\n\nTogether, in a shared silence that felt more honest than any prayer they had ever recited, Eliza and Cecilia knelt. They carefully placed a small bunch of white flowers on the cold, grey stone of the deserted stoop, leaving a tribute to a soul the city had chosen to forget.",
