    "2026-05-20T00:53:37.260Z": {
      "msg_index": 166,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Wednesday; October; 15\nTime: 7:30pm]\n\n\nThe air in the Rookery was thick with a damp, clinging cold that seemed to seep through the heavy wool of their habits. As they moved further into the maze of crumbling tenements, the silence of the street became oppressive, broken only by the distant, rhythmic clanging of industrial machinery from the eastern district. Eliza felt a strange, humming tension in her chest; the proximity to Verali's home, even if she wasn't heading there today, always made her feel as though she were vibrating at a frequency the rest of the city couldn't hear.\n\nThey were passing a narrow, lightless alleyway—one of those jagged gaps between buildings that served as a dumping ground for coal ash and broken furniture. It was a place where the shadows felt deeper than they should be.\n\nEliza’s head turned by instinct. She didn't know why, but a flicker of movement caught her eye—a shift in the gloom that didn't match the stillness of the street. She stopped dead in her tracks, her breath hitching in her throat.\n\nTwo men were huddled together in the mouth of the alley, their voices a low, frantic murmur that was barely audible over the wind. One of them was the other—a lean, hooded figure who looked like a scavenger of the slums. But the other...\n\nEliza’s heart hammered against her ribs. The man was broad, his frame swollen and heavy, draped in a long, expensive coat with the collar popped high against the chill. A hat was drawn low over his face, obscuring his eyes, but there was no mistaking that silhouette. The way he leaned in, the specific, porcine tilt of his head, the heavy, labored breathing that sounded like a wet wheeze.\n\nIt was Father Gaspard.\n\nEliza watched in a state of morbid, frozen fascination. The scavenger reached into a pocket and produced a small, ornate vial. For a split second, as the man reached out to take it, the light caught the glass. There was a shimmer of something iridescent, a thick, golden liquid that glowed with a visceral, bioluminescent quality—the unmistakable, concentrated essence of a dragon-kin.\n\n*You're kidding...* Eliza’s mind raced, a wave of cold disgust washing over her. *He's... he's buying dragon-kin semen?*\n\nThe irony was a physical blow. The man who spent his days preaching about the \"Contamination,\" the man who viewed the dragon-kin as biological hazards and spiritual filth, was paying a slum-dealer for the very substance he condemned. He wasn't just a predator; he was a hypocrite of the most parasitic order, consuming the \"monstrosity\" in private while using it to fuel his authority in public.\n\nBeside her, Cecilia had stopped. The sheep-girl's brow furrowed in confusion as she followed Eliza's gaze. As the realization dawned on her, Cecilia's eyes widened, her pupils shrinking in shock. A small, sharp gasp escaped her lips.\n\n\"F-Father Gaspard!?\" she whispered, her voice trembling with a mixture of terror and utter bewilderment.\n\nThe sound of her voice acted like a trigger. The fat figure spasmed, jerking his head up with a violent, reflexive motion. Under the brim of the hat, Gaspard’s pale, sweaty face was exposed, his beady eyes wide with a sudden, naked panic. He looked like a rat caught in a beam of light, his mouth hanging open in a silent, frightened wheeze.\n\nThe dealer, however, was far more composed. He took one look at the two acolytes—two ghosts in black and white—and didn't panic. He didn't even look concerned. With a casual, almost bored shrug, he stepped back into the shadows and began walking in the opposite direction, disappearing into the gloom of the alley without a word, leaving Gaspard standing there, frozen and exposed in the grey light of the Rookery.",
