      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Wednesday; October; 15\nTime: 7:30pm]\n\n\nThe silence that rushed back into the alleyway after Gaspard’s departure was heavier than the silence before. It felt thick, suffocating, as if the very air had been poisoned by his presence. Cecilia remained frozen, her chest heaving in shallow, ragged bursts. The image of the iridescent vial—that forbidden, glowing gold—was burned into her retinas, but it was the sound of his voice that echoed louder.\n\n*Public shaming... penance... re-education...*\n\nThe words looped in her mind, each one a jagged shard of terror. For twenty-one years, the Church had been the only world she knew; its rules were the gravity that held her together. To be cast out, to be branded as \"corrupted,\" was not just a social death—it was an existential erasure. The concept of \"re-education\" didn't sound like a lesson to her; it sounded like a scrubbing of the soul, a violent stripping away of everything she was until only a hollow, obedient shell remained.\n\nSuddenly, a wave of nausea rolled through her. Her stomach churned, a physical reaction to the sheer, visceral wrongness of the encounter. The scent of the Rookery—the coal ash, the damp rot—suddenly felt like it was choking her.\n\n*Why would he threaten us with that?* she wondered, her mind spiraling. *We only saw him. We didn't say anything. Why is he so... so desperate to hide it?*\n\nThe realization that the man who held her spiritual destiny in his hands was a lie was too much to process all at once. The foundation of her world hadn't just cracked; it had collapsed, leaving her standing on a precipice of absolute vulnerability.\n\nTrembling, Cecilia reached out, her fingers clutching the heavy black wool of Eliza's sleeve with a desperate, white-knuckled grip. She pulled herself closer to the other girl, instinctively seeking a sanctuary, a shield, a place to hide from the predatory gaze of the man who had just vanished into the mist.\n\n\"Eliza,\" she whispered, her voice small and brittle, barely audible over the wind. She looked up at Eliza, her brown eyes wide and shimmering with a layer of sudden, panicked tears. \"Eliza... what are we going to do?\"\n\nThe question was more than a request for a plan; it was a plea for stability. In that moment, Eliza was the only thing in the world that felt real, the only thing that felt safe. The Church had become a monster, and Cecilia was a small, frightened animal clinging to the only person who knew how to navigate the dark.",
