      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Wednesday; October; 15\nTime: 7:30pm]\n\n\nThe grip on her sleeve was tight, an almost frantic pressure that told Eliza everything about Cecilia's state of mind. The sheep-girl was trembling, her small frame shivering not from the biting Rookery chill, but from a sheer, existential terror. For Cecilia, the world had just tilted on its axis; the shepherd had been revealed as a wolf, and the fear of the \"darkness\" the Church warned them about had suddenly taken a physical, wheezing form.\n\nEliza didn't look at her immediately. Her gaze remained fixed on the grey, swirling mist of the alleyway, staring at the exact point where Father Gaspard had vanished. She could still see the ghost of that iridescent gold vial in her mind's eye—the \"divine\" essence being bought like common street-trash. The sight didn't fill her with shock, but with a cold, crystalline clarity. Gaspard wasn't a monster of legend; he was just a small, greedy man with a very expensive habit.\n\n\"He won't do anything, Cecilia,\" Eliza said. Her voice was a low monotone, devoid of the breathless deference she used in the Cathedral. It was dry, steady, and carried a weight of certainty that felt alien in the face of such a powerful man.\n\nCecilia let out a small, shuddering breath, her eyes searching Eliza's face for an answer.\n\n\"He would have to invent accusations that don't fit two 'perfect' acolytes like us,\" Eliza reminded her, the irony of the word *perfect* tasting like copper in her mouth. She finally turned her head, meeting Cecilia's panicked gaze. She allowed a small, reassuring smile to touch her lips—a genuine one, the kind she only reserved for those who had stepped outside the veil. \"And that would bring scrutiny down on him as well. He knows that. He is far too terrified of his own exposure to risk a public trial for us.\"\n\nThe logic was simple, tactical, and absolute. In the game of masks, the one with the most to lose is the one who is most vulnerable. Gaspard wasn't the hunter here; he was a man standing on a landmine, and he knew it.\n\nEliza reached out and tugged Cecilia gently, urging her to move. \"Come on, let's keep going. Just... forget about him. He's nothing.\"\n\nAs they resumed their walk, stepping over the soot-stained stones of the Rookery, Eliza felt the rhythm of her own heart slowing. She played the part of the protector, the steady guide, but inside, a flicker of anxiety returned.\n\nHer mind flashed forward to November. Her twenty-second birthday. The Biological Deadline.\n\nThe fear hadn't disappeared; it had simply changed shape. Before, Gaspard was a predator who wanted to claim her body. Now, he was a predator who had been seen. She wondered if the desperation in his eyes meant he would double down on his cruelty—if he would try to \"punish\" her through the lottery, using his influence to ensure she was tied to him not just by law, but by a vengeful desire to break her.\n\n*Can he actually hurt me?* she wondered, her hand instinctively drifting to the pocket where the lozenges were hidden. *Even if he takes the vessel... he can never take the soul.*\n\nShe looked at Cecilia, who was still clinging to her side, and felt a surge of protective warmth. They were no longer just roommates; they were survivors of the same lie. And as they placed their final flowers on the doorsteps of the dead, Eliza knew that the only way out of the fog was to keep walking together.",
