      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe following two days were a blur of ritual and repetition. The memory of Father Gaspard in the alleyway lingered like a foul scent, but Eliza had folded it into her secret store of leverage. She moved through the Cathedral with a newfound, icy composure, her eyes seeing the hypocrisy in every golden ornament and the fragility in every stern command. Beside her, Cecilia remained a fragile thing, still clinging to Eliza's presence as if she were the only anchor in a world that had suddenly become a sea of lies.\n\nIt was a Tuesday morning, the air inside the stone walls tasting of cold incense and old dust. Eliza was descending the utilitarian stairwells, her soft slippers padding silently against the granite. The Cathedral was a place of enforced silence, a void where the only sounds were the distant echoes of chanting and the rhythmic ticking of the great clock in the plaza.\n\nAs she rounded a tight, narrow corner on the third floor, the silence was shattered.\n\nA sudden, violent cacophony of clattering and tinkling erupted from the flight below—a sound of heavy metal meeting stone with a series of sharp, jarring thuds. It was followed immediately by a round of intense, guttural swearing that echoed up the stairwell, a jarring intrusion of vulgarity in a place of supposed purity.\n\nEliza paused, her heart quickening. Curiosity, a trait the Church spent years trying to prune from its acolytes, flared within her. She hurried down the stairs, her habit fluttering around her ankles, until she reached the landing below.\n\nThere, sprawled across the stairs, was a worker from the city. He was a lean man with grease-stained clothes and a face lined with stress. He stood with both hands clamped over his head, staring down in sheer, wide-eyed disbelief at the wreckage before him. A large wooden crate had suffered a catastrophic failure; the bottom had simply given way, dumping its entire contents across the steps in a chaotic landslide of industrial debris.\n\nTangles of thick, insulated cabling coiled like metallic snakes across the stone. Heavy galvanized brackets, strange glass vials, and blackened metal components—things Eliza didn't recognize, things that looked like they belonged in the belly of a factory rather than a house of God—were scattered everywhere.\n\nThe worker slumped against the wall, his shoulders sagging. He looked at the mess with a hollow expression, the sort of defeat that comes when a man realizes his day has just become an eternity of one mistake.\n\nEliza felt a sudden, sharp pang of sympathy. She knew what it was to be a small gear in a great, uncaring machine, to be one mistake away from a world of pain. She cleared her throat, her voice soft and breathlessly formal.\n\n\"Would... would you like some help?\"\n\nThe man looked up at her, and for a moment, Eliza thought he might actually weep. He looked at her as if she had been sent by Lady Idella herself, a miracle of mercy appearing in a place of misery.\n\n\"I... yes, sister,\" he stammered, his voice shaking. \"I would really appreciate it. I'm a dead man if this doesn't get up there on time.\"\n\nThe box was beyond saving, a shattered husk of pine and nails. Together, they began the arduous process of reclamation. They worked in a strange, focused silence, loading the heavy cables and metal brackets into their arms, the weight of the industrial components pressing into Eliza's slender frame.\n\nShe followed him upward, the worker’s breathing heavy and labored. They climbed past the fourth floor, then the fifth, the air growing thinner and the silence of the upper reaches becoming more oppressive.\n\nWhen the man stepped onto the sixth floor without stopping, a cold stone settled in the pit of Eliza's stomach.\n\nShe looked around. The sixth floor was the domain of the senior clergy, a place of private studies and restricted access. But the man wasn't stopping at a side office. He was heading toward the most restricted corridor—the path leading to the apartments of the High Inquisitor.\n\nNo acolyte was normally permitted here. To be caught on the High Inquisitor's floor without a direct summons was to invite a level of scrutiny that could be fatal.\n\nEliza felt the phantom weight of the \"mask\" slide back into place. She schooled her features, erasing any trace of curiosity or empathy, and replaced them with a look of profound, vacant humility. She lowered her gaze, let her shoulders slump, and prepared to put on the best performance of her life.\n\n*Steady,* she told herself, her pulse drumming in her ears. *Be the ghost. Be the vessel. Be invisible.*",
