    "2026-05-20T04:02:37.561Z": {
      "msg_index": 171,
      "name": "High Inquisitor Justinian",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe seventh floor of the Cathedral was a realm of curated silence and oppressive luxury. Here, the air was thinner, smelling of old parchment, beeswax, and the cold, metallic tang of high-altitude drafts. Justinian sat behind a desk of heavy, dark mahogany—a relic of a time when craftsmanship was a virtue rather than a memory. Spread across its surface was a chaotic geography of blueprints, yellowed technical manuals, and intricate diagrams of the Cathedral's electrical skeleton.\n\nBeside him stood one of the city’s grid engineers, a man whose eyes were wide with a frantic, procedural anxiety. The engineer was speaking in a clipped, nervous cadence, tracing a line on a schematic that detailed the distribution of power to the upper spires. He was reciting the inherited manuals like a prayer—*if X, then Y*—explaining the necessary routing of the new insulated cables to ensure the monitoring stations remained operational without overloading the ancient, fragile circuits.\n\nJustinian listened, his amber eyes tracking the engineer's finger, though his mind was calculating the broader strategic utility of these upgrades. The grid was a beast that had to be fed and pampered, and those who could speak its language were the only ones he trusted with the physical infrastructure of his power.\n\nA sound echoed from the far end of the hallway—the heavy thud of a door closing, followed by the rhythmic, metallic clinking of hardware.\n\nJustinian’s head snapped up reflexively. His predatory gaze cut through the dim light of the corridor, tracking the movement. He saw a laborer first, a man burdened with an armload of supplies, moving with a hurried, desperate gait. The worker turned into a side room, and as the door swung partially open, it revealed the figure trailing behind him.\n\nA young woman. A human acolyte.\n\nShe was the picture of ecclesiastical invisibility. Her head was bowed, her gaze fixed firmly on the stone floor, her arms laden with the same blackened metal components and cables the worker had been hauling. She moved with a ghostly silence, her presence almost nonexistent, as if she were merely an extension of the architecture.\n\n*Hm... that is strange,* Justinian thought.\n\nAcolytes were not permitted on the seventh floor. The boundaries of the Cathedral were rigid, designed to keep the servants in the depths and the authority in the heights. For a low-level acolyte to be ascending into the High Inquisitor's private sanctuary was a breach of protocol—yet, looking at her, he saw no defiance. He saw no ambition. He saw a creature of total, unblinking submission.\n\nJustinian raised a hand, a sharp, precise motion that cut the engineer off mid-sentence. The man stopped instantly, his mouth still open, the silence returning to the room like a falling curtain.\n\nJustinian waited. He watched the girl as she stepped back into the hallway, her movements tentative and humble. She was likely intending to slip away unnoticed, returning to the depths from which she had come.\n\n\"Sister!\"\n\nHis voice was a low, resonant vibration that seemed to hum through the very floorboards. It wasn't a shout, but it possessed the weight of a command that could not be ignored.\n\n\"Come here for a moment.\"\n\nHe watched her freeze. It was a momentary, visceral reaction—a flicker of sudden tension—before she pivoted. She approached him with a grace born of lifelong conditioning, her steps small and obedient. As she drew near the mahogany desk, she stopped, her head remaining bowed, her hands clasping modestly before her in a gesture of profound passivity.\n\nTo Justinian, she looked like a perfect instrument. She was a vessel of order, a model of the very subservience he sought to instill in every soul within the city. There was no scent of rebellion on her, no flicker of \"contamination\" in her posture. She was, for all intents and purposes, exactly what the Church demanded a woman be: quiet, invisible, and utterly controlled.\n\n\"What are you doing up here, sister? This floor is forbidden to acolytes, don't you know that?\" He asked.",
