    "2026-05-20T04:14:27.065Z": {
      "msg_index": 172,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nThe voice of the High Inquisitor didn't just sound in her ears; it felt as though it resonated in her marrow. It was a sound of absolute authority, the kind of voice that didn't request information but demanded the truth be surrendered. For a split second, the armor of her \"mask\" felt thin. Her heart hammered against her ribs, a frantic bird trapped in a cage, and the instinct to flee—to run back down the stairs and vanish into the shadows of the third floor—was nearly overwhelming.\n\nBut she didn't move. She knew that any sudden movement, any flicker of hesitation, would be interpreted as a sign of instability. She remained a statue of piety, her gaze fixed on the polished mahogany of his desk, focusing on a small scratch in the wood to avoid the piercing amber of his eyes.\n\nShe swallowed hard, the action feeling loud in the suffocating silence of the room. She nodded slowly, the movement small and rhythmic.\n\n\"I... I do, Father,\" she whispered, her voice a breathless, soft, and deferential hum. \"It... it was not my intent to trespass.\"\n\nShe could feel his gaze on her, a heavy, predatory weight. He was analyzing her, searching for a crack in the facade, a scent of heresy, or a tremor of fear that might betray a hidden secret. Eliza kept her breathing shallow and regular, her tone carefully calibrated to the exact frequency of an obedient daughter of the Church.\n\n\"There was an accident on the stairs, Father,\" she continued, her words succinct and devoid of unnecessary flourish. She knew that wasting the High Inquisitor's time was a sin in itself, a mark of a mind that did not understand the hierarchy of the world. \"A laborer’s crate failed. The contents were scattered across the landing... I saw him struggling and... I only wished to be helpful. I did not mean to intrude upon your sanctuary.\"\n\nShe kept her hands clasped tightly in front of her, her knuckles white. Every muscle in her body was coiled, ready to accept whatever judgment he might pass. She was projecting a total vacuum of agency, presenting herself as a simple, mindless tool of the Church's will.\n\n*I am a ghost,* she reminded herself, her inner voice a sharp, cold contrast to the softness of her speech. *I am a vessel. There is nothing here for him to find.*\n\nShe waited, frozen in her posture of submission, her mind already calculating the distance between her current position and the exit, while her heart continued its frantic, hidden drumming beneath the heavy cloth of her habit.",
