    "2026-05-20T04:48:37.604Z": {
      "msg_index": 173,
      "name": "High Inquisitor Justinian",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:00pm]\n\n\nJustinian remained still, his amber eyes tracing the contours of the girl's posture. He watched the way she held herself—the slight slump of the shoulders, the absolute stillness of her hands. He was a student of behavior, a master of reading the subtle tells of the human spirit, and in this girl, he saw nothing but a void of ambition. She was not trying to impress him, nor was she trembling with the guilty fear of a conspirator. She was simply... empty.\n\nThe logic of her story was sound. He had witnessed the physical evidence; the laborer had been genuine in his desperation, and the girl had been a mere instrument of assistance. In a city where most people were driven by a hidden, jagged hunger for more, her genuine contriteness was a refreshing anomaly.\n\nFinally, he gave a slow, singular nod. The tension in the room seemed to dissipate, though the air remained heavy with his presence.\n\n\"Alright, sister,\" he said, his voice resonating with a cold, distant kind of benevolence. \"All is forgiven. Your kindness shows the quality of your spirit. It is a rare thing to find such instinctive obedience in the face of another's failure.\"\n\nHe dismissed her with a slight wave of his hand. As Eliza turned to depart, retreating back into the shadows of the corridor, a thought occurred to him. He found himself curious about this particular specimen of piety. Most acolytes were a blur of identical black cloth and whispered prayers, but there was something about this girl's total erasure of self that intrigued him.\n\n\"Just a moment,\" he called out, his voice cutting through the silence like a blade.\n\nThe girl paused and pivoted back toward him, her movements tentative and precise.\n\n\"What was your name, sister?\"\n\nShe faced him once more, her gaze remaining modestly lowered, the fabric of her habit rustling softly as she shifted. \"Eliza, Father.\"\n\n\"Eliza,\" Justinian muttered. He turned the name over on his tongue, weighing the syllables as if they were a piece of evidence. It was a simple name, a human name. He looked down at the chaotic sprawl of blueprints and technical manuals cluttering his mahogany desk—the messy, physical reality of the Cathedral's hidden veins—and then back up at her.\n\n\"Since you are already here, perhaps you can do something for me,\" he stated. The request was not an invitation, but a directive. \"Run down to the archives and fetch the utilities map of the Cathedral grounds. Bring them directly back to me.\"\n\nThe girl's eyes widened fractionially—a small, human flicker of surprise—before she snapped back into her state of total compliance.\n\n\"Yes, Father. Right away,\" she whispered.\n\nShe turned and vanished down the hall, her footsteps fading into the rhythmic silence of the upper floors. Justinian didn't watch her leave. He had no interest in the person, only in the utility of the tool.\n\nWithout missing a beat, he turned back to the grid engineer, who had been standing in a state of frozen, breathless anticipation. Justinian gestured toward the schematic with a sharp, impatient flick of his wrist.\n\n\"Continue,\" the High Inquisitor commanded. \"You were explaining the load-bearing capacity of the fourth-floor junction. Do not let the interruption distract you from the precision of the report.\"",
