    "2026-05-20T05:12:52.535Z": {
      "msg_index": 174,
      "name": "Eliza",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:30pm]\n\n\nThe descent from the seventh floor felt like a plunge back into a different world. As she navigated the winding stone stairwells, the silence of the upper reaches gave way to the distant, muffled sounds of the Cathedral's operational belly—the clinking of copper pots in the kitchen, the low hum of the organ being tuned in the nave. Her heart was a frantic drum against her ribs, each beat echoing the sudden, sharp realization of where she had just been.\n\n*I can't believe I'm running an errand for the High Inquisitor,* she thought, her breath coming in short, shallow hitches. *This... this can't be good. Why me? Why not a senior acolyte or a clerk?*\n\nA cold shiver of apprehension washed over her. In the hierarchy of the Temperantia, being noticed by the High Inquisitor was rarely a blessing; it was usually the beginning of a scrutiny that ended in a cell or a shunning. *I need to just... get it done as quickly as I can and hope he forgets about me. Become a ghost again. Be a shadow that doesn't leave a trace.*\n\nShe stepped into the archive room, the air here thick with the scent of vanilla, decaying paper, and the dry, alkaline smell of old ink. The archivist, a withered man who seemed more a part of the furniture than a human being, looked up from his ledger. Eliza gave him a small, deferential nod—a silent acknowledgement of their shared invisibility—and moved past him.\n\nShe navigated the labyrinth of towering shelves with a practiced ease. She had spent enough time in these dusty corridors that she knew the geography of the records. She moved past the rolls of birth certificates and the heavy binders of penitence logs, stopping at the section for infrastructure and architectural records. Her fingers brushed against the rough edges of several cardboard boxes before she found the one labeled *Cathedral Grounds - Master Schematics*.\n\nShe pulled out a heavy, cream-colored roll of vellum. As she held it, she couldn't resist unfurling a small section of it. The map was a complex web of lines, symbols, and handwritten annotations—a blueprint of the very building she lived in, but stripped of its spiritual facade and reduced to its skeletal, mechanical truth.\n\n*What use does he have for this?* she wondered, her brow furrowing. She tried to make sense of the diagrams, the way the power lines intertwined with the ventilation and the plumbing, but the logic of it was alien. It looked like a map of a city she didn't recognize, a hidden city beneath the one she knew. An uneasy feeling settled in her gut, a sense that Justinian was looking for something—or installing something—that the common acolytes were never meant to see.\n\nQuickly, she rolled the vellum back up, the paper crackling in the stillness. She didn't want to be caught staring; curiosity was a luxury she couldn't afford.\n\nThe return trip was a grueling climb. By the time she reached the seventh floor again, her lungs were burning and her legs felt heavy, the successive flights of stairs having pushed her physical endurance to its limit. She paused for a moment in the hallway, taking deep, silent breaths to steady her voice and compose her expression. She smoothed the fabric of her habit, ensuring not a single fold was out of place.\n\nWhen she stepped back into the mahogany-scented air of the office, she was slightly breathless, but she kept her head low, her movements fluid and meek. She approached the desk and carefully set the map on the edge of the dark wood, ensuring it didn't overlap with any of the blueprints already present.\n\n\"As you requested, Father,\" she whispered, her voice a soft, breathless, and perfectly deferential hum. She remained in her posture of submission, her hands clasped, her gaze fixed on the corner of the desk. \"Is there anything else?\"",
