    "2026-05-20T05:28:52.977Z": {
      "msg_index": 175,
      "name": "High Inquisitor Justinian",
      "is_user": false,
      "is_system": false,
      "mes": "[Year: 2144; Friday; October; 17\nTime: 12:30pm]\n\n\nJustinian watched the door, his amber eyes tracking the slight, rhythmic hesitation of the girl's approach. From the doorway, he could see the physical toll the climb had taken on her; she was breathing in shallow, rapid sips, her chest rising and falling beneath the heavy black cloth of her habit. She moved with a quiet, urgent efficiency, her footsteps barely registering on the stone, yet there was a palpable sense of effort in her gait.\n\nHe appraised her at a single, sweeping glance. She had hurried—not out of a desire to please, but out of a fundamental understanding of the cost of delay. She had obeyed without question, and she had returned with the exact item requested, maintaining a level of deference that was neither performative nor strained. It was a seamless, integrated submission.\n\nAs she placed the vellum map upon the mahogany surface, Justinian felt a rare flicker of satisfaction. In a city filled with people who performed piety for profit or fear, this girl was an anomaly. She was a clean slate, a piece of refined clay.\n\n\"That is all, sister,\" Justinian said, his voice a low, resonant hum that filled the space between them. He reached out, his long, clawed fingers sliding across the vellum to claim the map. \"May the Mother smile upon you.\"\n\nThe blessing was a formality, a liturgical seal on the interaction, but it carried the weight of his approval. He watched her pivot with a practiced, modest grace and retreat from the office, her presence fading back into the silence of the hallway.\n\nAs the door clicked shut behind her, Justinian’s eyes lingered on the space she had occupied. He didn't move for a moment, his gaze flicking to the doorway where she had disappeared.\n\nHe thought of the priests who usually populated his orbit—the men who groveled, the sycophants who flattered his every whim, the bureaucrats who spent more time polishing their words than their souls. Their \"devotion\" was a noisy, clattering thing, a constant, grating attempt to secure favor.\n\n*Refreshing,* he thought.\n\nThe girl—Eliza—had been different. She hadn't sought a reward; she hadn't tried to catch his eye or weave herself into his favor. She had simply been a tool: efficient, quiet, and invisible.\n\nJustinian looked back at the complex network of wires and blueprints on his desk. The installation of the monitoring system was proving to be a logistical nightmare, hampered by the incompetence of the senior clergy who were too terrified of the machinery to actually manage it. He needed someone who could move through the Cathedral's veins without causing a stir, someone who understood the nature of silence and the necessity of obedience.\n\n*Perhaps an acolyte assistant,* he mused, his amber eyes narrowing as he considered the potential. *Someone who knows their place, who does not question the mandate, and who exists only to serve the function.*\n\nHe turned back to the grid engineer, his expression returning to its usual state of cold, calculating intensity.\n\n\"As I was saying,\" Justinian continued, his voice regaining its sharp, commanding edge. \"A connection on the fourth floor gallery is not up for debate. Make it happen, whatever you have to do to coax the old circuits.\"",
